终肿瘤疗效是否有负面影响，文中提到数值上有关联但统计不显著，需更大样本验证。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究人群：NSCLC队列（n=20，一线治疗，PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50%，无既往抗PD-1暴露）；黑色素瘤队列（n=20，一线治疗，允许既往辅助抗PD-1但需停药>6个月）。干预措施：单次健康供体粪便微生物移植（FMT，口服胶囊）后紧接着启动ICI治疗。NSCLC组使用帕博利珠单抗（pembrolizumab）单药；黑色素瘤组使用纳武利尤单抗（nivolumab）联合伊匹木单抗（ipilimumab）。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的客观缓解率（ORR）为80%（16/20），超过了预设的64%主要终点阈值。在黑色素瘤队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1加抗CTLA-4（双ICI）治疗的ORR为75%（15/20）。关于毒性信号，在黑色素瘤双ICI组中观察到严重不良事件（AEs）的发生率较高且起病更早：13名患者（65%）经历了3级或更高级别的AEs，其中腹泻或结肠炎是最常见的3级及以上AE（20%的患者），另有15%的患者出现心肌炎。严重毒性的中位起病时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后40天，早于文献报道的60天。相比之下，NSCLC单药抗PD-1组无3级或以上AEs发生。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT联合免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）在非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）和黑色素瘤中的具体客观缓解率（ORR），以及双免疫检查点抑制剂（dual ICI）治疗下的毒性信号特征。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"该研究通过FMT-LUMINate II期临床试验评估了FMT联合ICI在两种不同癌症背景下的临床活性与安全性。结果显示，FMT显著提高了NSCLC患者对单药抗PD-1治疗的反应率（80%），并改善了黑色素瘤患者对双免疫检查点阻断（抗PD-1+抗CTLA-4）的反应率（75%）。然而，在黑色素瘤双ICI背景下，FMT联合治疗伴随着更高的免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）风险，特别是心肌炎和早期发作的严重毒性。这种毒性信号与特定供体来源的微生物群（富含Prevotella spp.）有关，且仅在双ICI背景下出现，而在单药抗PD-1背景下未观察到类似毒性。这表明FMT的安全性与特定的ICI治疗方案及供体微生物特征存在交互作用。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": []\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的NSCLC（n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。NSCLC组接受单药抗PD-1（pembrolizumab），黑色素瘤组接受双免疫检查点抑制剂（nivolumab + ipilimumab）。供体聚类基于 shotgun metagenomic sequencing 数据，使用 Bray-Curtis 指数进行无监督聚类，并通过 PERMANOVA 评估组间差异。AEs分级依据 CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，接受FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的患者未出现3级或更高不良事件（AEs），仅15%患者因2级AE使用皮质类固醇；而在黑色素瘤队列中，65%患者经历3级或更高AEs，其中腹泻/结肠炎占20%，心肌炎发生率为15%（3例），显著高于文献报道的<1%。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）判定该组合总体安全，但指出黑色素瘤队列中的严重AEs起病更早（中位40天 vs 文献60天）。聚类分析显示，来自富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（特别是供体5）与3级及以上AEs的发生密切相关：所有由供体5提供的粪便导致黑色素瘤患者出现3级+ AEs，且2/3例心肌炎患者接受了供体5的FMT。这些毒性仅在双免疫检查点抑制剂（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）背景下观察到，而在单药抗PD-1治疗中未出现。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中安全性数据的详细统计结果、毒性事件的具体临床特征（如心肌炎发生率、起病时间）以及与供体微生物组相关的因果关联分析，作为核心证据支持关于FMT安全性的讨论。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"分析对象包括FMT-LUMINate黑色素瘤队列及之前发表的MIMIC试验（Phase 1, melanoma, anti-PD-1 monotherapy）数据。统计方法包括 Fisher's exact test 比较组间差异，LEfSe 进行线性判别分析效应大小，以及 Spearman's r 相关性分析微生物定植与免疫细胞频率的关系。\",\n                \"finding\": \"通过无监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个 distinct clusters (P = 0.001)。Cluster B（包含供体5和11）以高丰度的普雷沃菌属（如 Segatella copri, Prevotella sp. Marseille-P4119, P. stercorea）为特征。在黑色素瘤患者中，接受 Cluster B 供体FMT后，出现3级+ AEs的患者中这些菌属显著富集（LDA score > 2, P < 0.05）。进一步分析显示，在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的患者中，S. copri 的定植与外周血中 CD4+ T细胞亚群比例增加相关（P = 0.026），而这种关联在单药抗PD-1治疗中不存在。这表明普雷沃菌属的定植是双免疫检查点抑制背景下毒性增加的驱动因素。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R23\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"展示供体微生物组特征与免疫相关不良反应（irAEs）之间的机制性联系，特别是普雷沃菌属定植与特定T细胞亚群变化的关联，用于解释为何某些供体会增加毒性风险。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT联合ICI治疗的安全性与供体微生物组特征及ICI治疗方案之间的复杂相互作用。首先，FMT本身在NSCLC（单药抗PD-1）中表现出良好的耐受性，无3级以上AEs。然而，在黑色素瘤（双药抗PD-1/CTLA-4）中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC认可，但出现了加速发作的严重AEs，特别是心肌炎。这种毒性信号并非随机分布，而是与特定的供体微生物组特征紧密相关。通过供体聚类分析，研究发现富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（Cluster B）与3级以上AEs的发生显著相关。机制上，这种关联依赖于ICI的治疗背景：普雷沃菌属的定植及其引发的CD4+ T细胞变化仅在双免疫检查点抑制（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）条件下导致毒性，而在单药抗PD-1条件下不表现。这提示普雷沃菌属可能是双免疫检查点抑制背景下免疫相关不良反应的潜在驱动因子，强调了根据ICI方案选择合适供体的重要性。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管已将其列为特殊关注AE。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化‘普雷沃菌属丰富度’阈值以排除高风险供体，文中未给出明确操作指南。\",\n              \"长期随访中，这些早期AEs对最终肿瘤疗效是否有负面影响，文中提到数值上有关联但统计不显著，需更大样本验证。\"\n            ]\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC, n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。黑色素瘤患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（nivolumab）和抗CTLA-4（ipilimumab）治疗；NSCLC患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（pembrolizumab）治疗。供体聚类基于41个来自9名供体的样本进行Bray-Curtis不相似度分析。AEs分级依据CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在黑色素瘤队列中，19名患者（95%）出现任何级别的与治疗相关的不良事件（AEs），其中12名（60%）出现3级或更高级别的AEs，1名出现4级AEs。最常见的3级或以上AE是腹泻或结肠炎（20%的患者）。有3名患者（15%）发生心肌炎，这高于文献报道的<1%的发生率。严重毒性的中位发作时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后的40天，早于文献报告的60天。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）认为这些严重AEs比既往文献报道的发作更早。在NSCLC队列中，没有患者出现3级或更高级别的AEs。通过非监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个截然不同的簇（P = 0.001）。B簇（包含供体5和供体11）以高丰度的Prevotella spp.为特征。差异丰度分析证实，P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae GGB1146 SGB1472在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的黑色素瘤患者中，若发生3级或以上AEs则显著富集。后FMT定植的P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae SGB1472与3级或以上AEs显著相关（P < 0.001, P = 0.023, P < 0.001）。供体5提供的粪便在所有接受双ICI治疗的黑色素瘤患者中均导致3级或以上AEs，且两名发生快速发作心肌炎的患者均接受了来自供体5的FMT。然而，当仅使用抗PD-1单药治疗时（如之前的MIMIC试验中的供体5数据或本研究的NSCLC队列），来自Cluster B的供体并未引起毒性。综合所有经历3级或以上AEs的患者（包括当前研究和之前的MIMIC试验），来自Cluster B供体的粪便仅在联合PD-1和CTLA-4阻断的患者中引起毒性（P = 0.051）。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中关于安全性数据的具体统计结果、毒性事件临床特征（特别是心肌炎和腹泻/结肠炎的发作时间），以及供体微生物组聚类分析与免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）关联的详细机制证据。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT结合不同免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）背景下的安全性差异及其潜在的微生物机制。首先，FMT联合抗PD-1在NSCLC中表现出良好的安全性，无3级或以上AEs；而在联合抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4的黑色素瘤中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC判定为可接受，但出现了加速发作的严重免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），特别是心肌炎和中位发作时间提前至40天。其次，供体微生物组的组成决定了这种毒性风险。通过非监督聚类，供体被分为富含Prevotella spp.的Cluster B和其他Cluster A。Cluster B供体（特别是供体5）与严重的irAEs（包括心肌炎）密切相关，但这种关联具有严格的上下文依赖性：它仅在抗CTLA-4存在的情况下显现，而在抗PD-1单药治疗背景下不引发毒性。这表明特定的微生物类群（如Prevotella spp.）与ICI骨架之间存在情境依赖性的相互作用，可能驱动了双重阻断下的过度免疫反应。因此，供体选择对于未来FMT试验的安全性至关重要，特别是当联合使用抗CTLA-4时。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管观察到其与特定菌属定植及CD4+ T细胞亚群变化的关联。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化以避免Cluster B供体，目前仅指出需排除此类供体。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"questions\": [\n          {\n            \"gap_key\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Supply a concrete, attributed case for the coordinated review.\",\n            \"question\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"required_output_ids\": [\n              \"O01\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"reading_mode\": \"focused_local_search\",\n        \"required_outputs\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Source-based findings, conditions, methods and limits useful to the review.\",\n            \"output_id\": \"O01\",\n            \"output_type\": \"practical_material\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"source_hash\": \"8dd08b84648262841f9d35af692160252692383791e7c7d076ecd654063fb0f3\",\n        \"task_hash\": \"4fcf708140bdb8b00ce198191a4362b1ee8545d9d5569d77e4118fb1833a9f61\"\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material_trimmed\": {\n        \"dropped_keys\": {\n          \"material_ready\": 4,\n          \"paper_id\": 42,\n          \"references\": 413,\n          \"review_id\": 23,\n          \"schema_version\": 46,\n          \"status\": 9,\n          \"task_id\": 34,\n          \"workflow\": 21\n        },\n        \"dropped_chars\": 592,\n        \"note\": \"深读材料里的记账字段与论文自带的参考文献表已省略；科学内容（问句材料/发现/条件）保留。需要时可用 locator 读原文。\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The authors explicitly state that this is a phase 2 trial, not a phase 3 trial. They mention that the findings will be assessed as part of the CanBiome2 randomized trial (NCT06623461), which is described as a randomized trial evaluating FMT in combination with ICI in 128 patients, but the current paper does not report the results of this phase 3 trial. The study design was open-label and single-arm for the cohorts reported here, lacking the randomization required for a phase 3 RCT status.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Search for published results from the CanBiome2 trial (NCT06623461) or other large-scale randomized phase III trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated cancer patients to determine if any have been completed and reported since this publication.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires evidence of a completed large randomised phase III trial. This material provides only a phase 2 trial. While the authors reference an ongoing/forthcoming phase 3 trial (CanBiome2), they do not provide its results or confirm its completion within this text.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of FMT-LUMINate, a multicenter, open-label, phase 2 trial evaluating healthy donor fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) in patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) or melanoma. The trial enrolled 40 patients (20 NSCLC, 20 melanoma). In the NSCLC cohort receiving anti-PD-1 monotherapy, the objective response rate (ORR) was 80%. In the melanoma cohort receiving dual anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 therapy, the ORR was 75%. The study found that clinical response was associated with the loss of baseline deleterious bacterial species rather than the engraftment of donor-specific strains. Safety data indicated no grade 3+ adverse events in the NSCLC cohort, but a higher incidence of severe immune-related adverse events (including myocarditis) in the melanoma cohort, particularly among recipients of donors rich in Prevotella spp.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The authors explicitly state that this is a phase 2 trial, not a phase 3 trial. They mention that the findings will be assessed as part of the CanBiome2 randomized trial (NCT06623461), which is described as a randomized trial evaluating FMT in combination with ICI in 128 patients, but the current paper does not report the results of this phase 3 trial. The study design was open-label and single-arm for the cohorts reported here, lacking the randomization required for a phase 3 RCT status.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Search for published results from the CanBiome2 trial (NCT06623461) or other large-scale randomized phase III trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated cancer patients to determine if any have been completed and reported since this publication.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires evidence of a completed large randomised phase III trial. This material provides only a phase 2 trial. While the authors reference an ongoing/forthcoming phase 3 trial (CanBiome2), they do not provide its results or confirm its completion within this text.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of FMT-LUMINate, a multicenter, open-label, phase 2 trial evaluating healthy donor fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) in patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) or melanoma. The trial enrolled 40 patients (20 NSCLC, 20 melanoma). In the NSCLC cohort receiving anti-PD-1 monotherapy, the objective response rate (ORR) was 80%. In the melanoma cohort receiving dual anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 therapy, the ORR was 75%. The study found that clinical response was associated with the loss of baseline deleterious bacterial species rather than the engraftment of donor-specific strains. Safety data indicated no grade 3+ adverse events in the NSCLC cohort, but a higher incidence of severe immune-related adverse events (including myocarditis) in the melanoma cohort, particularly among recipients of donors rich in Prevotella spp.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0583\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a91f0ed643887e9f1b1d\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ef95a97dcd7bd8c151c4\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-501f35983c2f020a5c71\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-81ec1ef0c2a046851171\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-d46f32b2af56fa1c47e2\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-02757a568956f4b210b5\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-47775c7896730e2260bc\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b08d87be9985923039c9\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-4d651eaa044aee02c8ec\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-1294ef6fcf50c0be4a42\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-6e217865835c2f670e17\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b1f79ce65e003459f3ed\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-786845b1cd45dd24fdc8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-f0ebbdf389d42cf46d30\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementaryFiles_fetch_transport_TimeoutError\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0273\",\n      \"title\": \"The Gut Microbiome and Cancer Immunotherapy: Can We Use the Gut Microbiome as a Predictive Biomarker for Clinical Response in Cancer Immunotherapy?\",\n      \"year\": \"2021\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3390/cancers13194824\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0273\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\\\\c3429e894a9546214bd063ce941ed466\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙述性综述方法。电子数据库搜索策略包括关键词“cancer”、“immunotherapy/immune checkpoint inhibitor”和“microbiome/microbiota”。纳入标准限定为：成人（>18岁）、英文全文、同行评审期刊发表的研究。排除动物模型研究以专注于临床应用证据。对纳入的10项研究进行定性综合，按癌症类型（黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC等）和治疗结果（疗效、irAEs、FMT干预）分类整理发现。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"首次专门针对临床研究的综述，汇总了肠道微生物组作为ICIs疗效预测生物标志物和irAEs风险因子的证据，强调了FMT在逆转耐药性方面的初步临床可行性。\",\n            \"limits\": \"所纳入的研究样本量普遍较小（多数n≤42），存在异质性（不同癌症类型、不同ICI药物、不同测序方法如16S rRNA与宏基因组）。观察性研究无法确立因果关系。未对纳入的非随机研究进行严格的质量偏倚风险评估。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"接受ICIs治疗的晚期癌症患者（黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC等）\",\n            \"finding\": \"基线肠道微生物组的多样性和组成与ICIs疗效显著相关。高α-多样性通常与较长的PFS和OS相关。特定菌属富集与响应正相关（如Fecalibacterium prausnitzii, Bifidobacterium longum, Akkermansia muciniphila），而某些拟杆菌目（Bacteroidales）丰度高则与非响应相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"接受CTLA-4抑制剂治疗的黑色素瘤患者\",\n            \"finding\": \"低丰度的Bacteroidetes门细菌与免疫检查点抑制剂诱导的结肠炎（GI毒性）相关；相反，较高的Bacteroidetes丰度与抵抗结肠炎发生相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"抗PD-1难治性转移性黑色素瘤患者接受来自响应者的FMT\",\n            \"finding\": \"两项突破性临床试验显示，将来自免疫治疗响应者的粪菌移植给难治性患者，结合抗PD-1治疗，可使部分患者重新获得临床响应（Baruch et al. 3/10响应；Davar et al. 6/15响应），且安全性良好。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管免疫疗法提高了部分晚期癌症患者的生存率，但仅少数患者受益且常伴随严重免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）。目前尚缺乏基于临床证据的指导方针来利用肠道微生物组预测治疗反应或管理副作用。本研究试图厘清肠道微生物组作为预测生物标志物的潜力及其在增强疗效和减少副作用中的作用。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"临床癌症患者（晚期转移性黑色素瘤、肝细胞癌、非小细胞肺癌及肾细胞癌），聚焦于免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）治疗前后的肠道微生物组特征及其与疗效和不良事件的关系。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该综述旨在评估肠道微生物组对癌症患者免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）临床结局及相关不良事件（AEs）的影响。作者检索了截至2021年3月的PubMed、Medline和ScienceDirect数据库，筛选出10项涉及成年人的临床研究。研究综合分析了基线微生物组的多样性（α/β多样性）和组成成分与ICIs疗效（无进展生存期PFS、总生存期OS）及胃肠道毒性（如结肠炎）之间的关联，并探讨了粪菌移植（FMT）在难治性患者中的潜在调节作用。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于阐述当前领域从基础机制研究向临床转化过渡的现状与挑战，强调本综述关注临床数据的必要性。\",\n            \"material\": \"综述指出目前尚无基于临床证据的指南来利用微生物组指导免疫治疗，且既往综述多包含动物模型，导致临床应用存在差距。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景与现状概述\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于讨论当前证据等级的局限性，并为未来需要标准化、大规模RCT研究提供依据。\",\n            \"material\": \"提到现有研究在测序方法（16S vs 宏基因组）、样本采集时间点上的异质性，以及缺乏标准化的偏倚评估工具。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"方法论局限性与未来方向\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"可用于分析微生物组-宿主互作是否具有癌症类型特异性，还是存在通用的“响应型”菌群特征。\",\n            \"material\": \"汇总了黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC中不同的优势菌种（如黑色素瘤中Faecalibacterium，HCC中Akkermansia/Ruminococcaceae，NSCLC中Akkermansia/Alistipes）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"对比不同癌症类型的微生物组特征\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些关联主要基于观察性研究，存在癌症类型异质性和样本量小的限制，不能直接推断因果机制。不同研究中具体差异菌种存在不一致性。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了多项临床研究数据，表明基线肠道微生物组的特定组成（如Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, Bifidobacterium longum, Akkermansia muciniphila的高丰度）和较高多样性与ICIs治疗的良好临床响应（延长PFS/OS）相关。同时指出Bacteroidales的高丰度与非响应相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持“肠道微生物组特征可预测免疫治疗疗效”的观点，列举具体有益菌和有害菌作为证据。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅两项研究涉及此方面，且主要针对CTLA-4抑制剂，推广至其他ICI需谨慎。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"揭示了肠道微生物组与免疫治疗不良反应（特别是结肠炎）的联系。低Bacteroidetes丰度与CTLA-4诱导的结肠炎风险增加相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于说明微生物组不仅影响疗效，还影响安全性/耐受性，可作为全面评估免疫治疗预后的维度。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"样本量极小，仅为概念验证性质。供体和受体间的个体差异、预处理抗生素的使用等因素可能影响结果。尚未阐明具体的分子机制或关键菌株。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"通过FMT临床试验（Baruch et al., Davar et al.）展示了从响应者向难治者移植微生物组可恢复部分患者的治疗响应，并观察到CD8+ T细胞激活增加和IL-8表达髓系细胞减少。这为微生物组介导免疫治疗效应的因果假设提供了最强的临床间接证据。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于论证微生物组在介导免疫治疗效应中的潜在因果作用，以及通过微生物组调节（如FMT）改善预后的可能性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"主要是相关性观察，未详细区分抗生素种类、使用时机及具体影响的菌群通路。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"指出抗生素使用（尤其是ICIs期间）与较差的PFS和OS相关，提示抗生素引起的菌群失调可能是导致免疫治疗耐药的一个因素。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释环境因素（如抗生素）如何通过改变微生物组进而影响治疗机制，补充机制部分的背景。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文提供了关于肠道微生物组如何影响实体瘤免疫治疗疗效的临床证据汇总，特别指出了特定菌群（如Faecalibacterium, Akkermansia）与疗效的正向关联，以及抗生素使用或特定菌群缺失与疗效不佳或毒性的关联。此外，它提供了FMT作为克服免疫耐药的临床干预案例，是讨论微生物组调控机制和临床转化潜力的重要素材。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述仅纳入临床研究，排除了动物实验，因此其关于机制的解释（如T细胞激活）主要基于人类临床样本的相关性或FMT后的表型变化，而非直接的分子通路解析。\",\n          \"发现的菌群与疗效的关联多为观察性结果，存在混杂因素（如饮食、生活方式、合并用药），不能直接视为因果证据，除非有FMT等干预性研究的支持。\",\n          \"不同研究间使用的测序区域（V3-V4, V4, V4-V5）和分析方法不同，导致具体菌种鉴定可能存在偏差，比较时需考虑技术异质性。\",\n          \"FMT研究样本量极小（n=10, n=15），结果仅代表初步安全性和可行性，不能代表普遍疗效。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"肠道微生物组多样性（Alpha/Beta diversity）\",\n          \"特定菌属丰度（Fecalibacterium, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia, Bacteroides）\",\n          \"免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）疗效（PFS, OS, Response Rate）\",\n          \"免疫相关不良事件（irAEs, Colitis）\",\n          \"粪菌移植（FMT）干预\",\n          \"抗生素使用对微生物组及疗效的影响\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-403bce67afb0fdb07c1f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\\\\c3429e894a9546214bd063ce941ed466\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0584\",\n      \"title\": \"Navigating the gut–metabolite–immune axis: enhancing efficacy and mitigating toxicity of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803970\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:290154100\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0584\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"The authors conducted a comprehensive literature search across PubMed/MEDLINE, Web of Science, and Embase up to May 1, 2026. They included original studies, RCTs, cohort studies, and preclinical investigations examining the impact of gut microbiota/metabolites on ICI efficacy or toxicities. Data extraction followed STORMS guidelines. As a narrative review, formal risk-of-bias assessment, quality grading, and statistical meta-analysis were not performed. The synthesis is organized thematically: mechanisms of ICIs, heterogeneity of microbiome-ICI interactions, microbial metabolites as mediators, clinical heterogeneity of irAEs, and microbiome-based interventions.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive mechanistic synthesis linking specific gut taxa and metabolites to ICI efficacy and irAE mitigation, highlighting context-dependent biomarkers rather than universal signatures.\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it lacks formal risk-of-bias assessment, quality grading, and statistical meta-analysis. Findings are largely derived from preclinical models or small clinical cohorts with limited covariate adjustment. The review does not establish causal relationships for all proposed mechanisms due to observational data dominance.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Anti-CTLA-4 therapy; Melanoma patients and mouse models\",\n            \"finding\": \"CTLA-4 blockade reshapes the microbiota, favoring Bacteroides species (B. fragilis, B. thetaiotaomicron), which are critical for therapeutic efficacy via Th1 responses and dendritic cell activation.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Anti-PD-1 therapy; Metastatic melanoma patients\",\n            \"finding\": \"Responders exhibit higher alpha-diversity and enrichment of Faecalibacterium and Ruminococcaceae; however, only a fraction of human taxa successfully colonize murine models.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Anti-PD-1 therapy; Advanced NSCLC patients\",\n            \"finding\": \"Relative abundance of Akkermansia muciniphila is prognostic; low abundance (<4.8%) correlates with superior overall survival compared to high abundance, challenging the binary 'good/bad' bacteria concept.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Various ICI regimens; Preclinical and clinical cohorts\",\n            \"finding\": \"Microbial influence is context-dependent: Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes associations with irAE risk invert across cancer types (protective in melanoma but linked to severe colitis in hepatobiliary malignancies).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Mechanistic pathways; In vivo and in vitro models\",\n            \"finding\": \"Specific microbes enhance efficacy via distinct pathways: LGG and B. fragilis activate cGAS-STING; Enterococcus expresses SagA to activate NOD2; Alistipes finegoldii uses CXCL16-CXCR6 axis. Metabolites like SCFAs and indoles protect against toxicity via AhR/PPARα/NF-κB modulation.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To systematically characterize the complex interplay between the gut microbiome, its metabolome, and ICI outcomes (efficacy and toxicity), addressing the clinical heterogeneity in patient responses and the lack of reproducible predictive models or intervention strategies.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the interaction between the gut microbiome (specific taxa and metabolites) and the efficacy/toxicity of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), including PD-1, PD-L1, CTLA-4, LAG-3, TIGIT, and TIM-3 targets. It covers preclinical mechanistic studies and early-phase clinical trials across various solid tumors (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC, gastric/colorectal cancer).\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This narrative review synthesizes current evidence on how the gut microbiota and their metabolic byproducts regulate ICI efficacy and immune-related adverse events (irAEs). It delineates specific microbial taxa (e.g., Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia) and metabolites (SCFAs, indole derivatives, TMAO) that modulate antitumor immunity and organ protection. The paper also evaluates emerging microbiome-targeted interventions, such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), next-generation probiotics with encapsulation technologies, postbiotics, and precision nutrition, highlighting the context-dependent nature of these interactions and the need for standardized, multi-omics approaches to develop precise microbial interventions.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Sets the stage for why microbiome modulation is a critical area of research in overcoming ICI limitations.\",\n            \"material\": \"Overview of CTLA-4, PD-1/PD-L1, LAG-3, TIGIT, and TIM-3 mechanisms, and the clinical challenge of interpatient heterogeneity and irAEs.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contextual background on ICI mechanisms and limitations\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Provides evidence on the feasibility and challenges of translating microbiome science into clinical practice, highlighting the gap between preclinical success and clinical implementation.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion of FMT trials (responder-derived vs. healthy donors), probiotic encapsulation technologies, postbiotics, and precision nutrition strategies.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Evaluation of microbiome-based interventions\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Offers a critical perspective on the current state of the field, suggesting areas where future research needs to improve reproducibility and causal inference.\",\n            \"material\": \"Critique of current study heterogeneity, sample processing issues, and the need for standardized multi-omics approaches.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Methodological considerations and future directions\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Associations are often based on small cohorts or preclinical models. The review explicitly notes that findings are not universally reproducible and depend heavily on study methodology and cohort characteristics.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific microbial taxa associated with ICI response across different cancer types and ICI regimens (e.g., Bacteroides for anti-CTLA-4, Faecalibacterium/Ruminococcaceae for anti-PD-1, Akkermansia abundance levels for NSCLC). Highlights that microbial roles are context-dependent and vary by tumor type and host factors.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to support arguments about the heterogeneity of microbiome-ICI interactions and the lack of a universal 'good' microbiome signature. Provides specific examples of taxa to discuss in the context of patient stratification.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Many mechanisms are derived from murine models or in vitro studies. Human concentration thresholds and spatial dynamics within the tumor microenvironment are often undefined. The review cautions that these mechanisms may not directly translate to clinical efficacy without further validation.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details molecular mechanisms by which microbes and metabolites modulate immunity: cGAS-STING activation by LGG and B. fragilis; NOD2 activation by Enterococcus; AhR-mediated barrier protection and cardiotoxicity mitigation by indoles and SCFAs; PERK-GSDME pyroptosis induction by TMAO.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides mechanistic depth for explaining how the gut microbiome influences ICI efficacy and toxicity. Useful for discussing specific signaling pathways (STING, NOD2, AhR, NF-κB) and their role in remodeling the tumor microenvironment and protecting distal organs.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review offers a structured framework for understanding how gut microbiota and metabolites modulate ICI efficacy and toxicity in solid tumors. It provides detailed mechanistic insights into specific taxa (e.g., Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia) and metabolites (SCFAs, indoles, TMAO), along with an evaluation of clinical interventions like FMT and probiotics. It is particularly useful for mapping mechanistic pathways and identifying context-dependent biomarkers, while cautioning against universal generalizations due to cohort and methodological heterogeneity.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review emphasizes that microbial biomarkers are highly context-dependent (tumor type, ICI regimen, host genetics). Do not generalize findings from one cancer type (e.g., melanoma) to others (e.g., hepatobiliary) without considering this variability.\",\n          \"Many mechanistic insights are derived from preclinical models. Clinical translation is still in early phases (Phase I/II trials). Avoid overstating the clinical applicability of specific microbial interventions.\",\n          \"The review is a narrative synthesis and does not provide statistical meta-analyses or formal quality assessments. Treat the synthesized evidence as indicative rather than definitive proof of causality.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiome composition and diversity\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1, PD-L1, CTLA-4, LAG-3, TIGIT)\",\n          \"microbial metabolites (SCFAs, indole derivatives, TMAO, bile acids)\",\n          \"immune-related adverse events (irAEs)\",\n          \"fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"probiotics and postbiotics\",\n          \"precision nutrition and dietary fiber\",\n          \"cGAS-STING pathway\",\n          \"NOD2 signaling\",\n          \"aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-0c71639dc3330990d568\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-2c047b67f79872fa4042\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-2d4faf2da7bc361a24e6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementaryFiles_fetch_transport_TimeoutError\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0585\",\n      \"title\": \"Gut Microbiota in Advanced Non-small-cell Lung Cancer Receiving Chemo-immunotherapy: An Ancillary Biomarker Study from the Phase III trial JCOG2007 (NIPPON).\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.jtho.2025.02.026\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"8016df1aa5a8160b7ccd412fa6851a6a508e2731\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"abstract\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"这是一项嵌入在随机对照III期临床试验（JCOG2007, NIPPON）中的辅助生物标志物研究。研究设计包括：1. 队列选择：从入组的295名患者中筛选出270名提供基线粪便样本的患者进行分析。2. 分组比较：比较两种一线治疗方案（PC vs NIC）。3. 微生物分析：对基线粪便样本进行16S rDNA测序，进行α/β多样性分析和LEfSe差异丰度分析。4. 临床关联分析：将微生物特征（多样性、特定菌属丰度）与主要临床终点（OS超过12或18个月的亚群区分、总体OS、≥Grade 4严重不良事件发生率）进行统计学关联分析，并进行了按治疗方案的亚组分析。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了在大型III期临床试验背景下，肠道微生物群与NSCLC化疗-免疫治疗预后及安全性关联的具体实证数据。识别了特定的细菌属（如Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus）作为潜在的预后和安全性标志物，并提示了方案特异性的微生物效应。\",\n            \"limits\": \"仅基于摘要信息，无法评估多变量校正模型的完整性、多重检验校正方法、16S测序的具体流程细节及批次效应处理。结果主要关联观察性，因果机制未阐明。NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31的结果需结合具体统计显著性和置信区间解读。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"整体队列（n=270），根据OS是否超过12或18个月划分的亚群\",\n            \"finding\": \"β多样性分析显示不同生存时间亚群具有不同的微生物结构。LEfSe分析发现，Fusicatenibacter、Butyricicoccus和Blautia在长生存患者中富集。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"整体队列，关注严重不良事件（≥ Grade 4 AEs）\",\n            \"finding\": \"较低的微生物α多样性及某些特定菌属的存在与较高的严重AE风险相关。相反，有益菌属（如Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus）的高丰度与较低的严重AE风险相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"按治疗方案分层（NIC臂 vs CP/PC臂）\",\n            \"finding\": \"在NIC组中，Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus的高丰度与更好的OS相关（HR分别为0.56和0.52），优于CP组。而在NIC组中，Prevotellaceae NK3B31的高丰度与更高的死亡风险相关（HR=2.33）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管免疫治疗改变了NSCLC的治疗格局，但缺乏可靠的生物标志物来指导治疗选择。肠道微生物群虽被视为潜在生物标志物，但其在NSCLC化疗-免疫联合治疗中的具体作用尚不清楚。本研究试图回答：肠道微生物群的组成和多样性是否能预测晚期NSCLC患者在接受不同化疗-免疫联合方案时的预后（OS）和治疗安全性（严重AEs）？\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"针对无驱动基因改变的晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，基于JCOG2007（NIPPON）III期临床试验的辅助生物标志物研究。研究对象为接受培美曲塞+铂类化疗联合帕博利珠单抗（PC组）或纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗联合铂类化疗（NIC组）的患者，分析基线粪便样本中的肠道微生物群。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究旨在探索肠道微生物群作为晚期NSCLC化疗-免疫治疗疗效和安全性生物标志物的潜力。研究利用JCOG2007试验中270名有基线粪便样本患者的16S rDNA测序数据，分析了微生物多样性及特定菌属丰度与总生存期（OS）及严重不良事件（AEs）之间的关联。结果显示，微生物结构随生存时间延长而变化，特定产丁酸菌属富集于长生存患者，且与较低的严重AE风险相关；此外，不同治疗方案下微生物对预后的影响存在差异。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于肠道微生物群如何调节实体瘤（特别是NSCLC）免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效及安全性的直接临床证据。其核心价值在于不仅关联了微生物与疗效（OS），还关联了与免疫治疗密切相关的毒性（严重AEs），并揭示了不同免疫检查点抑制剂组合（PD-1 vs PD-1+CTLA-4）下的微生物响应差异，有助于回答“哪些微生物有益/有害”以及“机制是否受药物类型影响”的问题。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"结果来自NSCLC特定人群，且为观察性关联。NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31显示负面关联，提示微生物效应具有复杂性和情境依赖性。不能直接外推至所有实体瘤或所有ICI单药治疗。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了肠道微生物群与ICI疗效关联的临床队列证据。具体发现：Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus的高丰度与更长的OS相关，特别是在NIC（纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗）方案中效果显著（HR 0.56/0.52）。同时，这些菌属也与较低的严重AE风险相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持综述中关于“有益微生物促进ICI疗效”的论点。可用于举例说明特定菌属（产丁酸菌）在改善预后中的作用。可用于讨论微生物与免疫治疗毒性的关联（安全性维度）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"本研究未提供直接的机制验证（如动物模型、体外实验或代谢产物测量）。关联不等于因果。方案特异性结果的生物学解释需谨慎，可能涉及药物代谢、免疫微环境改变等多重因素。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了微生物群落结构与临床结局（生存期和毒性）的关联数据，暗示了微生物可能通过调节宿主免疫反应（进而影响疗效和毒性）发挥作用，但未直接测定分子机制。指出了不同免疫检查点阻断策略（PD-1 vs CTLA-4+PD-1）下微生物效应的差异性（方案特异性）。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中讨论“微生物效应的情境依赖性”或“药物-微生物相互作用”。虽然不直接揭示分子机制，但其发现的特定菌属（如产丁酸菌）可作为后续机制研究的候选靶点或生物标志物。可用于论证为何需要针对不同免疫方案定制微生物干预策略。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"为综述提供引言部分的背景支持，强调开发微生物生物标志物的临床需求和紧迫性。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究背景指出免疫治疗改变了NSCLC治疗格局，但缺乏可靠生物标志物，肠道微生物群被视为潜在标志物。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景与概念框架\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于展示微生物效应的异质性，避免过度简化“好菌/坏菌”的二元论。提示在整合微生物数据时需要考虑具体的菌群组成和可能的拮抗作用。\",\n            \"material\": \"NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31高丰度与高死亡风险（HR=2.33）相关，而Fusicatenibacter等则有益。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"实例与反例\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"反映领域内对于微生物指导临床决策的展望，可用于综述的讨论部分，探讨未来转化医学的方向和挑战。\",\n            \"material\": \"结论建议将肠道微生物谱分析整合到临床实践中以优化一线治疗策略。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"实践知识\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究为观察性附属研究，关联结果不能直接推断因果关系。\",\n          \"结果基于16S rDNA测序，分辨率限于属水平，且未提供功能基因组或代谢组数据，机制解释受限。\",\n          \"NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31的负面结果需警惕，可能与样本量、统计效力或特定亚群特征有关，不宜简单概括为该类菌普遍有害。\",\n          \"方案特异性结果（NIC vs PC）的差异可能受药物相互作用、患者基线特征或其他混杂因素影响，需谨慎解读。\",\n          \"摘要未提供详细的统计校正方法（如多重检验校正、协变量调整），影响结果的确信度评估。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiota composition\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)\",\n          \"non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC)\",\n          \"chemo-immunotherapy\",\n          \"overall survival (OS)\",\n          \"adverse events (AEs)\",\n          \"16S rDNA sequencing\",\n          \"Fusicatenibacter\",\n          \"Butyricicoccus\",\n          \"Blautia\",\n          \"Prevotellaceae NK3B31\",\n          \"microbial diversity (alpha/beta)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study highlights regimen-specific effects, noting that microbial associations with outcomes differed between the PD-1 inhibitor-based regimen (PC) and the dual checkpoint blockade regimen (NIC). It identifies specific bacterial genera (Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus, Blautia) enriched in patients with longer OS (>12 or >18 months). It also notes that lower microbial alpha diversity and presence of certain taxa were linked to a higher risk of serious (≥ Grade 4) AEs. The authors conclude that gut microbiota may serve as a biomarker for chemo-immunotherapy in advanced NSCLC.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"No further action required; the gap is fulfilled by the existence of the JCOG2007 phase III RCT described in this study.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"fulfilled\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports on the JCOG2007 trial, a completed phase III randomized controlled trial comparing pembrolizumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (PC) and nivolumab-ipilimumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (NIC) in treatment-naïve advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients. The trial enrolled 295 patients, with an ancillary biomarker analysis performed on baseline fecal samples from 270 patients using 16S rDNA sequencing. The study found that gut microbiota composition was associated with overall survival (OS) and adverse events (AEs). Specifically, higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter and Butyricicoccus was linked to better OS in the NIC arm compared to the PC arm, and these genera were also associated with a lower risk of serious AEs. Conversely, higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31 was associated with higher mortality risk in the NIC arm.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study highlights regimen-specific effects, noting that microbial associations with outcomes differed between the PD-1 inhibitor-based regimen (PC) and the dual checkpoint blockade regimen (NIC). It identifies specific bacterial genera (Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus, Blautia) enriched in patients with longer OS (>12 or >18 months). It also notes that lower microbial alpha diversity and presence of certain taxa were linked to a higher risk of serious (≥ Grade 4) AEs. The authors conclude that gut microbiota may serve as a biomarker for chemo-immunotherapy in advanced NSCLC.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"No further action required; the gap is fulfilled by the existence of the JCOG2007 phase III RCT described in this study.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"fulfilled\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports on the JCOG2007 trial, a completed phase III randomized controlled trial comparing pembrolizumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (PC) and nivolumab-ipilimumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (NIC) in treatment-naïve advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients. The trial enrolled 295 patients, with an ancillary biomarker analysis performed on baseline fecal samples from 270 patients using 16S rDNA sequencing. The study found that gut microbiota composition was associated with overall survival (OS) and adverse events (AEs). Specifically, higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter and Butyricicoccus was linked to better OS in the NIC arm compared to the PC arm, and these genera were also associated with a lower risk of serious AEs. Conversely, higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31 was associated with higher mortality risk in the NIC arm.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0585\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"input_attributed\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"no complete research body was indexed\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_empty\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_challenge_or_login\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"only abstract or non-title snippets were available\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"abstract_only\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0148\",\n      \"title\": \"Response to immune checkpoint inhibition is associated with the gut microbiome in advanced KRAS-mutated non-small cell lung cancer\",\n      \"year\": \"2023\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1101/2023.10.30.23297712\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0148\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\\\\51a215242479c34d622b6d11c5525875\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"Observational cohort study. 1. Participant selection: 40 recruited, 33 eligible (KRAS-mutated NSCLC, baseline stool collected). 2. Sequencing: Shotgun metagenomics (Illumina HiSeq 2000). 3. Processing: KneadData for host removal, HUMAnN3 for functional profiles, MetaPhlAn3 for taxonomy. Normalization via arcsine square-root (taxa) and log (pathways). 4. Statistical Analysis: Shannon index for alpha-diversity; PCA on clr-transformed data for beta-diversity; PERMANOVA for variance explanation; Bayesian logistic-normal linear regression (Pibble model) to identify differential abundance of species/pathways associated with DCR, PFS12, and irAEs, adjusting for covariates. Credible intervals used for significance (75% and 90%).\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"First study to profile the gut microbiome exclusively in KRAS-mutated NSCLC patients treated with ICI. Identifies specific microbial signatures (SCFA-producers, Akkermansia) overlapping with findings in other tumor types (melanoma, RCC), suggesting potential tumor-independent mechanisms.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Small sample size (n=33), single time-point (baseline only), limited statistical power (wide credible intervals for some associations like Akkermansia). Cannot establish causality. Regional/methodological differences may limit generalizability compared to larger multi-center cohorts.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Responders (DCR/PFS12) vs Non-responders\",\n            \"finding\": \"No significant difference in alpha or beta diversity. Responders enriched in SCFA-producing species (Agathobaculum butyriciproducens, Clostridium leptum, Bifidobacterium longum, Eubacterium ramulus, Fusicatenibacter saccharivorans) and Akkermansia muciniphila (wider CI). Responders also showed higher abundance of pro-inflammatory pathways (LPS, heme synthesis). Non-responders enriched in Bacteroides spp. and Blautia genus.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Patients with irAEs vs Patients without irAEs\",\n            \"finding\": \"No diversity differences. Patients unaffected by irAEs enriched in biotin and SCFA synthesis pathways. Patients developing irAEs enriched in Alistipes finegoldii, Bifidobacterium longum, and Bacteroides uniformis, and amino acid synthesis pathways.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Covariate analysis (PERMANOVA)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Metastatic disease stage and sex explained most variation in species composition. Response, ICI regimen, stage, and age explained 4-6% of pathway variation, but none reached statistical significance.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To determine if the gut microbiome composition and function are associated with response to anti-PD-1 immunotherapy and the development of irAEs specifically in patients with KRAS-mutated NSCLC, a population where such associations have not been previously established.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"Advanced KRAS-mutated non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients treated with anti-PD-1 immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI). Cohort: 33 patients, baseline stool samples only.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This study investigates the association between the gut microbiome and clinical outcomes in a homogeneous cohort of advanced KRAS-mutated NSCLC patients receiving ICI therapy. The authors performed shotgun metagenomic sequencing on baseline stool samples to analyze microbial composition and functional pathways. They compared responders versus non-responders (defined by DCR and PFS12) and patients with vs. without immune-related adverse events (irAEs), adjusting for confounders like ICI regimen, metastatic stage, age, sex, and BMI. The study found no differences in overall alpha or beta diversity but identified specific species and metabolic pathways differentially abundant between groups.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Useful for a section discussing why microbiome biomarkers may vary across populations and the need for standardized methodologies or large multi-center validation.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion compares findings with studies from China, Japan, and other European cohorts, noting differences in associated species (e.g., Bacteroides massiliensis in Chinese cohort vs. Bacteroides in this cohort). Attributes differences to diet, geography, and methodological factors.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contextualize regional/methodological heterogeneity in microbiome studies.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Supports the argument that microbiome effects might be modified by host genetics or tumor genotype, necessitating stratified analyses in future reviews.\",\n            \"material\": \"Focuses exclusively on KRAS-mutated NSCLC, a subgroup with poor prognosis and heterogeneous response to standard therapies.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Highlight the importance of genetic subgroups in NSCLC.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Study is limited to KRAS-mutated NSCLC. Sample size is small (n=33). Associations are observational. Some findings (e.g., Akkermansia) did not reach high statistical confidence (90% BCL) due to sample size.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific microbial features associated with ICI response in KRAS-mutated NSCLC: enrichment of SCFA-producing species (e.g., Agathobaculum, Bifidobacterium) and Akkermansia in responders; enrichment of Bacteroides/Blautia in non-responders.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used as evidence for the association between beneficial microbes (SCFA producers) and improved ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Supports the hypothesis that certain microbial signatures predict response across different cancer types.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Pathway-level inference from metagenomics. Causality of these pathways in driving response is not established.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Reports that responders also had higher abundance of pro-inflammatory pathways (LPS, heme synthesis), challenging the notion that only 'healthy' microbiomes predict response.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Useful for discussing the complexity of microbial mechanisms in ICI response, indicating that inflammatory signals may also be part of the effective anti-tumor immune activation.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms are inferred from correlation. Direct causal links between specific bacterial metabolites and immune checkpoint blockade efficacy are not demonstrated in this study.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Suggests potential mechanisms involving fiber fermentation (SCFA production) and vitamin synthesis (biotin) as mediators of ICI efficacy and protection from irAEs.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides mechanistic hypotheses (dietary fiber -> SCFA -> enhanced immunity) that can be discussed in the context of how the microbiome modulates ICI efficacy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Cross-sectional association at baseline. Does not prove these microbes cause irAEs.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Associates irAE development with specific microbes (Alistipes, Bifidobacterium, Bacteroides uniformis) and amino acid synthesis pathways.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Helps explore the link between microbiome and immune toxicity (irAEs), which is a key aspect of ICI modulation. May inform strategies to manage side effects.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"Provides empirical evidence linking specific gut microbial taxa (SCFA producers, Akkermansia) and pathways to ICI response in KRAS-mutated NSCLC. Offers a comparative perspective on whether microbial biomarkers are tumor-type specific or universal, and highlights the complexity of 'pro-inflammatory' vs 'health-associated' signatures in responders.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The study is observational; associations do not imply causation.\",\n          \"Small sample size limits statistical power and generalizability.\",\n          \"Single time-point sampling cannot capture dynamic changes in the microbiome during treatment.\",\n          \"Findings are specific to KRAS-mutated NSCLC; extrapolation to other NSCLC subtypes or cancers requires caution, although overlaps with other tumor types are noted.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"KRAS-mutated NSCLC\",\n          \"Anti-PD-1 Immunotherapy\",\n          \"Gut Microbiome Composition\",\n          \"Short Chain Fatty Acid (SCFA) Producers\",\n          \"Akkermansia muciniphila\",\n          \"Bacteroides/Blautia Genus\",\n          \"Immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs)\",\n          \"Metabolic Pathways (Biotin, LPS, Heme)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-8a2eafa351c193b6e72f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\\\\51a215242479c34d622b6d11c5525875\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0474\",\n      \"title\": \"Tumor microenvironment-mediated resistance to immune checkpoint inhibitors in non-small cell lung cancer: Mechanisms, combination strategies and clinical perspectives (Review)\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3892/mco.2026.2971\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0474\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\\\\627d038304660198900f99576a1ce6c8\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性文献回顾方法。检索PubMed索引的英文出版物（1946-2026年）、主要肿瘤学指南、关键II/III期临床试验、荟萃分析及机制研究。检索词涵盖NSCLC、ICI、TME、耐药及相关分子靶点。对临床证据按试验阶段、人群相关性、终点成熟度及监管意义进行加权；对临床前和假设生成性研究主要用于支持机制概念。通过分类归纳TME组成、分子通路、物理化学抑制因素及系统性因素，构建耐药机制框架，并据此评估不同组合疗法的临床转化潜力。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC中TME介导耐药的整合性框架，强调从孤立因素转向相互连接的生物学过程视角；明确了不同联合疗法（免疫-免疫、免疫-抗血管、免疫-放化疗、免疫-靶向）的临床证据层级和适用情境。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为叙事性综述，其结论依赖于作者对纳入文献的选择和解释；未提供新的原始实验数据；部分机制（如微生物组干预）仅基于初步研究，缺乏大规模临床验证；特定生物标志物（如ctDNA阈值）尚待标准化。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"TME细胞成分异质性\",\n            \"finding\": \"巨噬细胞并非简单的M1/M2二元模型，而是存在激活状态的连续谱系；MDSCs亚型特异性功能不同（单核型通过ARG1/iNOS抑制，多形核型通过ROS抑制）；CAF和ECM重塑通过增加间质硬度促进免疫排斥。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"内在肿瘤机制\",\n            \"finding\": \"STK11缺失抑制I型干扰素信号，KEAP1缺失激活抗氧化程序，两者共同导致低PD-L1表达和低CD8+ T细胞浸润；EGFR/ALK驱动突变通常伴随低TMB和不活跃的免疫微环境。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"联合治疗证据等级\",\n            \"finding\": \"纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗在CheckMate 227中显示疗效（受PD-L1/TMB影响）；阿替利珠单抗+贝伐珠单抗+化疗在IMpower150中证实对转移性非鳞状NSCLC有效，包括肝转移和EGFR突变TKI失败后人群；LAG-3/TIM-3抑制剂在NSCLC中的临床采纳仍需成熟试验确认。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"生物标志物应用\",\n            \"finding\": \"PD-L1检测存在空间异质性和平台间不完全互换性；高TMB预测价值受测序平台和肿瘤纯度影响；空间转录组学可区分免疫表型；液体活检（ctDNA/exosome）需前瞻性验证阈值。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"解决NSCLC中ICI原发性和获得性耐药限制长期临床获益的问题；阐明TME各组分如何协同导致不同免疫表型，并厘清当前联合治疗策略的证据等级与局限性。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者中，肿瘤微环境（TME）介导的免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药机制、联合治疗策略及生物标志物框架。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇叙事性综述，旨在将TME介导的ICI耐药视为一个相互连接的生物学过程，而非孤立因素的集合。文章系统梳理了免疫抑制细胞群、基质重塑、血管功能障碍、缺氧、代谢重编程以及抗原呈递缺陷和致癌驱动信号如何协同作用，形成免疫沙漠、免疫排斥或耗竭性炎症表型。在此基础上，综述评估了针对这些机制的联合治疗策略，区分了III期支持的临床标准与早期探索性方法，并提出了整合PD-L1、TMB、干扰素γ特征、空间组学、ctDNA等指标的操作化生物标志物框架，以指导NSCLC的精准免疫治疗。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"作为背景材料，帮助读者理解肠道微生物组只是影响ICI疗效的众多系统性因素之一，且其作用受到肿瘤类型和局部微环境的制约。\",\n            \"material\": \"介绍了NSCLC中ICI耐药的多维度机制，包括内在肿瘤基因改变、局部TME抑制和系统性因素。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供NSCLC中ICI治疗的临床背景和耐药复杂性，说明为何单一靶点治疗往往失败，从而引出多因素（包括微生物组）调控的重要性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"在评估微生物组 biomarker 的价值时，可作为参照系，说明新型生物标志物面临的挑战（如标准化、验证），并提示未来可能需要多组学整合模型。\",\n            \"material\": \"讨论了PD-L1、TMB、空间组学和液体活检等生物标志物的现状、局限性和整合需求。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供关于生物标志物和患者分层的信息，特别是当讨论微生物组作为预测因子时，需要与其他已知生物标志物（如PD-L1, TMB）进行比较和整合。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"明确指出微生物组相关干预目前仍处于“有强烈机制吸引力但需要前瞻性临床验证”的阶段，避免将其过度解读为已确立的标准治疗。\",\n            \"material\": \"区分了III期支持的联合疗法（如双免疫、免疫+抗血管）与早期探索性疗法（如微生物组调节、IDO抑制）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供关于联合治疗策略的证据分级，帮助判断基于微生物组的干预措施在当前临床实践中的地位。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅限NSCLC人群；关联多为观察性，因果机制未详述；Fusobacterium的作用在其他癌种更明确，此处仅为对比提示。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC队列中与ICI疗效相关的特定肠道微生物菌群信息：Akkermansia muciniphila、Bifidobacterium spp. 和 Ruminococcus 被报道有助于促进有利的免疫微环境；而 Fusobacterium 的关联在结直肠癌中更强，在NSCLC中需谨慎外推。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节ICI疗效”的具体实例，展示有益菌和潜在有害菌在NSCLC中的不同表现。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制描述较为概括，未深入具体的微生物-宿主分子互作细节；主要基于综述转述的其他研究结果；强调需要前瞻性验证。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"阐述了系统性因素（包括微生物组、营养状态、全身炎症）如何作为TME介导耐药的一部分影响ICI疗效。指出微生物组特征可能通过塑造有利的免疫微环境来增强ICI反应，但同时也强调了这种关联需要NSCLC特异性数据集的支持，且目前微生物组干预（如FMT、益生菌）仍属探索性阶段，缺乏标准化评估和安全监测。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释微生物组影响ICI疗效的系统性背景机制，以及当前干预手段的局限性。帮助区分已观察到的关联与尚未验证的因果机制。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解Gut Microbiome如何影响ICI疗效提供了关键的宿主背景框架。虽然其主要焦点是NSCLC的TME，但它详细阐述了系统性因素（包括肠道微生物组）与局部TME及ICI疗效之间的关联，并指出了当前研究的局限性和未来方向。对于综述而言，它提供了关于微生物组在NSCLC中具体菌种作用的实证数据，以及关于系统性炎症和营养状态如何调节免疫应答的背景知识，同时强调了需要疾病特异性验证的重要性。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述聚焦于NSCLC，其关于微生物组的发现不能直接外推到所有实体瘤或其他癌症类型，尽管文中提到了结直肠癌的对比。\",\n          \"文中提到的微生物组与ICI疗效的关联主要基于观察性研究和初步数据，因果关系尚未完全确立，干预效果也未经验证。\",\n          \"综述本身是叙事性的，其结论依赖于作者对文献的选择和综合，可能存在选择偏倚。\",\n          \"对于Fusobacterium等菌种，文中明确指出其在NSCLC中的作用不如在结直肠癌中明确，需谨慎解读。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"NSCLC肿瘤微环境（TME）\",\n          \"免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药机制\",\n          \"肠道微生物组（Gut Microbiome）与ICI疗效\",\n          \"系统性炎症与宿主状态\",\n          \"联合治疗策略证据分级\",\n          \"生物标志物框架（PD-L1, TMB, Spatial Omics, ctDNA）\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\\\\627d038304660198900f99576a1ce6c8\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0271\",\n      \"title\": \"The role of the gut microbiome on the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in Japanese responder patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer.\",\n      \"year\": \"2019\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.21037/tlcr.2019.10.23\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0271\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\\\\5a16b2ba1f3b6771b60cbdbf52912ac1\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用回顾性队列设计。纳入标准为确诊为晚期NSCLC、接受ICI治疗时长>3个月的日本患者。收集治疗期间的单次粪便样本，使用Illumina MiSeq平台对16S rRNA基因的V1-V2区域进行测序。利用QIIME流程进行OTU聚类（97%相似度）和Alpha多样性分析。使用LEfSe算法识别反应者与非反应者之间显著差异的分类群。临床结局以治疗失败时间（TTF）为主要终点，使用Kaplan-Meier法和Log-rank检验分析特定细菌属的高/低丰度与TTF的关系。PD-L1表达通过商业实验室的22C3 pharmDx检测进行评估。统计使用EZR软件，P<0.05视为显著。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"在亚洲（日本）NSCLC人群中，初步识别出与ICI疗效相关的特定肠道细菌属（如Lactobacillus, Clostridium, Bilophila等），提供了微生物组作为潜在疗效生物标志物的观察性证据。\",\n            \"limits\": \"样本量极小（仅17例），存在选择偏倚（仅纳入治疗>3个月的患者，可能排除早期进展或不耐受者）；单中心回顾性研究；仅采集单次粪便样本，无法反映治疗前后菌群动态变化；未控制抗生素使用等混杂因素的具体细节（虽记录但未深入校正）；部分关键图表不可读，具体统计细节受限。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"对比ICI反应者（n=6）与非反应者（n=11）的肠道菌群组成\",\n            \"finding\": \"反应者的肠道菌群中乳酸杆菌（Lactobacillus）、梭菌属（Clostridium）和Syntrophococcus显著富集；非反应者中Bilophila、Sutterella和Parabacteroides显著富集。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"特定细菌属丰度与治疗失败时间（TTF）的关系\",\n            \"finding\": \"高丰度的Bilophila与显著延长的TTF相关（HR 6.27, P=0.0037）。高丰度的Lactobacillus和Clostridium倾向于延长TTF，但统计学上不显著（P分别为0.219和0.352）。Syntrophococcus高丰度也显示延长TTF的趋势（P=0.057）。Parabacteroides丰度与TTF无显著关联。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"肠道菌群Alpha多样性\",\n            \"finding\": \"反应者与非反应者之间的Alpha多样性无显著差异，且未影响TTF。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"在晚期NSCLC患者中，是否存在特定的肠道微生物组特征可以作为预测免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）长期疗效的生物标志物？目前缺乏能够筛选出能从ICI中长期获益患者的强效生物标志物。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"日本晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗超过3个月的人群。样本量为17例，分为反应者（R, n=6）和非反应者（NR, n=11）。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究旨在探讨肠道微生物组组成与晚期NSCLC患者对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效之间的关联。研究回顾性分析了17名在日本京都府立医科大学接受ICI治疗且持续时间超过3个月的患者的粪便样本。通过16S rRNA基因测序分析肠道菌群多样性及物种丰度，并结合临床疗效数据（基于RECIST 1.1标准定义的响应状态及治疗失败时间TTF），比较了反应者与非反应者在肠道微生物组成上的差异，并评估特定细菌属的丰度与临床预后指标的相关性。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供NSCLC这一特定实体瘤类型的微生物组数据，填补泛癌种综述中的瘤种特异性空白。\",\n            \"material\": \"日本NSCLC患者队列的微生物组构成数据及与TTF的关联。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为实体瘤中微生物组影响ICI疗效的一个具体案例，特别是强调亚洲人群的数据，有助于评估地域/种族对微生物组-疗效关系的潜在影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"展示单一时间点采样方法的局限性。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究仅在治疗期间采集一次粪便样本，未评估基线或治疗前后的动态变化。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"在综述的方法学讨论部分，可用此为例说明单次采样可能无法捕捉微生物组的动态演变及其对免疫治疗反应的复杂影响，支持需要纵向研究设计的观点。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"样本量小（n=17），仅为观察性关联，不能确立因果关系。仅针对日本人群，外推至其他种族需谨慎。仅测量了治疗期间的单次样本，未涵盖基线或动态变化。部分关键细菌（如Lactobacillus）的关联未达到统计学显著性（P>0.05），仅表现为趋势。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了在晚期NSCLC患者中，肠道菌群组成与ICI疗效关联的具体物种列表。发现反应者富集Lactobacillus, Clostridium, Syntrophococcus，而非反应者富集Bilophila, Sutterella, Parabacteroides。其中Bilophila高丰度与显著延长的TTF（即更好的疗效/更慢的疾病进展）相关，这与部分其他研究可能存在的负面关联形成对比或补充，提示需关注菌种特异性而非整体多样性。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节实体瘤中ICI的疗效”这一子问题。可作为证据说明某些特定细菌的存在与更好的临床结果（更长TTF）相关，或者相反，某些细菌与较差结果相关。可用于讨论不同研究中微生物组标志物不一致的原因（如人群差异、菌种特异性）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些机制解释主要基于作者引用的既往研究（如小鼠模型），并非本研究直接证实的因果机制。本研究本身未进行机制验证实验（如动物共接种、免疫表型分析等）。因此，这些内容应被视为“假说”或“背景知识”，而非本研究的实证发现。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"作者在讨论中提出了潜在的机制假设：Lactobacillus可能通过促进树突状细胞成熟和调节宿主免疫来增强T细胞动员；而Sutterella和Bilophila可能在肿瘤微环境中引发炎症，导致对ICI不敏感。此外，提到小鼠模型中Lactobacillus可抑制Bilophila诱导的炎症和肠屏障功能障碍。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导该效应的机制是什么”这一子问题。虽然这是作者基于文献的推测而非本研究的直接实验验证，但它提供了将观察到的微生物组变化与已知免疫学机制联系起来的理论框架。可用于综述中讨论微生物组影响免疫治疗的潜在生物学通路（如DC成熟、T细胞浸润、炎症调控）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该研究提供了关于肠道微生物组如何关联固体肿瘤（NSCLC）免疫治疗疗效的直接观察数据。其核心价值在于指出了特定细菌属（有益菌与有害菌）与临床预后（TTF）的方向性关联，尽管由于样本量限制，部分关联未达到统计学显著性。对于综述而言，它提供了具体的“菌-效应”对应关系素材，特别是关于Bilophila与较差预后的负向关联，以及Lactobacillus/Clostridium的正向趋势，可用于支持或反驳不同研究中微生物组对ICI疗效影响的异质性。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究样本量极小（17例），统计效力不足，许多具有生物学意义的趋势（如Lactobacillus）未达到统计学显著性。解读时应避免夸大其确定性，应视为探索性证据。\",\n          \"研究对象仅限于日本NSCLC患者，且均为长期治疗者（>3个月），存在严重的选择偏倚（幸存者偏差）。结果可能不适用于早期治疗失败者或其他种族群体。\",\n          \"作者提出的机制（如Lactobacillus促进DC成熟）是基于文献综述的推测，本研究并未提供直接的分子或细胞水平证据。在综述中引用时，必须明确区分这是作者的假设还是本研究的实证结果。\",\n          \"Bilophila高丰度与延长TTF（更好疗效）的关联可能与部分其他研究报道的负面作用相反，这突显了微生物组研究的高度情境依赖性和潜在矛盾，需谨慎对待单一研究的结论。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiome composition in NSCLC\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy biomarkers\",\n          \"Lactobacillus abundance and ICI response\",\n          \"Clostridium abundance and ICI response\",\n          \"Bilophila abundance and ICI resistance\",\n          \"Time to treatment failure (TTF) as clinical endpoint\",\n          \"Japanese population specificity in microbiome studies\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-3aa25875e4222837763e\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\\\\5a16b2ba1f3b6771b60cbdbf52912ac1\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0188\",\n      \"title\": \"Intestinal fungal signatures and their impact on immune checkpoint inhibitor efficacy: a multi-cohort meta-analysis\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41522-025-00827-2\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"53b2e830dfcc67b7fc270e0380e77514a307e72c\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0188\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\53b2e830dfcc67b7fc270e0380e77514a307e72c\\\\dba21a222f6a9fbb5615479d9af9082b\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用多队列荟萃分析结合体内验证的设计路线。首先，从NCBI/ENA收集8个队列的粪便宏基因组数据，进行标准化质控、去宿主序列及真菌分类学注释。其次，使用MMUPHin校正批次效应，通过Logistic回归和Cox回归筛选与客观缓解率（ORR）、无进展生存期（PFS）及总生存期（OS）相关的真菌物种，并进行Meta分析以识别跨队列一致性的标志物。同时，构建细菌-真菌共现网络分析互作模式，并利用PAM算法定义真菌肠型。最后，在B16F10黑色素瘤小鼠模型中，通过灌胃Debaryomyces hansenii并联合PD-1单抗治疗，观察肿瘤生长、CD8+ T细胞浸润及免疫因子表达，以验证关键真菌的功能性作用。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了首个针对多种实体瘤的大规模多队列肠道真菌与ICI疗效关联的证据，识别出具有预测价值的真菌物种（如A. avenaceus, D. hansenii）和肠型。\",\n            \"limits\": \"多数样本来自欧洲，地理局限性可能导致外推偏差；部分队列（如NSCLC）样本量小或响应率低，结果需谨慎解读；真菌DNA提取困难导致低丰度信号可能存在技术噪声；体内实验仅验证单一菌种，未阐明完整分子机制。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"黑色素瘤队列\",\n            \"finding\": \"Rhizophagus irregularis高丰度与较差疗效相关；Aspergillus avenaceus高丰度与较好疗效相关；Asper_high肠型比例较高且预后较好；Debaryomyces hansenii高丰度抑制疗效。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"NSCLC队列\",\n            \"finding\": \"Aspergillus pseudonomiae高丰度与良好预后相关；Saccharomyces cerevisiae高丰度与疗效受损相关；Sacc_type肠型在部分指标上显示优势。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"微生物互作分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"黑色素瘤响应者中细菌-真菌互作网络更复杂且正相关性更强；NSCLC中趋势相反，可能受样本量和测序深度影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"小鼠体内验证\",\n            \"finding\": \"灌胃Debaryomyces hansenii显著促进肿瘤生长，削弱PD-1单抗疗效，伴随肿瘤组织中CD8+ T细胞比例下降及CCL5/CXCL10/PD-L1表达降低。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"既往研究多聚焦于肠道细菌，而肠道真菌在ICIs疗效中的作用尚不明确。本研究试图回答：哪些特定的肠道真菌物种或群落结构与ICIs疗效相关？真菌与细菌的相互作用如何影响治疗反应？是否存在可验证的因果机制？\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究对象为接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）治疗的黑色素瘤、非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）和肾细胞癌（RCC）患者。研究范围涵盖8个队列的976例粪便宏基因组数据，以及小鼠体内验证实验。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究旨在探究肠道真菌对ICIs疗效的影响。通过整合8个队列的宏基因组测序数据，识别与ICI疗效相关的真菌标志物及真菌肠型。研究发现特定真菌丰度与疗效相关，且响应者中细菌-真菌互作更强。进一步通过小鼠模型验证了Debaryomyces hansenii通过减少CD8+ T细胞浸润降低PD-1单抗疗效的机制。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供方法学参考和局限性警示\",\n            \"material\": \"研究采用了标准化的真菌注释流程（Kraken2 + Bracken + 自定义数据库），并使用了MMUPHin进行批次校正。同时，作者坦诚了低测序深度、缺乏阴性对照、地理局限性等不足。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"在综述中评估当前肠道真菌研究的可靠性时，可作为方法论案例，说明如何处理宏基因组数据中的低丰度信号和批次效应，以及如何审慎解读观察性关联。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供跨界互作的背景知识\",\n            \"material\": \"研究发现响应者中细菌-真菌互作网络更复杂，且某些真菌（如D. hansenii）可能通过影响细菌或免疫微环境发挥作用。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"支持综述中关于“微生物组整体生态”而非单一物种重要性的论点，强调细菌-真菌协同作用在ICI疗效中的潜在角色。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"关联结果基于观察性荟萃分析，存在混杂因素风险；部分物种（如植物病原菌）的临床意义尚未完全明确；NSCLC队列样本量限制使得结论不如黑色素瘤稳健。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了具体的真菌物种与ICI疗效关联的方向性证据。例如，在黑色素瘤中，Aspergillus avenaceus正向关联，而Rhizophagus irregularis和Debaryomyces hansenii负向关联；在NSCLC中，Aspergillus pseudonomiae正向关联。这有助于解释为何不同研究中微生物标志物存在异质性，提示需按菌种甚至癌症类型细分。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中讨论肠道真菌作为生物标志物的潜力，特别是强调真菌组成的异质性及癌症类型依赖性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"体内实验仅展示了表型和相关性变化，未深入解析具体的分子通路（如具体细胞因子级联反应）；其他阳性关联真菌（如A. avenaceus）的机制在本研究中未经验证；作者提及的机制多为文献推测，需结合其他研究综合判断。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了真菌影响ICI疗效的潜在机制线索。通过小鼠实验证实Debaryomyces hansenii可通过减少CD8+ T细胞浸润和降低趋化因子（CCL5, CXCL10）表达来抑制PD-1单抗疗效。此外，讨论了真菌细胞壁成分（如β-葡聚糖）激活免疫受体（如Dectin-1/CARD9）的可能性，以及真菌代谢物对细菌生长的影响。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中阐述真菌调节抗肿瘤免疫的具体途径，特别是真菌如何通过调节T细胞功能或免疫检查点表达来影响治疗反应。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"肠型的定义依赖于聚类算法和特定数据集，不同队列间分布存在差异；细菌肠型在本研究中未显示显著关联，突显了真菌肠型的独特性但也限制了通用性。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提出了“真菌肠型”概念，发现Aspergillus主导型（Asper_high）和Saccharomyces主导型（Sacc_type）与较好的ICI疗效相关。这为将复杂的微生物组数据简化为可操作的临床分层工具提供了依据。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中探讨微生物组分层的策略，比较基于物种丰度与基于群落结构（肠型）的方法优劣。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于肠道真菌（而非仅细菌）调节ICI疗效的关键实证素材。其核心价值在于区分了不同癌症类型（黑色素瘤vs NSCLC）中真菌标志物的特异性，揭示了真菌肠型作为分层工具的潜力，并通过动物实验初步建立了“特定真菌-免疫微环境-疗效”的因果链条，特别是针对负面调控因子（D. hansenii）的机制探索。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究主要基于黑色素瘤和NSCLC数据，RCC数据因样本量小而结论有限，不能直接推广至所有实体瘤。\",\n          \"发现的真菌标志物多为相关性，除D. hansenii外，其他物种的因果机制尚属假设，需在综述中区分观察性发现与机制性证据。\",\n          \"NSCLC中观察到的互作网络趋势与黑色素瘤相反，作者归因于样本量和测序深度，这在解释跨癌种一致性时需格外谨慎。\",\n          \"部分鉴定出的真菌（如植物病原菌）在人体肠道中的定植能力和致病性尚存争议，其临床意义需结合后续培养和功能研究验证。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"肠道真菌物种特异性标志物\",\n          \"真菌肠型（Enterotypes）与临床结局\",\n          \"跨界微生物互作（细菌-真菌网络）\",\n          \"真菌介导的免疫抑制机制（CD8+ T细胞耗竭）\",\n          \"不同实体瘤类型的真菌响应差异\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\53b2e830dfcc67b7fc270e0380e77514a307e72c\\\\dba21a222f6a9fbb5615479d9af9082b\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0118\",\n      \"title\": \"Gut Microbiota and Extraintestinal Cancers: Mechanistic Insights and Microbiome‐Targeted Interventions\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1002/jgh3.70409\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"32de51105abb9bbbfa8d5cfe870d789a5c960507\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0118\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\format_recovered_reading\\\\32de51105abb9bbbfa8d5cfe870d789a5c960507\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"The authors conducted a narrative synthesis of existing literature, organizing the content by cancer type (breast, lung, melanoma, prostate) and mechanism (immune, metabolic, hormonal). They summarize findings from animal models (germ-free mice, xenografts) and human observational/interventional studies (cohort studies, clinical trials of FMT/probiotics). The review categorizes microbial taxa by their pro-tumor or anti-tumor associations and discusses the translational potential of microbiome-based therapies.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive overview of the mechanistic links (immune, metabolic, hormonal) between gut microbiota and four major extraintestinal cancers. Summarizes key microbial taxa and their functional roles in cancer progression and therapy response.\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it does not present new primary data or a systematic meta-analysis. Many cited mechanisms are derived from animal models or small-scale human observational studies, limiting generalizability. The review acknowledges significant inter-individual variability and methodological heterogeneity in microbiome research.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Melanoma and NSCLC patients undergoing ICI therapy\",\n            \"finding\": \"Higher baseline gut microbial diversity and abundance of specific taxa (e.g., Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium, Ruminococcaceae) correlate with improved response rates and survival. Conversely, low diversity or dominance of Bacteroidales is linked to resistance.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Mouse models of melanoma and lung cancer\",\n            \"finding\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) from ICI-responsive donors can restore sensitivity to PD-1/PD-L1 blockade in resistant hosts, demonstrating a causal link between specific microbiomes and therapeutic efficacy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Breast cancer context\",\n            \"finding\": \"Gut microbes via the 'estrobolome' regulate estrogen metabolism; dysbiosis increasing beta-glucuronidase activity elevates free estrogen, promoting ER+ breast cancer risk. Certain taxa (e.g., ETBF) may translocate to breast tissue and promote metastasis via inflammation.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Prostate cancer context\",\n            \"finding\": \"Gut microbes can influence androgen receptor signaling; some bacteria synthesize androgens fueling castration-resistant prostate cancer (CRPC), while others (e.g., Prevotella stercorea) may suppress tumor growth.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"General immunotherapy context\",\n            \"finding\": \"Antibiotic use around the time of ICI initiation is associated with significantly reduced progression-free and overall survival, likely due to depletion of beneficial commensals required for immune priming.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To understand the systemic influence of gut dysbiosis on extraintestinal carcinogenesis and to evaluate how microbiome composition affects the efficacy of cancer therapies, particularly immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) and chemotherapy.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the role of gut microbiota in extraintestinal malignancies, specifically breast cancer, lung cancer (non-small cell lung cancer, NSCLC), melanoma, and prostate cancer. It covers mechanisms of interaction (metabolic, immune, hormonal) and microbiome-targeted interventions (FMT, probiotics, diet).\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This is a narrative review synthesizing current evidence on how the gut microbiome influences the development and treatment response of non-gastrointestinal cancers. It outlines the 'gut-microbiota-cancer axis', detailing mechanisms such as immune modulation, metabolic reprogramming (e.g., estrobolome, SCFAs), and inflammatory signaling. The paper highlights specific microbial taxa associated with tumor promotion or suppression across different cancer types and evaluates emerging therapeutic strategies like fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), defined live biotherapeutics, and dietary interventions.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Provides broader context on how gut microbes influence distant organs beyond just immune modulation, highlighting the systemic nature of the gut-cancer axis.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion of the 'estrobolome' in breast cancer and androgen-producing bacteria in prostate cancer.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contextual background on extraintestinal cancer mechanisms\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Offers insight into potential therapeutic avenues to overcome ICI resistance, though noting that most are still experimental.\",\n            \"material\": \"Summary of FMT trials, probiotic limitations, and dietary interventions.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Evaluation of intervention strategies\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Evidence is largely correlative in humans; causal proof comes from mouse models and small FMT trials. Results vary by cancer type and patient cohort.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Summarizes clinical evidence linking specific gut microbiome compositions (high diversity, presence of Akkermansia/Bifidobacterium) to improved ICI outcomes in melanoma and NSCLC. Highlights the negative impact of antibiotic use on ICI efficacy.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to support the association between microbiome profiles and ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Can cite the specific taxa identified as positive predictors.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Many mechanistic details are inferred from animal studies or in vitro data. The exact molecular pathways in humans are still being elucidated. The review notes that 'MMSP' (microbiome-mediated secretory phenotype) is a proposed unifying concept but requires further validation.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details mechanisms including: 1) Immune priming (enhancing CD8+ T-cell infiltration and dendritic cell maturation); 2) Metabolic modulation (SCFA production, bile acid alteration); 3) Hormonal regulation (estrobolome in breast cancer, androgen synthesis in prostate cancer); 4) Inflammatory signaling (NF-kB activation).\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to explain *how* the microbiome modulates ICI efficacy. Specifically, use the immune priming mechanism (DC/T-cell interaction) and metabolic pathways (SCFAs) as mechanistic explanations.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review provides a structured framework for linking gut microbiome features to ICI efficacy in solid tumors (melanoma, lung, breast, prostate). It offers specific mechanistic hypotheses (immune priming, metabolic modulation) and identifies key microbial taxa (Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) and interventions (FMT, antibiotics avoidance) that are directly relevant to the user's facets on ICI modulation and underlying mechanisms.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review covers multiple cancer types; findings in melanoma/lung may not apply to other solid tumors without verification.\",\n          \"Mechanisms described often rely on murine models; direct translation to human physiology should be cautious.\",\n          \"Correlation does not imply causation for many human observational findings; FMT trials provide stronger causal evidence but are limited in scale.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor (ICI) Response Modulation\",\n          \"Gut-Lung Axis in NSCLC\",\n          \"Gut-Skin/Melanoma Axis\",\n          \"Estrobolome and Breast Cancer\",\n          \"Androgen Metabolism and Prostate Cancer\",\n          \"Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) Clinical Trials\",\n          \"Microbial Taxa: Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium spp., Ruminococcaceae\",\n          \"Antibiotic Disruption of Microbiome\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\format_recovered_reading\\\\32de51105abb9bbbfa8d5cfe870d789a5c960507\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0612\",\n      \"title\": \"Pharmacomicrobiomics: exploiting the drug-microbiota interactions in anticancer therapies\",\n      \"year\": \"2018\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1186/s40168-018-0483-7\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:43964492\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0612\",\n        \"card_path\": \"\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0359\",\n      \"title\": \"Gut microbiome on immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy and consequent immune-related colitis: a review\",\n      \"year\": \"2023\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.5217/ir.2023.00019\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"9c9ca2b5565671000a2be4e099d807fda5fb25ab\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0359\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\9c9ca2b5565671000a2be4e099d807fda5fb25ab\\\\2d9b9a39c26f55a8c3ca574d04662b4b\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"As a narrative review, the authors systematically organized and summarized findings from previously published human clinical trials and preclinical studies. The approach involved categorizing evidence by: 1) Mechanisms of ICIs; 2) Correlations between baseline/response microbiome profiles and ICI efficacy; 3) Impact of microbiome-modulating interventions (FMT, probiotics, antibiotics) on ICI outcomes; 4) Underlying immunological mechanisms (e.g., SCFAs, cross-reactivity); and 5) The role of the microbiome in ICI-induced colitis, including diagnostic markers and treatment options. The authors compiled data from tables summarizing key human-based studies to compare associations across different malignancies and ICI types.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive synthesis of current human and preclinical evidence linking specific gut microbial taxa to ICI efficacy and toxicity across major solid tumors (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC).\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it does not present new primary data or perform a systematic meta-analysis. The summary highlights inconsistencies in the literature (e.g., conflicting biomarkers for colitis) rather than resolving them.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Clarifies the mechanistic pathways through which the microbiome influences immunity, including PRR activation, SCFA production, and T-cell cross-reactivity.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Mechanistic details are largely derived from animal models or small-scale human correlative studies; the precise causal chains in humans remain partially understood and warrant further research.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Evaluates the clinical potential of microbiome interventions (FMT, probiotics, antibiotics avoidance) as adjuncts to ICI therapy.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Clinical trial data on interventions are limited in sample size and scope (mostly Phase 1/2 or case series). The review notes that large-scale randomized controlled trials are needed to confirm efficacy and safety.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Melanoma treated with anti-CTLA-4\",\n            \"finding\": \"Enrichment of Faecalibacterium genus and Firmicutes was associated with longer progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS), whereas Bacteroides enrichment was linked to poorer outcomes.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Metastatic melanoma treated with anti-PD-1\",\n            \"finding\": \"Responders showed higher abundance of Ruminococcaceae family members (specifically Faecalibacterium, Bifidobacterium longum, Collinsella aerofaciens, and Enterococcus faecium) compared to non-responders.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"NSCLC and RCC treated with anti-PD-1\",\n            \"finding\": \"Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment correlated with favorable clinical response. In NSCLC responders, Ruminococcus, Alistipes, and Eubacterium were enriched, while Bifidobacterium adolescentis and Parabacteroides distasonis were less abundant.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Pre-treatment antibiotic use\",\n            \"finding\": \"Prior use of broad-spectrum antibiotics (especially beta-lactams) up to 30 days before ICI therapy was consistently associated with worse OS, lower response rates, and shorter PFS across multiple cancer types, likely due to induced dysbiosis.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"ICI-induced colitis risk prediction\",\n            \"finding\": \"Evidence is inconsistent regarding predictive biomarkers. Some studies link Firmicutes (e.g., Faecalibacterium prausnitzii) to colitis occurrence, while others associate Bacteroidetes (e.g., Bacteroides species) with resistance to colitis. Decreased diversity and shifts in composition are generally observed in patients developing colitis.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Microbiome modulation via FMT/Probiotics\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT from ICI responders improved clinical response in refractory melanoma patients. Probiotic administration showed mixed results: no effect on survival in one melanoma study, but improved PFS in an RCC study with dual ICI. Animal models suggest certain probiotics (e.g., Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus reuteri) can mitigate colitis severity without compromising anti-tumor immunity.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"The paper addresses the complex interplay between the gut microbiome and ICI therapy. Specifically, it seeks to summarize how microbial composition affects ICI efficacy (anti-tumor response) and toxicity (immune-related colitis), and evaluate whether manipulating the microbiome can enhance therapeutic benefits while mitigating side effects.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review covers the relationship between the gut microbiome and immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) therapy, specifically focusing on anti-tumor efficacy and immune-related adverse events (irAEs), with a primary emphasis on immune-related colitis. The scope includes human clinical studies and preclinical animal models involving melanoma, non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and other solid tumors.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This 2023 review article synthesizes existing literature on how the gut microbiome influences the outcomes of ICI therapy. It first outlines the mechanisms of ICIs (targeting CTLA-4, PD-1, and PD-L1). It then reviews evidence linking specific microbial compositions to improved or reduced anti-tumor responses in various cancers. Subsequently, it discusses interventions such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotics, and antibiotics to modulate these effects. Finally, it examines the association between the gut microbiome and ICI-induced colitis, exploring potential predictive biomarkers and therapeutic strategies using microbiome modulation to manage this adverse event.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Contextual background on ICI mechanisms and the general concept of immune-related adverse events.\",\n            \"material\": \"Overview of CTLA-4 and PD-1/PD-L1 pathways, and the spectrum of irAEs (skin, GI, liver, endocrine).\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To establish the clinical setting where microbiome interactions occur and to define the scope of toxicity management.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Examples of clinical interventions targeting the microbiome.\",\n            \"material\": \"Summary of FMT trials in refractory melanoma, probiotic studies in melanoma/RCC, and antibiotic usage impacts.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To illustrate current clinical practices and experimental approaches to leveraging the microbiome, highlighting gaps in large-scale trial data.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Diagnostic criteria and management of ICI-induced colitis.\",\n            \"material\": \"Colonoscopic findings, histological features, and treatment algorithms (corticosteroids, biologics, FMT).\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To provide clinical context for the severity and management of GI irAEs, which are closely tied to microbiome status.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Associations vary by cancer type and ICI class. Evidence is primarily observational/correlational in humans; causality is often inferred from animal models. Sample sizes in cited studies are modest.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific gut microbial signatures associated with ICI efficacy. For example, Akkermansia muciniphila and Faecalibacterium are linked to better responses in NSCLC and melanoma, respectively. Conversely, prior antibiotic use is linked to reduced efficacy.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to answer 'How does the gut microbiome modulate efficacy?' by providing concrete examples of beneficial and detrimental microbial profiles and the impact of external factors like antibiotics.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Conflicting results across studies mean no single taxon is universally accepted as a predictor. The review explicitly states evidence is inconsistent.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Discusses the inconsistent predictive value of microbiome composition for ICI-induced colitis. Some studies link Firmicutes to colitis, while others link Bacteroidetes to resistance.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Useful for discussing the complexity of predicting irAEs and the lack of consensus on biomarkers for colitis risk.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Many mechanistic insights are derived from murine models (e.g., cross-reactivity of Bifidobacterium breve). Human validation is ongoing. The review notes that intricate mechanisms remain fully understood.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Outlines mechanistic pathways: 1) Activation of innate immunity via PRRs leading to adaptive immune response; 2) Production of metabolites like SCFAs influencing T-cell differentiation; 3) Molecular mimicry/cross-reactivity between commensal bacteria and tumor antigens.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Directly addresses 'what mechanisms mediate that effect?' by detailing biological processes linking microbes to immune activation and tumor control.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Data is largely from case reports or small animal studies. Clinical guidelines mention FMT as optional, indicating limited robust evidence.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Describes how microbiome modulation (FMT, probiotics) may alleviate ICI-induced colitis by restoring regulatory T cells or reducing pro-inflammatory populations.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides evidence for mechanistic links between microbiome restoration and immune tolerance in the context of irAEs.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review serves as a foundational source for mapping the bidirectional relationship between gut microbiota and ICI outcomes. It provides specific bacterial taxa associated with both positive tumor response and negative immune-related adverse events (colitis), offering concrete examples for mechanistic and clinical facets. It also summarizes intervention strategies (FMT, probiotics, antibiotics) and their observed impacts, which is critical for discussing modulation strategies in the broader review.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review aggregates findings from diverse cancer types (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC) and ICI classes; associations may not be universal across all solid tumors or drug targets.\",\n          \"Mechanistic claims are often based on preclinical models; direct translation to human pathophysiology should be treated with caution.\",\n          \"Predictive biomarkers for colitis are inconsistent; do not assume a single microbial signature predicts colitis risk.\",\n          \"Interventional data (FMT, probiotics) are from small studies or early-phase trials; efficacy is not definitively proven for routine clinical use.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Gut microbiome composition (taxa: Akkermansia, Faecalibacterium, Bacteroides, etc.)\",\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs: anti-CTLA-4, anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1)\",\n          \"Solid tumors (Melanoma, NSCLC, RCC)\",\n          \"Anti-tumor efficacy (Response rates, PFS, OS)\",\n          \"Immune-related adverse events (irAEs), specifically ICI-induced colitis\",\n          \"Microbiome modulation interventions (FMT, Probiotics, Antibiotics)\",\n          \"Mechanisms (SCFAs, PRR signaling, T-cell cross-reactivity)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\9c9ca2b5565671000a2be4e099d807fda5fb25ab\\\\2d9b9a39c26f55a8c3ca574d04662b4b\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0430\",\n      \"title\": \"Microbiome bacterial influencers of host immunity and response to immunotherapy\",\n      \"year\": \"2024\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.xcrm.2024.101487\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"bba4d44f2619609e877d370b4051f1127ed24a5d\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0430\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\bba4d44f2619609e877d370b4051f1127ed24a5d\\\\3017dbe0b2027bcdb6d0bebb20ddfece\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性综述方法，系统回顾并分类整理了关于肠道微生物群与ICI疗效关联的临床观察研究、动物模型实验及早期临床试验。分析路线包括：1) 阐述微生物-宿主免疫互作的基础机制；2) 按细菌门类/科属（如Lachnospiraceae, Ruminococcaceae, Bacteroidetes, Akkermansiaceae等）归纳其在不同癌症类型中对ICI疗效的正向或负向影响；3) 总结基于微生物群的干预手段（FMT、益生菌、饮食调整）的临床转化进展；4) 讨论领域内的不一致性及未来研究方向。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了肠道微生物群影响ICI疗效的全面机制框架，涵盖了从受体激活、代谢产物（如SCFAs、肌苷）到特定免疫细胞亚群调控的多层次路径。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，其结论依赖于已发表研究的汇总，无法控制原始研究的偏倚；对于某些细菌的具体作用机制（如Lachnospiraceae的部分菌株）尚不明确，且临床转化证据多来自小规模试验或动物模型。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"识别了多个潜在的生物标志物候选者（如A. muciniphila, Ruminococcaceae丰度），并评估了FMT和细菌联合体的治疗潜力。\",\n            \"limits\": \"未能确立标准化的微生物组分析协议，指出当前领域缺乏统一的数据处理标准，限制了跨研究的比较和临床应用的可靠性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"多种实体瘤（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、HCC等）接受抗PD-1/PD-L1或抗CTLA-4治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"特定有益菌群（如Ruminococcaceae, Lachnospiraceae, Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium）在响应者中富集，通常与CD8+ T细胞浸润增加、Treg抑制减弱或Th1型免疫反应增强相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"多种实体瘤接受ICI治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"某些拟杆菌属（Bacteroides spp.）成员在非响应者中更常见，可能与外周Treg和髓源性抑制细胞（MDSCs）频率增加及细胞因子反应减弱有关；但部分菌株（如B. fragilis）在特定背景下可能具有免疫刺激作用。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"临床前模型及部分临床试验\",\n            \"finding\": \"粪菌移植（FMT）从ICI响应者到非响应者患者可恢复对PD-1阻断剂的敏感性；特定益生菌（如Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus）在动物模型中能增强ICI疗效，但在人体中的确切疗效仍需更多前瞻性验证。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"不同研究队列和分析方法\",\n            \"finding\": \"微生物群组成与ICI疗效的关联存在显著异质性，受地理、饮食、抗生素使用、测序技术及数据分析流程的影响，导致关键生物标志物的检测缺乏一致性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管已知肠道微生物群影响ICI疗效，但具体是哪些细菌或代谢物起作用、其作用机制是什么、以及如何将这些发现转化为有效的辅助治疗策略，目前仍不完全清楚。综述旨在整合现有证据，阐明微生物驱动的反应机制，并为未来的靶向干预提供方向。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"人类肠道微生物群（Gut Microbiota）与宿主免疫系统及癌症免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs，如抗PD-1/PD-L1、抗CTLA-4）疗效之间的相互作用；涵盖多种实体瘤（黑色素瘤、非小细胞肺癌、肝细胞癌、结直肠癌等）。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇综述，旨在梳理肠道微生物群如何影响抗肿瘤免疫反应及ICIs的治疗效果。文章首先概述了肠道菌群通过模式识别受体（TLRs/NLRs）和代谢产物调节宿主免疫稳态的机制，随后重点分析了特定细菌类群（如厚壁菌门、拟杆菌门、阿克曼菌属等）在ICI治疗响应者与非响应者中的丰度差异及其潜在的免疫调节机制。此外，综述还评估了基于微生物群的干预策略（如粪菌移植FMT、益生菌、细菌联合体）在增强ICI疗效方面的临床前和临床研究证据，并指出了当前研究在标准化、功能导向及个体差异方面的局限性。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"领域现状与挑战\",\n            \"material\": \"综述指出当前微生物组研究面临的主要限制，包括样本收集、测序技术和数据分析方法的差异导致的异质性，以及缺乏标准化的分析工具。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述的背景部分，说明为何需要标准化协议，以及为何单一研究的发现可能难以复现，强调跨研究整合的重要性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"干预策略的比较\",\n            \"material\": \"对比了抗生素治疗、FMT、益生菌和细菌联合体等不同干预方式的效果和风险，指出FMT在克服耐药性方面的潜力，但也提示了抗生素预处理可能带来的负面影响。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论不同微生物干预策略的优劣，为选择最佳辅助治疗方案提供依据，同时提醒注意干预措施对患者基线微生物组的潜在破坏。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"未来研究方向\",\n            \"material\": \"建议未来研究应关注功能而非单纯的物种组成，设计针对特定功能的免疫刺激联合体，并探索宿主因素（如BMI、性别、饮食）对微生物定植和治疗反应的影响。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述的展望部分，提出后续研究的关键缺口，引导读者关注从描述性研究向机制性和功能性研究的转变。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些关联多为观察性研究结果，存在因果推断的局限；不同研究间的一致性较差，需结合其他证据谨慎解读。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"详细列出了在不同癌症类型（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、HCC、CRC等）中，与ICI疗效正相关或负相关的特定细菌类群及其丰度变化。例如，指出Ruminococcaceae、Lachnospiraceae、Akkermansiaceae通常在响应者中富集，而某些Bacteroides物种在非响应者中更常见。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于回答“哪些微生物特征与ICI疗效相关”的子问题，提供具体的细菌-疗效关联证据，支持建立微生物组生物标志物的假设。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"许多机制细节主要基于动物模型或体外实验，在人体中的验证程度不一；部分机制（如AhR激活）的具体配体和后果仍存在争议。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"阐述了微生物群影响ICI疗效的具体机制，包括：1) 通过TLR/NLR受体激活先天免疫；2) 产生代谢产物（如SCFAs、肌苷）调节T细胞功能和肿瘤微环境；3) 调节Treg和效应T细胞的平衡；4) 促进趋化因子分泌以招募T细胞至肿瘤部位。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"直接用于回答“介导机制是什么”的问题，提供分子和细胞层面的解释，帮助理解微生物如何通过免疫途径增强或抑制治疗效果。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"临床数据主要来自小规模试验或单臂研究，缺乏大规模随机对照试验的支持；干预方案（如菌株选择、剂量、给药时机）尚未标准化。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"评估了FMT、益生菌和细菌联合体作为ICI辅助治疗的临床前和早期临床证据。例如，提到FMT在难治性黑色素瘤患者中的成功案例，以及特定益生菌（如Bifidobacterium EDP150）在结直肠癌患者中的安全性及初步疗效。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于探讨微生物群干预策略的有效性和可行性，为“如何通过调节微生物群改善ICI疗效”提供实践参考。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物群如何调节实体瘤免疫治疗疗效提供了详细的机制分类和证据汇总。它特别适用于回答“微生物群如何调节ICI疗效”及其“介导机制”的问题，通过列举具体的细菌类群、代谢物和免疫通路，为构建微生物-免疫互作的综合图景提供了丰富的素材。同时，它也提供了关于干预策略（FMT、益生菌）的现状评估及领域局限性的背景信息。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述涵盖多种实体瘤，不同癌症类型的微生物-免疫互作可能存在特异性，应用时需考虑癌症类型的情境依赖性。\",\n          \"文中提到的许多机制证据来自动物模型，不能直接等同于人体内的生理过程，需谨慎外推。\",\n          \"微生物群与ICI疗效的关联存在显著的异质性，不同研究的结果可能因人群、技术或分析方法而异，不应视为普适规律。\",\n          \"作者声称的某些细菌的积极作用或消极作用，需结合具体研究背景和对照组进行判断，避免过度概括。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"肠道微生物群组成与ICI疗效的相关性（响应者vs非响应者）\",\n          \"特定细菌类群（厚壁菌门、拟杆菌门、阿克曼菌属、双歧杆菌属等）的作用\",\n          \"微生物代谢产物（短链脂肪酸、肌苷、胆汁酸）的免疫调节作用\",\n          \"微生物-宿主免疫互作机制（TLR/NLR信号、T细胞分化、Treg抑制、DC成熟）\",\n          \"基于微生物群的干预策略（FMT、益生菌、细菌联合体、饮食调整）\",\n          \"微生物组研究的异质性与标准化挑战\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\bba4d44f2619609e877d370b4051f1127ed24a5d\\\\3017dbe0b2027bcdb6d0bebb20ddfece\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0155\",\n      \"title\": \"Dietary compounds and patterns associated with immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) outcomes in advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC).\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1200/jco.2025.43.16_suppl.2567\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"448f3d956dc7cb74cd1bee3958ce828f79d4e1c7\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"这是一项观察性临床研究设计。首先，在CHUM微生物组中心，由营养师使用经过验证的DHQ-II问卷前瞻性收集147名晚期NSCLC患者的饮食史。其次，采用单变量和多变量Cox回归分析，考察全球饮食模式和72种宏微观营养素（以中位数为截断值）与PFS的关联，并调整BMI等协变量。同时，分析饮食与irAEs发生率的关联。最后，对69名患者的粪便样本进行鸟枪法宏基因组测序（WMS），利用线性判别分析、热图和MaAsLin2工具确定细菌丰度的差异，以建立饮食-微生物组-临床结局的联系。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了在晚期NSCLC人群中，特定饮食模式（特别是高脂/低淀粉）和单一营养素（维生素K）与ICI治疗PFS及irAEs关联的临床证据。\",\n            \"limits\": \"研究为观察性设计，无法确立因果关系；样本量相对较小（尤其是微生物组子集仅69人）；未控制所有潜在混杂因素（如抗生素使用、其他生活方式因素）；结果需在随机对照试验中验证。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"通过宏基因组测序揭示了饮食-微生物组-临床结局的潜在生物学机制线索，将临床表型与特定细菌丰度变化联系起来。\",\n            \"limits\": \"微生物组分析仅为相关性信号，未进行功能验证或因果推断；所发现的细菌标志物需在其他队列中复现。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"全队列（N=147），调整基础代谢率后\",\n            \"finding\": \"总热量摄入与PFS无显著关联（p = 0.3）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"单变量分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"维生素K（HR 0.62, p=0.03）和脂肪（HR 0.65, p=0.04）摄入与改善的PFS相关；淀粉（HR 1.61, p=0.03）、碳水化合物（HR 1.56, p=0.04）、蔗糖（HR 1.62, p=0.03）和铁（HR 1.7, p=0.016）摄入与较差的PFS相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"多变量分析（调整BMI）\",\n            \"finding\": \"维生素K摄入仍显著关联于改善的PFS（HR 0.60, 95% CI 0.37-0.97, p=0.04）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"饮食模式比较\",\n            \"finding\": \"基于脂肪的饮食（如类生酮饮食：高脂、低淀粉）在单变量（HR 0.47, p=0.008）和多变量分析（HR 0.37, 95% CI 0.2-0.68, p=0.001）中均与改善的PFS相关。相比之下，西方饮食（高脂、高淀粉）相比高淀粉饮食，与任何等级irAEs风险增加相关（54% vs 24%, p=0.01）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"微生物组关联（N=69）\",\n            \"finding\": \"基于脂肪的饮食与有益共生菌（如Ruminococcus lactaris, Butyricimonas faecihominis, Lachnospiraceae spp）的富集相关，低脂饮食与有害菌Veillonella atypica相关。基于淀粉的饮食与高Prevotella spp相关。富含蔗糖的饮食与已知发酵蔗糖的Candidatus saccharibacteria富集相关。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管已知肠道微生物组调节ICI活性且饮食是其主要影响因素，但此前关于高纤维饮食在NSCLC中的效果尚不明确（与黑色素瘤不同）。本研究旨在解决：特定的饮食模式（如高脂/低碳水化合物 vs 高淀粉）和具体营养素（如维生素K、铁、蔗糖等）如何影响晚期NSCLC患者ICI治疗的无进展生存期（PFS）及免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），以及这种影响是否由肠道微生物组介导。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究对象为接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）单药或联合化疗治疗的晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者。研究情境涉及临床营养评估、生存结局分析以及肠道微生物组测序，旨在探索饮食因素对ICI疗效及毒性的影响。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该研究针对既往发现高纤维饮食在黑色素瘤中有益但在NSCLC中无关联的差异，进一步探究饮食模式和特定营养素对NSCLC患者ICI治疗预后的影响。研究团队通过前瞻性收集147例患者的饮食历史数据，结合72种宏微观营养素的系统筛查，分析其与无进展生存期（PFS）及免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的关联。同时，对其中69例患者的粪便样本进行鸟枪法宏基因组测序，以揭示饮食与微生物组变化之间的生物学联系。结果显示，基于脂肪的饮食模式与改善的PFS相关，而维生素K摄入亦独立关联于更好的预后；相反，淀粉和蔗糖摄入与较差的PFS相关。微生物组分析支持了饮食通过调节肠道菌群（如增加有益菌或促进蔗糖发酵菌）来介导这些临床结果的假设。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于肠道微生物组调节固体肿瘤（特别是NSCLC）中ICI疗效的具体临床证据和机制假设。其核心价值在于区分了不同饮食模式（高脂vs高淀粉）对ICI预后的差异化影响，并通过微生物组测序数据提出了“饮食-菌群-疗效”的中介路径。这有助于回答综述中关于“微生物组如何调节ICI疗效”及“介导机制”的问题，特别是提供了来自NSCLC这一特定癌种的实证数据，填补了此前仅关注黑色素瘤或泛癌种的空白。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅限观察性关联，非因果证明；结果受BMI等因素影响；微生物组数据仅来自部分亚组。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了晚期NSCLC患者中，基于脂肪的饮食（高脂低淀粉）与显著改善的无进展生存期（PFS）相关的临床数据，以及高淀粉/蔗糖饮食与较差预后的关联。同时指出西方饮食（高脂高淀粉）可能增加免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的风险。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于论证饮食模式作为调节ICI疗效的可变因素，特别是在NSCLC背景下，高脂饮食可能优于传统高碳水饮食。可用于对比不同癌种（如黑色素瘤）中饮食影响的异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅为相关性分析，未证明这些细菌直接导致疗效改善；需要功能实验验证细菌的作用；样本量有限，可能存在批次效应或个体差异。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"通过宏基因组测序揭示了特定饮食与肠道菌群组成的关联：高脂饮食富集有益共生菌（如Ruminococcus lactaris, Lachnospiraceae spp），低脂饮食与潜在有害菌（Veillonella atypica）相关；高淀粉饮食与Prevotella spp相关；高蔗糖饮食与蔗糖发酵菌（Candidatus saccharibacteria）相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释饮食影响ICI疗效的潜在微生物组机制。这些细菌标志物可作为后续研究中验证“菌群介导疗效”的具体靶点或生物标志物。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供NSCLC特异性背景下的饮食-微生物组-ICI疗效关联实例。\",\n            \"material\": \"NSCLC患者中膳食纤维先前未被证实有效，但本研究发现其他饮食成分（脂肪、维生素K）有效，提示癌种特异性。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论不同实体瘤中饮食干预效果的异质性，避免将黑色素瘤结论外推至所有固体肿瘤。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供irAEs与饮食关联的新视角。\",\n            \"material\": \"西方饮食（高脂高淀粉）与更高的irAEs发生率相关。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于探讨饮食不仅影响疗效，还可能影响免疫治疗的安全性/毒性谱，丰富综述对ICI副作用管理的讨论。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究为观察性队列，发现的饮食与PFS/微生物组的关联不能直接解释为因果关系，需谨慎用于指导临床实践。\",\n          \"微生物组分析仅涵盖69名患者，代表性有限，且MaAsLin2等统计方法仅揭示丰度差异，未提供功能通路的确凿证据。\",\n          \"作者建议进行随机试验，表明当前证据等级不足以确立推荐饮食方案。\",\n          \"需注意“高脂饮食”在此研究中定义为“高脂低淀粉”，可能与经典的生酮饮食不完全等同，且长期安全性未知。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Dietary patterns (keto-like/high-fat vs high-starch/Western)\",\n          \"Specific nutrients (Vitamin K, Sucrose, Iron, Starch)\",\n          \"Gut microbiome composition (Ruminococcus lactaris, Prevotella spp., Veillonella atypica)\",\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor outcomes (PFS, irAEs)\",\n          \"Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0155\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\448f3d956dc7cb74cd1bee3958ce828f79d4e1c7\\\\a25b4dde9fd403f11f68d98e870214ad\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"provisional\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\448f3d956dc7cb74cd1bee3958ce828f79d4e1c7\\\\a25b4dde9fd403f11f68d98e870214ad\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0390\",\n      \"title\": \"Dietary fiber and probiotics influence the gut microbiome and melanoma immunotherapy response\",\n      \"year\": \"2021\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.aaz7015\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"a980ecd60c8c25767f7e60710e0949f181673c92\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"abstract\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用人类观察性研究与平行临床前动物实验相结合的设计。在人类部分，对438名黑色素瘤患者进行粪便微生物组谱分析，并评估其临床病理特征、饮食偏好及商业益生菌使用情况，重点分析128名接受ICB治疗患者的无进展生存期（PFS）。在动物部分，使用小鼠模型，给予低纤维饮食或益生菌补充，随后进行抗PD-1治疗，评估治疗效果及肿瘤微环境中的免疫细胞浸润情况（特别是IFN-γ+细胞毒性T细胞）。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了早期证据，表明特定的饮食模式（高纤维、避免益生菌）可能与黑色素瘤患者更好的ICB治疗反应相关，并初步揭示了可能的免疫机制（IFN-γ+ T细胞）。\",\n            \"limits\": \"所给摘要未交代具体的统计调整变量（如是否校正了抗生素使用、其他药物或基线微生物组差异），因此因果推断受限。动物模型的结果不能直接外推至人类复杂的饮食和微生物组环境。仅针对黑色素瘤和特定ICB药物（抗PD-1），结论在其他实体瘤或不同免疫疗法中的普适性未知。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"128名接受ICB治疗的黑色素瘤患者，高膳食纤维摄入 vs 低膳食纤维摄入\",\n            \"finding\": \"较高的膳食纤维摄入量与显著改善的无进展生存期（PFS）相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"128名接受ICB治疗的黑色素瘤患者，高膳食纤维摄入且无商业益生菌使用 vs 其他组合\",\n            \"finding\": \"在高纤维摄入且不使用益生菌的患者中观察到最显著的获益。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"小鼠模型，低纤维饮食或益生菌补充 + 抗PD-1治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"低纤维饮食或益生菌使用导致抗PD-1治疗反应受损，表现为肿瘤微环境中干扰素-γ（IFN-γ）阳性细胞毒性T细胞频率降低。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管已知肠道细菌可调节免疫检查点阻断（ICB）的治疗反应，但饮食（特别是膳食纤维）和商业益生菌补充剂如何具体影响这一相互作用尚不清楚。本研究试图明确这些饮食相关因素是否以及如何影响免疫治疗的临床结局及其潜在机制。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"黑色素瘤患者（观察性队列，n=438，其中128名接受免疫检查点阻断治疗ICB）及平行的小鼠模型；研究情境为评估饮食纤维摄入、益生菌补充剂使用与肠道微生物组特征及对ICB治疗反应的影响。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该研究旨在探讨饮食因素（膳食纤维和益生菌）如何通过调节肠道微生物组影响癌症患者的免疫治疗反应。作者通过观察性研究分析了黑色素瘤患者的饮食记录和临床结果，并结合小鼠实验验证了低纤维饮食或益生菌使用对抗PD-1疗法反应的负面影响。研究发现高纤维摄入且不使用益生菌的患者预后更好，机制上涉及肿瘤微环境中干扰素-γ阳性细胞毒性T细胞的频率变化。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于饮食干预（纤维和益生菌）如何调节免疫检查点抑制剂疗效的具体实证数据。它连接了宏观饮食行为与微观免疫机制（T细胞浸润），特别指出了益生菌可能具有负面影响的反直觉发现，这对于理解非抗生素类微生物调节因素至关重要。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅限黑色素瘤队列；观察性设计，存在混杂因素风险；未提供微生物组物种层面的详细变化机制，仅关联临床结局。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了饮食因素（高纤维、无益生菌）与黑色素瘤患者ICB治疗反应（PFS改善）之间的正相关观察数据。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于综述中讨论饮食作为免疫治疗辅助因素的实证案例，特别是支持‘有益饮食模式’的观点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"基于小鼠模型，机制链条（从纤维/益生菌到T细胞）在人类中尚未完全证实；仅涉及抗PD-1通路；IFN-γ+ T细胞仅为单一指标，未涵盖其他免疫细胞亚群或细胞因子网络。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"在小鼠模型中揭示了低纤维饮食或益生菌使用导致抗肿瘤免疫反应受损的机制线索：肿瘤微环境中IFN-γ+细胞毒性T细胞频率降低。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释饮食/补充剂影响免疫治疗效果的潜在免疫学机制，即通过调节效应T细胞的浸润或功能来影响疗效。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"强调在研究微生物组-免疫治疗关系时，必须严格控制饮食和药物（包括益生菌）等环境变量，否则可能产生偏差。\",\n            \"material\": \"指出宿主遗传对微生物组变异贡献<10%，而环境和饮食/药物是主要塑造因素。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景与概念澄清\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"挑战‘益生菌总是有益’的常识，提示在免疫治疗期间，某些商业益生菌产品可能干扰内源性微生物组的有益作用或竞争定植位点，需区分益生元（纤维）与益生菌的不同影响。\",\n            \"material\": \"商业益生菌补充剂的使用与较差的ICB反应相关（尤其在低纤维背景下）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"反例或复杂情境实例\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究为观察性关联，不能确立因果关系。\",\n          \"益生菌的负面影响可能依赖于特定的菌株、剂量或宿主基线微生物组状态，摘要未提供细节。\",\n          \"小鼠模型的饮食控制和无菌/特定菌群条件可能与人类日常饮食环境存在巨大差异，外推需谨慎。\",\n          \"仅关注黑色素瘤，结果可能不适用于其他实体瘤类型。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"dietary_fiber_intake\",\n          \"probiotic_supplements\",\n          \"immune_checkpoint_blockade_ICB\",\n          \"melanoma\",\n          \"gut_microbiome_profiling\",\n          \"progression_free_survival_PFS\",\n          \"interferon-gamma_IFN_gamma_positive_cytotoxic_T_cells\",\n          \"tumor_microenvironment_TME\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0390\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\a980ecd60c8c25767f7e60710e0949f181673c92\\\\d7563bfca9f94ed81642114ad56446e9\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"input_attributed\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_not_scholarly_body\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_challenge_or_login\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_challenge_or_login\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"only abstract or non-title snippets were available\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"abstract_plus_snippets\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\a980ecd60c8c25767f7e60710e0949f181673c92\\\\d7563bfca9f94ed81642114ad56446e9\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0249\",\n      \"title\": \"Abstract 3523: A Black Raspberry dietary intervention to modify the gut microbiome and improve the response to immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n      \"year\": \"2022\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1158/1538-7445.am2022-3523\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"679c73db5e9e6e870722a218bd303c18770e8cdf\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"abstract\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用多阶段混合方法：1) 临床前小鼠模型：使用含5%冻干黑覆盆子粉的合成饮食喂养小鼠，检测肠道菌群变化；2) 人体临床试验（BEWELL Study）：设计为安慰剂对照、随机、交叉试验，纳入96名具有>30包年吸烟史且年龄55-77岁的高危肺癌人群，每日摄入2x80mL黑覆盆子花蜜4周，收集前后肠道微生物组及血液/尿液样本进行分析；3) 转化验证：将BEWELL研究中受试者的粪便移植至无菌或特定条件下的小鼠（C57BL/6J），构建人源化微生物组Avatar模型，皮下注射mc38结肠癌细胞，并给予抗PD-1抗体或同型对照，比较干预前后菌群移植组的肿瘤体积差异。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了黑覆盆子饮食干预影响人体肠道微生物组的初步证据，并建立了从人体菌群到小鼠肿瘤模型的转化验证路径\",\n            \"limits\": \"人体试验中目标菌种A. muciniphila未达到统计学显著性；肿瘤抑制结果来自初步实验（Preliminary experiments），样本量和统计效力有限；未直接证明机制，仅展示相关性\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"确认了黑覆盆子干预能引起非目标菌群的改变，提示其对微生物组有广泛影响\",\n            \"limits\": \"具体哪些菌群变化导致了肿瘤抑制效果尚不明确，缺乏详细的机制解析\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"在高危肺癌风险人群中验证了饮食干预的安全性和可行性（隐含于试验完成）\",\n            \"limits\": \"受试者为高危人群而非已确诊癌症患者，其生理状态可能与晚期癌症患者存在差异，外推至治疗场景需谨慎\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"小鼠模型，饮食含5%冻干黑覆盆子粉\",\n            \"finding\": \"增加了Akkermansia muciniphila的丰度，该菌属在黑色素瘤中与改善ICI响应相关\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"人体BEWELL试验，96名高危肺癌风险人群，每日2x80mL黑覆盆子花蜜，持续4周\",\n            \"finding\": \"未观察到A. muciniphila的显著变化（logistic regression with negative binomial Wald test p-value 0.056），但观察到其他分类群的变化\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"人源化小鼠Avatar模型，移植干预后的人体粪便，接种mc38结肠癌细胞，给予抗PD-1治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"初步实验显示，接受干预后菌群移植的小鼠肿瘤体积小于接受干预前菌群移植的小鼠（相对于同型对照，t-test, p-value 0.05）\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"主要问题是：如何识别并实施策略来操纵宿主肠道微生物组，从而提高实体瘤患者对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）的治疗反应？动机在于目前仅约20%的肿瘤对ICI产生响应，且已有数据表明ICI响应可能依赖于宿主微生物组。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究对象包括小鼠模型（C57BL/6J）和人类受试者（高肺癌风险人群）。研究情境涉及饮食干预对肠道微生物组的影响，以及这种改变对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效的潜在调节作用。范围限定于黑覆盆子（Black Raspberry, BRB）作为单一饮食干预手段。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该研究旨在探索通过饮食干预调节肠道微生物组以改善实体瘤对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）反应的可能性。研究首先在小鼠模型中验证冻干黑覆盆子粉能增加与ICI响应相关的Akkermansia muciniphila丰度。随后，开展了一项名为BEWELL的人体随机交叉对照试验，招募96名高危肺癌人群，每日饮用两次80mL黑覆盆子花蜜持续4周。结果显示，尽管未观察到A. muciniphila的显著变化，但其他菌群发生改变。最后，通过将干预后的人体粪便移植到小鼠体内构建“人体微生物组Avatar模型”，并接种结肠癌细胞进行抗PD-1治疗，初步发现接受干预后菌群的小鼠肿瘤生长受到抑制。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文提供了关于特定植物源性饮食成分（黑覆盆子）通过重塑肠道微生物组增强免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效的实证数据。虽然人体层面未显著改变关键指示菌Akkermansia，但通过人源化小鼠模型证实了干预后菌群具有促进抗肿瘤免疫反应的潜力。这对于综述中讨论‘饮食干预作为ICI辅助疗法’及‘微生物组介导的免疫调节机制’具有重要参考价值，特别是展示了从人体观察到人源动物模型验证的完整链条。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"人体试验中A. muciniphila变化不显著，说明并非所有饮食干预都通过单一菌种起作用；结果基于初步实验，需警惕过度解读因果性。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了黑覆盆子饮食干预对人体肠道微生物组组成的实际影响数据，以及这种改变在体外（小鼠Avatar模型）对ICI疗效的潜在正向调节作用。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于综述中‘饮食因素对微生物组及ICI疗效的影响’部分，作为植物多酚/浆果类饮食干预的具体案例。特别适用于讨论那些虽未显著改变标志性菌种但整体菌群功能可能发生有利转变的情况。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"该模型本身是工具而非机制解释；具体的分子机制（如抗原呈递、T细胞活化等）在本摘要中未详细阐述，仅表现为肿瘤体积减小。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"展示了利用‘人体微生物组Avatar模型’（Human microbiome avatar models）来桥接人体菌群变化与肿瘤免疫反应之间关系的实验设计。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于综述中‘机制研究方法与模型’部分，介绍如何通过人源化动物模型验证人体菌群对ICI响应的功能性影响，弥补单纯相关性研究的不足。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"背景与实例支持\",\n            \"material\": \"BEWELL Study的设计细节（随机、交叉、高危人群）及黑覆盆子花蜜的具体剂量（2x80mL/day, 4 weeks）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"为综述提供饮食干预临床试验的方法学参考和具体参数示例，帮助读者理解此类研究的实际操作难度和设计特点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"对比与反例思考\",\n            \"material\": \"人体中A. muciniphila无显著变化 vs 小鼠模型中肿瘤抑制效果。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提示在综述中讨论‘微生物组标志物预测疗效’的局限性：即人体菌群组成变化与功能输出（肿瘤控制）之间可能存在解耦，或需要更复杂的菌群互作网络而非单一菌种丰度来解释。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"作者声称的结果基于‘Preliminary experiments’，统计显著性（p=0.05）处于临界值，且未提供效应量或置信区间，规划时需将其视为探索性发现而非确证性结论。\",\n          \"人体受试者为‘high risk of developing lung cancer’而非已患癌并接受ICI治疗的患者，其免疫状态和肿瘤微环境与目标综述对象（实体瘤患者）存在本质差异，外推至治疗效果时需明确这一情境限制。\",\n          \"摘要未交代具体的微生物组测序分析方法、批次效应处理及多重检验校正细节，引用其菌群变化数据时应保持谨慎。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Black Raspberry (BRB) dietary intervention\",\n          \"Gut microbiome modulation by diet\",\n          \"Akkermansia muciniphila abundance\",\n          \"Human microbiome avatar models\",\n          \"Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) response in solid tumors\",\n          \"Anti-PD-1 therapy efficacy\",\n          \"mc38 colon cancer model\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0249\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\679c73db5e9e6e870722a218bd303c18770e8cdf\\\\121878364a274aef4620b0273e82f276\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"input_attributed\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"no complete research body was indexed\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"only abstract or non-title snippets were available\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"abstract_only\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\679c73db5e9e6e870722a218bd303c18770e8cdf\\\\121878364a274aef4620b0273e82f276\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0593\",\n      \"title\": \"Single-cell-based identification of drug synergy with immunotherapy via tumor microenvironment remodeling\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2025-014132\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:286644045\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"研究采用多步骤计算与实验结合的策略：1) 利用TCGA bulk RNA-seq数据和ImmuCellAI去卷积算法，鉴定与免疫浸润负相关或正相关的TIIMGs；2) 使用L1000高通量筛选平台，针对TIIMGs筛选739种化合物的转录组扰动效应，并通过drug-to-cell recall验证；3) 在CT26小鼠模型中进行体内给药，收集肿瘤组织进行scRNA-seq，利用NMF算法从肿瘤细胞中提取异质性程序并聚类为12个MPs；4) 开发MP评分算法（结合Cluster 1激活与Cluster 2抑制的加权）量化TME重塑程度；5) 基于MP评分优先选择候选药物（oxypurinol及其前体allopurinol），通过流式细胞术、LC-MS/MS检测肌苷水平、体外共培养及体内协同实验验证机制；6) 回顾性分析人类膀胱癌患者队列中抗痛风药与抗PD-1治疗的生存关联。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"建立了首个基于单细胞分辨率的药物诱导TME重塑量化框架（resTMe），定义了12个TME元程序（MPs）和MP评分算法，为预测药物-免疫疗法协同作用提供了新工具。\",\n            \"limits\": \"核心scRNA-seq数据仅来源于小鼠模型（CT26），缺乏人源类器官或PDX模型的直接验证，限制了框架在人类复杂TME中的直接外推性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"发现了别嘌醇（Allopurinol）作为抗PD-1疗法的潜在增效剂，阐明了其通过嘌呤补救途径增加肌苷分泌、增强肿瘤免疫原性和T细胞功能的分子机制。\",\n            \"limits\": \"机制验证主要在体外细胞系和小鼠模型中进行；人类临床证据仅来自小样本（n=50）、单中心的回顾性观察性研究，存在混杂因素风险，需前瞻性临床试验确认。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"证实了MDSCs的耗竭是响应性TME的关键标志物，并揭示了低剂量药物可能通过非细胞毒性机制重塑TME以促进免疫浸润。\",\n            \"limits\": \"对于其他候选药物（如epirubicin, vemurafenib）的具体分子机制阐述不如AP深入；不同药物对特定免疫亚群的影响差异较大，尚未形成统一的普适性规则。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"8种癌症类型TCGA数据分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"鉴定出肿瘤细胞特异性免疫浸润调节基因（TIIMGs），其中COAD拥有最多的dpGenes（792个），KIRC最少，揭示了不同癌症间TME调控网络的异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"L1000平台筛选739种化合物\",\n            \"finding\": \"识别出54种在多癌种中重复出现的候选药物，主要靶向CDKs、HDAC1、PI3K等通路。低剂量处理更倾向于产生非细胞毒性的免疫调节效应，而高剂量常伴随免疫抑制。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"CT26小鼠模型scRNA-seq分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"药物处理导致肿瘤细胞比例下降，免疫细胞（特别是巨噬细胞、DCs）浸润增加，但MDSCs显著减少。oxypurinol表现出最强的TME重塑能力，显著降低MDSCs并增加抗原呈递相关细胞。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"MP评分算法应用\",\n            \"finding\": \"定义了12个MPs，其中Cluster 2（含MPs 1,3,6,8,10,12）与抗原加工和呈递通路高度相关。oxypurinol获得最高MP评分，提示其具有最强的TME重塑潜力。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Allopurinol (AP) 联合抗PD-1治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"AP通过抑制黄嘌呤脱氢酶（XDH）增加肌苷合成并分泌至胞外，肌苷上调肿瘤细胞MHC I类和PD-L1表达，增强CD8+ T细胞介导的细胞毒性。在CT26模型中，AP联合抗PD-1实现4/6小鼠完全治愈。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"人类膀胱癌回顾性队列（n=50）\",\n            \"finding\": \"在接受抗PD-1治疗的膀胱癌患者中，同时服用抗痛风药物（如AP或febuxostat）的患者组（n=7）的总生存期（OS）显著优于未服用组（n=43）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"如何在复杂的肿瘤微环境中系统性地识别能够增强免疫疗法疗效的药物组合？目前缺乏量化药物诱导TME重塑的系统框架，且难以预测哪些非抗癌药物可作为免疫治疗的增效剂。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究聚焦于实体瘤（以小鼠结肠癌CT26模型和人类膀胱癌队列为主）的肿瘤微环境（TME），探讨药物诱导的TME重塑如何影响免疫检查点抑制剂（如抗PD-1/PD-L1）的疗效。研究对象包括8种癌症类型的TCGA数据、739种免疫调节化合物、小鼠体内的单细胞转录组数据以及临床回顾性队列。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该研究旨在解决缺乏系统性框架来量化药物诱导的TME重塑的问题。作者首先通过TCGA数据和ImmuCellAI算法鉴定了肿瘤细胞特异性免疫浸润调节基因（TIIMGs），并利用L1000平台筛选出能显著调控这些基因的化合物。随后，在小鼠CT26肿瘤模型中，通过单细胞RNA测序（scRNA-seq）分析了多种药物处理后的TME变化，识别出12个与TME重塑相关的元程序（Meta-programs, MPs）。基于MP富集情况开发了MP评分算法，用于量化TME对药物的响应程度。该算法成功鉴定出别嘌醇（Allopurinol, AP），一种痛风药物，发现其能通过促进肌苷（inosine）的合成与分泌，上调肿瘤细胞的MHC I类和PD-L1表达，从而增强CD8+ T细胞的杀伤活性。在体内实验中，低剂量AP联合抗PD-1治疗实现了4/6的小鼠完全肿瘤清除。此外，一项包含50名膀胱癌患者的回顾性研究显示，接受抗痛风药物治疗的患者在抗PD-1治疗下的总生存期显著优于未使用者。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于‘非传统免疫调节药物’（老药新用）如何通过代谢重编程和TME重塑增强ICI疗效的具体案例和机制解释。它不直接涉及肠道菌群，但提供了一个重要的对比维度：即宿主代谢状态（如痛风药物干预）和肿瘤局部代谢产物（肌苷）如何独立于或协同于微生物组影响ICI疗效。其建立的MP评分框架可用于归类不同药物对TME细胞组成的影响模式。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"该研究未涉及肠道菌群，因此不能直接回答菌群如何调制ICI疗效的问题，只能作为平行机制或交互作用的潜在背景。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了非微生物来源的代谢物（肌苷）增强ICI疗效的证据。研究发现别嘌醇通过增加肌苷分泌，增强CD8+ T细胞功能，这与某些肠道菌群代谢产物（如丁酸盐、次级胆汁酸）调节免疫微环境的机制形成互补或对比视角。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于讨论除了微生物组之外，宿主自身代谢干预和肿瘤局部代谢环境如何调节ICI疗效。可作为‘代谢微环境’章节的重要素材。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制仅限于别嘌醇-肌苷轴，不能推广为所有ICI增效剂的通用机制。人类证据仅为相关性，因果链条在人体中尚待确证。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"详细阐述了别嘌醇增强ICI疗效的具体分子机制：抑制XDH -> 增加肌苷 -> 上调MHC I/PD-L1 -> 增强T细胞杀伤。同时指出MDSCs减少是响应性TME的标志。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释‘代谢产物介导的免疫增强’这一具体机制路径。可与菌群代谢产物（如短链脂肪酸）的作用机制进行比较，区分内源性代谢物与外源性（菌群）代谢物的不同作用靶点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"基于小鼠模型数据，人类组织中MDSCs的功能异质性更大，需谨慎外推。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了MDSCs耗竭作为ICI响应生物标志物的实证支持。在scRNA-seq分析中，响应性TME显著特征为MDSCs比例降低。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中关于‘免疫抑制细胞群体在ICI耐药中的作用’或‘预测ICI响应的生物标志物’部分。可与其他研究对比不同免疫抑制细胞（如Tregs, TAMs）在ICI疗效中的权重。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供药物重定位（Drug Repurposing）在免疫肿瘤学中的应用实例。\",\n            \"material\": \"别嘌醇（痛风药）被鉴定为抗PD-1增效剂，并在回顾性临床队列中显示生存获益。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论现有非抗癌药物如何被重新评估用于增强ICI疗效，拓展‘哪些因素调制ICI疗效’的范围 beyond 微生物组和宿主遗传背景。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供TME细胞组成变化的定量分析框架。\",\n            \"material\": \"MP评分算法和12个元程序的定义，量化了抗原呈递、盐应激反应等生物学过程在药物处理后的变化。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为方法论参考，说明如何从单细胞数据中提取TME重塑的特征，用于比较不同干预手段（包括可能的菌群移植或饮食干预）对TME细胞景观的影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供代谢物（肌苷）直接调节T细胞功能的体外和体内证据。\",\n            \"material\": \"AP处理导致肌苷大量分泌，增强OT-I T细胞对B16-OVA肿瘤的杀伤力。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于构建‘代谢-免疫’互作网络，可能与某些产生类似代谢物的菌群菌株的作用机制形成对照或协同讨论。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究的核心对象是小鼠模型和人类膀胱癌回顾性队列，未直接研究肠道菌群。在将其用于回答‘菌群如何调制ICI’问题时，必须明确其作为‘非菌群代谢机制’的对比或补充角色，而非直接证据。\",\n          \"人类临床数据仅为小样本回顾性观察（n=50），存在选择偏倚和混杂因素，不能作为因果关系的确凿证据，仅能作为假设生成的线索。\",\n          \"别嘌醇的机制特异性强（肌苷轴），不能将其效果泛化为所有代谢调节药物或所有ICI增效剂的共同机制。\",\n          \"MP评分和元程序是基于小鼠CT26模型定义的，在不同癌症类型或人源模型中的适用性需进一步验证。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Tumor Microenvironment (TME) Remodeling\",\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs) Synergy\",\n          \"Metabolic Reprogramming (Inosine/Purine Salvage Pathway)\",\n          \"Myeloid-Derived Suppressor Cells (MDSCs) Depletion\",\n          \"Drug Repurposing for Oncology\",\n          \"Single-cell Transcriptomics in Drug Response\",\n          \"Antigen Presentation (MHC Class I)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study developed a meta-program (MP) scoring algorithm based on scRNA-seq data from mouse tumors to quantify TME remodeling, identifying myeloid-derived suppressor cell (MDSC) depletion as a hallmark of responsive TMEs. It established a resource (scIMD) and web tool (resTMe) for screening immunomodulatory drugs. The analysis covered eight cancer types for gene identification but performed in vivo validation primarily in the CT26 colon carcinoma model.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_C2_inosine_tme\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Use this study to support the mechanistic plausibility of inosine-mediated ICI enhancement and to cite indirect human clinical evidence linking xanthine oxidase inhibition to improved survival. However, explicitly state that direct human TME inosine quantification and concentration-response correlation data remain unavailable in this source. Future search strategies should target metabolomics studies profiling human tumor interstitial fluid or tissue specifically for purine metabolites in ICI-treated cohorts.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"This material does not provide the absolute physiological concentration of inosine within the human tumor microenvironment (TME); the concentration data reported is from mouse cell culture supernatants. Furthermore, it does not report a direct statistical correlation between measured human TME inosine levels and ICI efficacy (e.g., ORR, PFS, OS). The human clinical evidence provided is an association between gout medication usage (as a proxy for inosine elevation) and survival, not a correlation based on quantified inosine metabolite levels in patient tissues or fluids. The study explicitly notes its limitation that drug scRNA-seq data were derived from mouse models.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study investigated the synergistic effect of allopurinol (AP) with anti-PD-1 therapy. In mouse CT26 cell cultures, LC-MS/MS analysis showed AP treatment increased extracellular inosine levels by approximately 500 to 1000-fold in supernatants, while cell lysate levels remained unchanged, indicating active export. Mechanistically, AP inhibits xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), elevating hypoxanthine which is converted to inosine via the salvage pathway. In vivo, AP combined with anti-PD-1 achieved tumor-free outcomes in 4/6 mice. In a retrospective human cohort of 50 bladder cancer patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy, the 7 patients concurrently taking gout medications (AP or febuxostat) showed significantly better overall survival compared to the 43 patients who did not. The authors propose that elevated inosine enhances tumor immunogenicity (upregulating MHC class I and PD-L1) and supports T-cell function.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study developed a meta-program (MP) scoring algorithm based on scRNA-seq data from mouse tumors to quantify TME remodeling, identifying myeloid-derived suppressor cell (MDSC) depletion as a hallmark of responsive TMEs. It established a resource (scIMD) and web tool (resTMe) for screening immunomodulatory drugs. The analysis covered eight cancer types for gene identification but performed in vivo validation primarily in the CT26 colon carcinoma model.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_C2_inosine_tme\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Use this study to support the mechanistic plausibility of inosine-mediated ICI enhancement and to cite indirect human clinical evidence linking xanthine oxidase inhibition to improved survival. However, explicitly state that direct human TME inosine quantification and concentration-response correlation data remain unavailable in this source. Future search strategies should target metabolomics studies profiling human tumor interstitial fluid or tissue specifically for purine metabolites in ICI-treated cohorts.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"This material does not provide the absolute physiological concentration of inosine within the human tumor microenvironment (TME); the concentration data reported is from mouse cell culture supernatants. Furthermore, it does not report a direct statistical correlation between measured human TME inosine levels and ICI efficacy (e.g., ORR, PFS, OS). The human clinical evidence provided is an association between gout medication usage (as a proxy for inosine elevation) and survival, not a correlation based on quantified inosine metabolite levels in patient tissues or fluids. The study explicitly notes its limitation that drug scRNA-seq data were derived from mouse models.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study investigated the synergistic effect of allopurinol (AP) with anti-PD-1 therapy. In mouse CT26 cell cultures, LC-MS/MS analysis showed AP treatment increased extracellular inosine levels by approximately 500 to 1000-fold in supernatants, while cell lysate levels remained unchanged, indicating active export. Mechanistically, AP inhibits xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), elevating hypoxanthine which is converted to inosine via the salvage pathway. In vivo, AP combined with anti-PD-1 achieved tumor-free outcomes in 4/6 mice. In a retrospective human cohort of 50 bladder cancer patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy, the 7 patients concurrently taking gout medications (AP or febuxostat) showed significantly better overall survival compared to the 43 patients who did not. The authors propose that elevated inosine enhances tumor immunogenicity (upregulating MHC class I and PD-L1) and supports T-cell function.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0593\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-d48cd64bd12b302ad2ba\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-95bd9a77b14edf06222c\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a7194a689f46f19f0fc9\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-5f82ba52a99d38e52a55\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-5bcddf895da96806b16c\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-c44cb221036f78d05573\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"missing\": [\n              \"jitc-14-3-g001.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g002.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g003.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g004.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g005.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g006.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s001.docx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s002.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s003.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s004.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s005.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s006.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s007.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s008.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s009.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s010.tif\"\n            ],\n            \"reason\": \"graphic_members_unmatched\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementary_bundle_had_no_safe_matching_images\",\n            \"status\": \"unparsed\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0224\",\n      \"title\": \"From Alpha Diversity to Zeitgebers: Functional Implications and Potential Mechanisms Linking the Gut Microbiome to Sleep and Circadian Disruption (Part II)\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.neubiorev.2026.106609\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"5dbcf9d0db62adc57ef25ab84c8b892e6281343d\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"structured_partial\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"A scoping review methodology was used to select and synthesize preclinical and human studies. Studies were included if they assessed sleep/circadian disruption, gut microbiome outcomes, and at least one functional biomarker (hormones, metabolites, neurotransmitters, or inflammation). Data were stratified by sample type (plasma/serum, GI tissue, brain tissue, etc.) and categorized using hierarchical databases (HMDB, KEGG, LIPID MAPS). Results were grouped by intervention type (sleep disruption vs. circadian disruption) and biomarker class to identify convergent functional signatures.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive synthesis of functional biomarker responses to sleep/circadian disruption across multiple biological compartments, highlighting convergent metabolic and inflammatory signatures despite taxonomic variability.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Most included studies are observational or cross-sectional, limiting causal inference. Human data are sparse and heterogeneous. The review does not establish direct causal mediation by specific taxa but rather associations. Specific mechanisms like kynurenine pathway metabolites were noted as under-measured in current literature.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Preclinical sleep disruption\",\n            \"finding\": \"Increased HPA axis activity (CRH, ACTH, cortisol/corticosterone); decreased GABA, serotonin, melatonin; increased pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α); decreased fecal SCFAs; downregulated amino acid and lipid metabolism; upregulated glycolysis/TCA cycle.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Preclinical circadian disruption\",\n            \"finding\": \"Increased primary bile acids and decreased secondary bile acids; increased LPS biosynthesis and bacterial virulence pathways; localized GI inflammation (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-17).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Human studies\",\n            \"finding\": \"Limited data; generally paralleled preclinical inflammatory responses (increased IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α, LPS); decreased fecal SCFAs associated with poor sleep quality/short duration; conflicting cortisol responses compared to rodents.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To synthesize evidence on how sleep and circadian disruption affect not just the composition but the functional capacity of the gut microbiome and host physiology, specifically identifying mechanistic pathways (neuroendocrine, immune, metabolic) that link these systems.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review synthesizes preclinical (rodent) and translational (human) studies examining the bidirectional relationship between sleep/circadian disruption and the gut microbiome. It focuses on functional biomarkers—hormones, neurotransmitters, inflammatory mediators, metabolites, and functional genes—in addition to microbial composition.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This scoping review (Part II of a series) integrates findings from 83 studies (59 rodent, 24 human) that measured functional biomarkers alongside sleep/microbiome outcomes. It identifies consistent mechanistic links between sleep disruption and host homeostasis changes mediated by the microbiome, including HPA axis activation, altered bile acid metabolism, inflammation-related shifts, and disrupted tryptophan pathways. The authors argue that while taxonomic responses vary, functional signatures converge, suggesting metabolic and signaling biomarkers are more stable indicators of microbiome involvement in sleep pathology than composition alone.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This paper provides a detailed mechanistic framework linking sleep/circadian disruption to gut microbiome function via neuroendocrine, immune, and metabolic pathways. While it does not directly address Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs), it offers critical background on how sleep dysregulation alters the gut environment (inflammation, barrier integrity, metabolite profiles) which may indirectly influence systemic immunity and potentially tumor microenvironment interactions relevant to solid tumors.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Does not mention cancer or ICIs. The link to ICI efficacy is inferential based on general immunomodulatory effects.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Describes how sleep/circadian disruption creates a pro-inflammatory gut environment (increased LPS, cytokines, reduced SCFAs) and alters barrier integrity. This context is relevant for understanding baseline immune status which could modulate ICI efficacy.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to discuss how lifestyle factors like sleep deprivation might create a systemic inflammatory milieu that could theoretically antagonize anti-tumor immunity or alter the tumor microenvironment.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms described are in the context of sleep pathology, not cancer immunotherapy. Direct applicability to ICI resistance/sensitivity is not established.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details specific mechanisms: HPA axis hyperactivity, TLR4/LPS signaling, FXR/TGR5 bile acid receptor signaling, and AhR activation by indoles. These are potential molecular targets or mediators of host-microbe-immune interactions.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to hypothesize mechanisms by which microbiome-derived signals (e.g., SCFAs, bile acids, indoles) might regulate immune cell function (e.g., Tregs, Th17, macrophages) relevant to checkpoint blockade response.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Background on host-microbe-immune axis regulation\",\n            \"material\": \"Sleep disruption leads to increased systemic inflammation (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α) and LPS translocation, reducing SCFA production and altering bile acid ratios.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To provide context on how non-pharmacological factors (sleep) can induce systemic inflammation and metabolic shifts that may confound or interact with ICI efficacy. Highlights the importance of considering host physiological state (inflammation, barrier function) when interpreting microbiome-ICI relationships.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Metabolite-mediated immune modulation\",\n            \"material\": \"Secondary bile acids suppress Th17 cells via RORγt; SCFAs promote Treg differentiation; Indoles activate AhR.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To illustrate specific microbial metabolites that have known immunomodulatory effects. These pathways (bile acids, SCFAs, indoles) are candidates for mediating microbiome effects on immune responses, which may be relevant to ICI mechanisms.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The paper focuses on sleep/circadian disruption, not cancer or ICI treatment. Any connection to ICI efficacy must be inferred from general immunological principles.\",\n          \"Human data on functional biomarkers in the context of sleep are limited; most mechanistic details come from rodent models.\",\n          \"The review emphasizes association over causation; do not present these findings as proven causal drivers of ICI outcomes.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"HPA axis activation and stress hormones\",\n          \"Pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α)\",\n          \"Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) and gut barrier integrity\",\n          \"Bile acid metabolism (primary vs. secondary)\",\n          \"Tryptophan metabolism (kynurenine vs. indole/serotonin pathways)\",\n          \"Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) translocation and endotoxemia\",\n          \"Neurotransmitter modulation (GABA, serotonin)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch4.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0224\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5dbcf9d0db62adc57ef25ab84c8b892e6281343d\\\\b82a7cd4a2336b03a57c0a020e83e9ac\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_empty\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_not_scholarly_body\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"known_losses\": [\n              \"headings/tables/math/image pixels are not preserved by this plain HTML projection\"\n            ],\n            \"reason\": \"body converted to structured material; headings/tables/math/image pixels may be lost\",\n            \"status\": \"derived\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"structured_partial\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5dbcf9d0db62adc57ef25ab84c8b892e6281343d\\\\b82a7cd4a2336b03a57c0a020e83e9ac\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0583\",\n      \"title\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation plus immunotherapy in non-small cell lung cancer and melanoma: the phase 2 FMT-LUMINate trial\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41591-025-04186-5\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"研究设计为前瞻性、多中心、单臂II期临床试验。入组标准包括：NSCLC患者需PD-L1 TPS ≥50%且无一线可操作基因改变；黑色素瘤患者不限BRAF状态。所有患者在开始ICI治疗前一周内接受来自健康供体的单次FMT（口服胶囊）。主要终点是NSCLC队列的ORR。次要终点包括黑色素瘤ORR、1年无进展生存期（PFS）、总生存期（OS）、疾病控制率（DCR）及安全性。微生物组分析采用鸟枪法宏基因组测序，结合定量PCR和高通量培养组学验证细菌变化。此外，通过血浆代谢组学和流式细胞术分析免疫代谢景观，并在抗生素处理的荷瘤小鼠模型中通过回输“丢失”的细菌混合物来验证因果机制。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了FMT联合ICI在NSCLC和黑色素瘤一线治疗中的临床活性证据，证实FMT可提高ORR。识别出“基线有害菌清除”作为潜在的药效学生物标志物，并揭示了其与色氨酸代谢抑制及CD8+ T细胞扩增的关联。\",\n            \"limits\": \"研究为单臂设计，缺乏安慰剂对照，难以完全排除自然病程或其他混杂因素。样本量较小（每队列20人），限制了亚组分析和统计效力。黑色素瘤队列中观察到的严重毒性信号（如心肌炎）需要更大规模的随机试验进一步确认。供体选择对毒性的影响提示个体化差异，但具体筛选标准尚未确立。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"NSCLC患者接受FMT+抗PD-1\",\n            \"finding\": \"ORR达到80%（16/20），满足主要终点；1年OS为100%；未观察到3级及以上与治疗相关的不良事件。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"黑色素瘤患者接受FMT+抗PD-1+抗CTLA-4\",\n            \"finding\": \"ORR为75%（15/20）；观察到较文献报道更早发生的严重免疫相关不良反应（irAEs），包括心肌炎；部分严重AEs与富含Prevotella的供体有关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"微生物组分析与临床响应关联\",\n            \"finding\": \"响应者（R）与非响应者（NR）相比，在FMT后表现出显著更高的基线细菌物种丢失幅度。这种丢失主要由有害菌（如Enterocloster, Clostridium, Streptococcus）构成，而非供体菌株的定植。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"机制验证（动物实验）\",\n            \"finding\": \"在抗生素处理的小鼠中，将FMT响应者基线样本中丢失的有害细菌混合物重新引入，抵消了FMT联合ICI的抗肿瘤效应，证实消除有害菌是治疗获益的必要条件。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管免疫检查点抑制剂改善了NSCLC和黑色素瘤的预后，但超过半数的患者存在原发性耐药。目前尚不清楚健康供体FMT联合抗PD-1（NSCLC）或抗PD-1联合抗CTLA-4（黑色素瘤）在一线治疗中的临床活性和安全性，以及FMT增强ICI疗效的人类机制。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"该研究针对晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）和皮肤黑色素瘤患者，评估健康供体粪便微生物群移植（FMT）联合一线免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗的临床疗效、安全性及机制。NSCLC队列接受抗PD-1单药治疗，黑色素瘤队列接受抗PD-1联合抗CTLA-4双免疫检查点抑制治疗。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"这是一项多中心、开放标签的II期临床试验（FMT-LUMINate），旨在评估单次口服胶囊FMT在启动ICI治疗前对原发性耐药患者的影响。研究纳入20名NSCLC患者和20名黑色素瘤患者。主要终点为NSCLC队列的客观缓解率（ORR）。结果显示，NSCLC队列ORR为80%，超过预设阈值；黑色素瘤队列ORR为75%。安全性方面，NSCLC组无3级及以上不良事件，而黑色素瘤组出现较早发的严重免疫相关不良反应（irAEs），特别是心肌炎，且与特定供体菌群有关。机制研究发现，响应者并非通过获得特定的供体菌株定植获益，而是通过显著清除基线存在的有害细菌物种（如Enterocloster和Clostridium属），从而降低色氨酸代谢途径活性并改善全身免疫微环境。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于FMT联合ICI治疗实体瘤的关键临床数据，特别是其在克服原发性耐药方面的潜力。更重要的是，它提出了一个区别于传统“有益菌定植”假说的新机制框架：即治疗获益主要源于FMT诱导的基线有害病原体（pathobionts）的清除，进而调节宿主代谢和免疫微环境。此外，它还揭示了供体菌群特征（如Prevotella丰度）与特定ICI方案（双免疫检查点抑制）相互作用下的安全性风险。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅适用于未经过ICI治疗的原发性耐药患者；NSCLC结果基于高PD-L1表达人群；黑色素瘤结果包含不同BRAF状态。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了FMT联合ICI在NSCLC（抗PD-1）和黑色素瘤（抗PD-1/CTLA-4）中的具体ORR数据（分别为80%和75%），证明了其在一线治疗中提高临床活性的能力。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物群如何调节实体瘤中ICI的疗效”，提供具体的临床响应率证据，支持FMT作为增敏策略的有效性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制主要在人类队列中通过相关性分析发现，并通过小鼠模型进行功能验证；不同肿瘤类型中富集的有益菌种类有限重叠，但有害菌清除模式一致。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"阐明了FMT起效的潜在机制：响应者的获益主要依赖于基线有害细菌物种（如Enterocloster, Clostridium）的大量丢失，而非供体菌株的成功定植。这种丢失与血浆中色氨酸代谢产物（喹啉酸、犬尿氨酸）水平的降低以及效应性CD8+ T细胞的增加相关。动物实验证实，重新引入这些丢失的有害菌会阻断ICI疗效。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导该效应的机制是什么”，提供微生物组重塑的具体分子和免疫学路径，挑战传统的“益生菌定植”主导观点，强调“去致病化”的重要性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅在黑色素瘤双免疫治疗队列中观察到显著的毒性信号；在NSCLC单药治疗或未使用双免疫治疗的既往研究中未观察到相同程度的毒性。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"揭示了特定供体菌群特征（富含Prevotella spp.）与双免疫检查点抑制（抗PD-1/CTLA-4）背景下严重免疫相关不良反应（如心肌炎、早期发作的3级以上AEs）之间的关联。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于讨论ICI疗效与安全性之间的权衡，以及微生物组在预测和管理irAEs中的作用，提示未来FMT研究中供体筛选的重要性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"为综述中关于“FMT作用机制的争议”提供反面证据或补充视角，即定植效率可能不是决定疗效的唯一或主要因素。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究指出FMT疗效不依赖于供体-宿主相似性或菌株水平定植，而是依赖于基线微生物组的整体重塑和有害菌清除。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景信息，用于对比不同研究中关于FMT机制的解释（定植vs清除）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"跨研究的一致性证据，支持“有害菌清除”作为FMT疗效通用机制的假设。\",\n            \"material\": \"提到TACITO试验也观察到类似的结果，即在FMT组中观察到基线物种的消除，而在安慰剂组中没有。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为外部验证，增强综述中关于机制结论的可信度。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究的机制结论（有害菌清除导致疗效）是基于观察性关联和小鼠功能验证，需谨慎推断其在所有FMT场景下的普适性。\",\n          \"黑色素瘤队列中的严重毒性信号可能与双免疫检查点抑制的背景有关，不能直接外推至单药ICI治疗。\",\n          \"供体选择对毒性的影响提示个体化差异，但本研究未建立标准化的供体筛选算法，仅作为警示。\",\n          \"NSCLC队列的高ORR可能受限于高PD-L1表达人群的预后较好，需注意人群特异性。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"FMT联合ICI的临床疗效（NSCLC, Melanoma）\",\n          \"FMT介导的微生物组重塑机制（有害菌清除 vs 有益菌定植）\",\n          \"肠道微生物-免疫代谢轴（色氨酸代谢, 喹啉酸, 犬尿氨酸）\",\n          \"ICI相关不良反应（irAEs）的微生物驱动因素\",\n          \"供体选择对治疗安全性和有效性的影响\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material\": {\n        \"_progressive_task_requirements\": {\n          \"knowledge_gaps\": [\n            \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\"\n          ],\n          \"questions\": [\n            {\n              \"gap_key\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Supply a concrete, attributed case for the coordinated review.\",\n              \"question\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n              \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n              \"required_output_ids\": [\n                \"O01\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"required_outputs\": [\n            {\n              \"description\": \"Source-based findings, conditions, methods and limits useful to the review.\",\n              \"output_id\": \"O01\",\n              \"output_type\": \"practical_material\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        \"_progressive_task_signature\": \"37c81ea7cb18384d\",\n        \"current_question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC, n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。黑色素瘤患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（nivolumab）和抗CTLA-4（ipilimumab）治疗；NSCLC患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（pembrolizumab）治疗。供体聚类基于41个来自9名供体的样本进行Bray-Curtis不相似度分析。AEs分级依据CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在黑色素瘤队列中，19名患者（95%）出现任何级别的与治疗相关的不良事件（AEs），其中12名（60%）出现3级或更高级别的AEs，1名出现4级AEs。最常见的3级或以上AE是腹泻或结肠炎（20%的患者）。有3名患者（15%）发生心肌炎，这高于文献报道的<1%的发生率。严重毒性的中位发作时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后的40天，早于文献报告的60天。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）认为这些严重AEs比既往文献报道的发作更早。在NSCLC队列中，没有患者出现3级或更高级别的AEs。通过非监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个截然不同的簇（P = 0.001）。B簇（包含供体5和供体11）以高丰度的Prevotella spp.为特征。差异丰度分析证实，P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae GGB1146 SGB1472在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的黑色素瘤患者中，若发生3级或以上AEs则显著富集。后FMT定植的P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae SGB1472与3级或以上AEs显著相关（P < 0.001, P = 0.023, P < 0.001）。供体5提供的粪便在所有接受双ICI治疗的黑色素瘤患者中均导致3级或以上AEs，且两名发生快速发作心肌炎的患者均接受了来自供体5的FMT。然而，当仅使用抗PD-1单药治疗时（如之前的MIMIC试验中的供体5数据或本研究的NSCLC队列），来自Cluster B的供体并未引起毒性。综合所有经历3级或以上AEs的患者（包括当前研究和之前的MIMIC试验），来自Cluster B供体的粪便仅在联合PD-1和CTLA-4阻断的患者中引起毒性（P = 0.051）。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中关于安全性数据的具体统计结果、毒性事件临床特征（特别是心肌炎和腹泻/结肠炎的发作时间），以及供体微生物组聚类分析与免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）关联的详细机制证据。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT结合不同免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）背景下的安全性差异及其潜在的微生物机制。首先，FMT联合抗PD-1在NSCLC中表现出良好的安全性，无3级或以上AEs；而在联合抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4的黑色素瘤中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC判定为可接受，但出现了加速发作的严重免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），特别是心肌炎和中位发作时间提前至40天。其次，供体微生物组的组成决定了这种毒性风险。通过非监督聚类，供体被分为富含Prevotella spp.的Cluster B和其他Cluster A。Cluster B供体（特别是供体5）与严重的irAEs（包括心肌炎）密切相关，但这种关联具有严格的上下文依赖性：它仅在抗CTLA-4存在的情况下显现，而在抗PD-1单药治疗背景下不引发毒性。这表明特定的微生物类群（如Prevotella spp.）与ICI骨架之间存在情境依赖性的相互作用，可能驱动了双重阻断下的过度免疫反应。因此，供体选择对于未来FMT试验的安全性至关重要，特别是当联合使用抗CTLA-4时。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管观察到其与特定菌属定植及CD4+ T细胞亚群变化的关联。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化以避免Cluster B供体，目前仅指出需排除此类供体。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"fulfilled\": false,\n        \"paper_identity\": {\n          \"canonical_paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\",\n          \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41591-025-04186-5\",\n          \"title\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation plus immunotherapy in non-small cell lung cancer and melanoma: the phase 2 FMT-LUMINate trial\"\n        },\n        \"paper_title\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation plus immunotherapy in non-small cell lung cancer and melanoma: the phase 2 FMT-LUMINate trial\",\n        \"plain_text\": \"\",\n        \"prior_question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究人群：NSCLC队列（n=20，一线治疗，PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50%，无既往抗PD-1暴露）；黑色素瘤队列（n=20，一线治疗，允许既往辅助抗PD-1但需停药>6个月）。干预措施：单次健康供体粪便微生物移植（FMT，口服胶囊）后紧接着启动ICI治疗。NSCLC组使用帕博利珠单抗（pembrolizumab）单药；黑色素瘤组使用纳武利尤单抗（nivolumab）联合伊匹木单抗（ipilimumab）。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的客观缓解率（ORR）为80%（16/20），超过了预设的64%主要终点阈值。在黑色素瘤队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1加抗CTLA-4（双ICI）治疗的ORR为75%（15/20）。关于毒性信号，在黑色素瘤双ICI组中观察到严重不良事件（AEs）的发生率较高且起病更早：13名患者（65%）经历了3级或更高级别的AEs，其中腹泻或结肠炎是最常见的3级及以上AE（20%的患者），另有15%的患者出现心肌炎。严重毒性的中位起病时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后40天，早于文献报道的60天。相比之下，NSCLC单药抗PD-1组无3级或以上AEs发生。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT联合免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）在非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）和黑色素瘤中的具体客观缓解率（ORR），以及双免疫检查点抑制剂（dual ICI）治疗下的毒性信号特征。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"该研究通过FMT-LUMINate II期临床试验评估了FMT联合ICI在两种不同癌症背景下的临床活性与安全性。结果显示，FMT显著提高了NSCLC患者对单药抗PD-1治疗的反应率（80%），并改善了黑色素瘤患者对双免疫检查点阻断（抗PD-1+抗CTLA-4）的反应率（75%）。然而，在黑色素瘤双ICI背景下，FMT联合治疗伴随着更高的免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）风险，特别是心肌炎和早期发作的严重毒性。这种毒性信号与特定供体来源的微生物群（富含Prevotella spp.）有关，且仅在双ICI背景下出现，而在单药抗PD-1背景下未观察到类似毒性。这表明FMT的安全性与特定的ICI治疗方案及供体微生物特征存在交互作用。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": []\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的NSCLC（n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。NSCLC组接受单药抗PD-1（pembrolizumab），黑色素瘤组接受双免疫检查点抑制剂（nivolumab + ipilimumab）。供体聚类基于 shotgun metagenomic sequencing 数据，使用 Bray-Curtis 指数进行无监督聚类，并通过 PERMANOVA 评估组间差异。AEs分级依据 CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，接受FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的患者未出现3级或更高不良事件（AEs），仅15%患者因2级AE使用皮质类固醇；而在黑色素瘤队列中，65%患者经历3级或更高AEs，其中腹泻/结肠炎占20%，心肌炎发生率为15%（3例），显著高于文献报道的<1%。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）判定该组合总体安全，但指出黑色素瘤队列中的严重AEs起病更早（中位40天 vs 文献60天）。聚类分析显示，来自富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（特别是供体5）与3级及以上AEs的发生密切相关：所有由供体5提供的粪便导致黑色素瘤患者出现3级+ AEs，且2/3例心肌炎患者接受了供体5的FMT。这些毒性仅在双免疫检查点抑制剂（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）背景下观察到，而在单药抗PD-1治疗中未出现。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中安全性数据的详细统计结果、毒性事件的具体临床特征（如心肌炎发生率、起病时间）以及与供体微生物组相关的因果关联分析，作为核心证据支持关于FMT安全性的讨论。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"分析对象包括FMT-LUMINate黑色素瘤队列及之前发表的MIMIC试验（Phase 1, melanoma, anti-PD-1 monotherapy）数据。统计方法包括 Fisher's exact test 比较组间差异，LEfSe 进行线性判别分析效应大小，以及 Spearman's r 相关性分析微生物定植与免疫细胞频率的关系。\",\n                \"finding\": \"通过无监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个 distinct clusters (P = 0.001)。Cluster B（包含供体5和11）以高丰度的普雷沃菌属（如 Segatella copri, Prevotella sp. Marseille-P4119, P. stercorea）为特征。在黑色素瘤患者中，接受 Cluster B 供体FMT后，出现3级+ AEs的患者中这些菌属显著富集（LDA score > 2, P < 0.05）。进一步分析显示，在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的患者中，S. copri 的定植与外周血中 CD4+ T细胞亚群比例增加相关（P = 0.026），而这种关联在单药抗PD-1治疗中不存在。这表明普雷沃菌属的定植是双免疫检查点抑制背景下毒性增加的驱动因素。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R23\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"展示供体微生物组特征与免疫相关不良反应（irAEs）之间的机制性联系，特别是普雷沃菌属定植与特定T细胞亚群变化的关联，用于解释为何某些供体会增加毒性风险。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT联合ICI治疗的安全性与供体微生物组特征及ICI治疗方案之间的复杂相互作用。首先，FMT本身在NSCLC（单药抗PD-1）中表现出良好的耐受性，无3级以上AEs。然而，在黑色素瘤（双药抗PD-1/CTLA-4）中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC认可，但出现了加速发作的严重AEs，特别是心肌炎。这种毒性信号并非随机分布，而是与特定的供体微生物组特征紧密相关。通过供体聚类分析，研究发现富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（Cluster B）与3级以上AEs的发生显著相关。机制上，这种关联依赖于ICI的治疗背景：普雷沃菌属的定植及其引发的CD4+ T细胞变化仅在双免疫检查点抑制（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）条件下导致毒性，而在单药抗PD-1条件下不表现。这提示普雷沃菌属可能是双免疫检查点抑制背景下免疫相关不良反应的潜在驱动因子，强调了根据ICI方案选择合适供体的重要性。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管已将其列为特殊关注AE。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化‘普雷沃菌属丰富度’阈值以排除高风险供体，文中未给出明确操作指南。\",\n              \"长期随访中，这些早期AEs对最终肿瘤疗效是否有负面影响，文中提到数值上有关联但统计不显著，需更大样本验证。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究人群：NSCLC队列（n=20，一线治疗，PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50%，无既往抗PD-1暴露）；黑色素瘤队列（n=20，一线治疗，允许既往辅助抗PD-1但需停药>6个月）。干预措施：单次健康供体粪便微生物移植（FMT，口服胶囊）后紧接着启动ICI治疗。NSCLC组使用帕博利珠单抗（pembrolizumab）单药；黑色素瘤组使用纳武利尤单抗（nivolumab）联合伊匹木单抗（ipilimumab）。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的客观缓解率（ORR）为80%（16/20），超过了预设的64%主要终点阈值。在黑色素瘤队列中，FMT联合抗PD-1加抗CTLA-4（双ICI）治疗的ORR为75%（15/20）。关于毒性信号，在黑色素瘤双ICI组中观察到严重不良事件（AEs）的发生率较高且起病更早：13名患者（65%）经历了3级或更高级别的AEs，其中腹泻或结肠炎是最常见的3级及以上AE（20%的患者），另有15%的患者出现心肌炎。严重毒性的中位起病时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后40天，早于文献报道的60天。相比之下，NSCLC单药抗PD-1组无3级或以上AEs发生。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT联合免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）在非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）和黑色素瘤中的具体客观缓解率（ORR），以及双免疫检查点抑制剂（dual ICI）治疗下的毒性信号特征。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"该研究通过FMT-LUMINate II期临床试验评估了FMT联合ICI在两种不同癌症背景下的临床活性与安全性。结果显示，FMT显著提高了NSCLC患者对单药抗PD-1治疗的反应率（80%），并改善了黑色素瘤患者对双免疫检查点阻断（抗PD-1+抗CTLA-4）的反应率（75%）。然而，在黑色素瘤双ICI背景下，FMT联合治疗伴随着更高的免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）风险，特别是心肌炎和早期发作的严重毒性。这种毒性信号与特定供体来源的微生物群（富含Prevotella spp.）有关，且仅在双ICI背景下出现，而在单药抗PD-1背景下未观察到类似毒性。这表明FMT的安全性与特定的ICI治疗方案及供体微生物特征存在交互作用。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": []\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的NSCLC（n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。NSCLC组接受单药抗PD-1（pembrolizumab），黑色素瘤组接受双免疫检查点抑制剂（nivolumab + ipilimumab）。供体聚类基于 shotgun metagenomic sequencing 数据，使用 Bray-Curtis 指数进行无监督聚类，并通过 PERMANOVA 评估组间差异。AEs分级依据 CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在NSCLC队列中，接受FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的患者未出现3级或更高不良事件（AEs），仅15%患者因2级AE使用皮质类固醇；而在黑色素瘤队列中，65%患者经历3级或更高AEs，其中腹泻/结肠炎占20%，心肌炎发生率为15%（3例），显著高于文献报道的<1%。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）判定该组合总体安全，但指出黑色素瘤队列中的严重AEs起病更早（中位40天 vs 文献60天）。聚类分析显示，来自富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（特别是供体5）与3级及以上AEs的发生密切相关：所有由供体5提供的粪便导致黑色素瘤患者出现3级+ AEs，且2/3例心肌炎患者接受了供体5的FMT。这些毒性仅在双免疫检查点抑制剂（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）背景下观察到，而在单药抗PD-1治疗中未出现。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中安全性数据的详细统计结果、毒性事件的具体临床特征（如心肌炎发生率、起病时间）以及与供体微生物组相关的因果关联分析，作为核心证据支持关于FMT安全性的讨论。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"分析对象包括FMT-LUMINate黑色素瘤队列及之前发表的MIMIC试验（Phase 1, melanoma, anti-PD-1 monotherapy）数据。统计方法包括 Fisher's exact test 比较组间差异，LEfSe 进行线性判别分析效应大小，以及 Spearman's r 相关性分析微生物定植与免疫细胞频率的关系。\",\n                \"finding\": \"通过无监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个 distinct clusters (P = 0.001)。Cluster B（包含供体5和11）以高丰度的普雷沃菌属（如 Segatella copri, Prevotella sp. Marseille-P4119, P. stercorea）为特征。在黑色素瘤患者中，接受 Cluster B 供体FMT后，出现3级+ AEs的患者中这些菌属显著富集（LDA score > 2, P < 0.05）。进一步分析显示，在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的患者中，S. copri 的定植与外周血中 CD4+ T细胞亚群比例增加相关（P = 0.026），而这种关联在单药抗PD-1治疗中不存在。这表明普雷沃菌属的定植是双免疫检查点抑制背景下毒性增加的驱动因素。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R23\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"展示供体微生物组特征与免疫相关不良反应（irAEs）之间的机制性联系，特别是普雷沃菌属定植与特定T细胞亚群变化的关联，用于解释为何某些供体会增加毒性风险。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT联合ICI治疗的安全性与供体微生物组特征及ICI治疗方案之间的复杂相互作用。首先，FMT本身在NSCLC（单药抗PD-1）中表现出良好的耐受性，无3级以上AEs。然而，在黑色素瘤（双药抗PD-1/CTLA-4）中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC认可，但出现了加速发作的严重AEs，特别是心肌炎。这种毒性信号并非随机分布，而是与特定的供体微生物组特征紧密相关。通过供体聚类分析，研究发现富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（Cluster B）与3级以上AEs的发生显著相关。机制上，这种关联依赖于ICI的治疗背景：普雷沃菌属的定植及其引发的CD4+ T细胞变化仅在双免疫检查点抑制（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）条件下导致毒性，而在单药抗PD-1条件下不表现。这提示普雷沃菌属可能是双免疫检查点抑制背景下免疫相关不良反应的潜在驱动因子，强调了根据ICI方案选择合适供体的重要性。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管已将其列为特殊关注AE。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化‘普雷沃菌属丰富度’阈值以排除高风险供体，文中未给出明确操作指南。\",\n              \"长期随访中，这些早期AEs对最终肿瘤疗效是否有负面影响，文中提到数值上有关联但统计不显著，需更大样本验证。\"\n            ]\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC, n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。黑色素瘤患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（nivolumab）和抗CTLA-4（ipilimumab）治疗；NSCLC患者接受FMT联合抗PD-1（pembrolizumab）治疗。供体聚类基于41个来自9名供体的样本进行Bray-Curtis不相似度分析。AEs分级依据CTCAE v5.0。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在黑色素瘤队列中，19名患者（95%）出现任何级别的与治疗相关的不良事件（AEs），其中12名（60%）出现3级或更高级别的AEs，1名出现4级AEs。最常见的3级或以上AE是腹泻或结肠炎（20%的患者）。有3名患者（15%）发生心肌炎，这高于文献报道的<1%的发生率。严重毒性的中位发作时间为开始抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4治疗后的40天，早于文献报告的60天。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）认为这些严重AEs比既往文献报道的发作更早。在NSCLC队列中，没有患者出现3级或更高级别的AEs。通过非监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个截然不同的簇（P = 0.001）。B簇（包含供体5和供体11）以高丰度的Prevotella spp.为特征。差异丰度分析证实，P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae GGB1146 SGB1472在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的黑色素瘤患者中，若发生3级或以上AEs则显著富集。后FMT定植的P. sp. Marseille-P4119、P. stercorea和Prevotellaceae SGB1472与3级或以上AEs显著相关（P < 0.001, P = 0.023, P < 0.001）。供体5提供的粪便在所有接受双ICI治疗的黑色素瘤患者中均导致3级或以上AEs，且两名发生快速发作心肌炎的患者均接受了来自供体5的FMT。然而，当仅使用抗PD-1单药治疗时（如之前的MIMIC试验中的供体5数据或本研究的NSCLC队列），来自Cluster B的供体并未引起毒性。综合所有经历3级或以上AEs的患者（包括当前研究和之前的MIMIC试验），来自Cluster B供体的粪便仅在联合PD-1和CTLA-4阻断的患者中引起毒性（P = 0.051）。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中关于安全性数据的具体统计结果、毒性事件临床特征（特别是心肌炎和腹泻/结肠炎的发作时间），以及供体微生物组聚类分析与免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）关联的详细机制证据。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"本研究揭示了FMT结合不同免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）背景下的安全性差异及其潜在的微生物机制。首先，FMT联合抗PD-1在NSCLC中表现出良好的安全性，无3级或以上AEs；而在联合抗PD-1和抗CTLA-4的黑色素瘤中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC判定为可接受，但出现了加速发作的严重免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），特别是心肌炎和中位发作时间提前至40天。其次，供体微生物组的组成决定了这种毒性风险。通过非监督聚类，供体被分为富含Prevotella spp.的Cluster B和其他Cluster A。Cluster B供体（特别是供体5）与严重的irAEs（包括心肌炎）密切相关，但这种关联具有严格的上下文依赖性：它仅在抗CTLA-4存在的情况下显现，而在抗PD-1单药治疗背景下不引发毒性。这表明特定的微生物类群（如Prevotella spp.）与ICI骨架之间存在情境依赖性的相互作用，可能驱动了双重阻断下的过度免疫反应。因此，供体选择对于未来FMT试验的安全性至关重要，特别是当联合使用抗CTLA-4时。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管观察到其与特定菌属定植及CD4+ T细胞亚群变化的关联。\",\n              \"供体筛选标准如何具体量化以避免Cluster B供体，目前仅指出需排除此类供体。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"questions\": [\n          {\n            \"gap_key\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Supply a concrete, attributed case for the coordinated review.\",\n            \"question\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是本章核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"required_output_ids\": [\n              \"O01\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"reading_mode\": \"focused_local_search\",\n        \"required_outputs\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Source-based findings, conditions, methods and limits useful to the review.\",\n            \"output_id\": \"O01\",\n            \"output_type\": \"practical_material\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"source_hash\": \"8dd08b84648262841f9d35af692160252692383791e7c7d076ecd654063fb0f3\",\n        \"task_hash\": \"4fcf708140bdb8b00ce198191a4362b1ee8545d9d5569d77e4118fb1833a9f61\"\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material_trimmed\": {\n        \"dropped_keys\": {\n          \"material_ready\": 4,\n          \"paper_id\": 42,\n          \"references\": 413,\n          \"review_id\": 23,\n          \"schema_version\": 46,\n          \"status\": 9,\n          \"task_id\": 34,\n          \"workflow\": 21\n        },\n        \"dropped_chars\": 592,\n        \"note\": \"深读材料里的记账字段与论文自带的参考文献表已省略；科学内容（问句材料/发现/条件）保留。需要时可用 locator 读原文。\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The authors explicitly state that this is a phase 2 trial, not a phase 3 trial. They mention that the findings will be assessed as part of the CanBiome2 randomized trial (NCT06623461), which is described as a randomized trial evaluating FMT in combination with ICI in 128 patients, but the current paper does not report the results of this phase 3 trial. The study design was open-label and single-arm for the cohorts reported here, lacking the randomization required for a phase 3 RCT status.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Search for published results from the CanBiome2 trial (NCT06623461) or other large-scale randomized phase III trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated cancer patients to determine if any have been completed and reported since this publication.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires evidence of a completed large randomised phase III trial. This material provides only a phase 2 trial. While the authors reference an ongoing/forthcoming phase 3 trial (CanBiome2), they do not provide its results or confirm its completion within this text.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of FMT-LUMINate, a multicenter, open-label, phase 2 trial evaluating healthy donor fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) in patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) or melanoma. The trial enrolled 40 patients (20 NSCLC, 20 melanoma). In the NSCLC cohort receiving anti-PD-1 monotherapy, the objective response rate (ORR) was 80%. In the melanoma cohort receiving dual anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 therapy, the ORR was 75%. The study found that clinical response was associated with the loss of baseline deleterious bacterial species rather than the engraftment of donor-specific strains. Safety data indicated no grade 3+ adverse events in the NSCLC cohort, but a higher incidence of severe immune-related adverse events (including myocarditis) in the melanoma cohort, particularly among recipients of donors rich in Prevotella spp.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The authors explicitly state that this is a phase 2 trial, not a phase 3 trial. They mention that the findings will be assessed as part of the CanBiome2 randomized trial (NCT06623461), which is described as a randomized trial evaluating FMT in combination with ICI in 128 patients, but the current paper does not report the results of this phase 3 trial. The study design was open-label and single-arm for the cohorts reported here, lacking the randomization required for a phase 3 RCT status.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Search for published results from the CanBiome2 trial (NCT06623461) or other large-scale randomized phase III trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated cancer patients to determine if any have been completed and reported since this publication.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires evidence of a completed large randomised phase III trial. This material provides only a phase 2 trial. While the authors reference an ongoing/forthcoming phase 3 trial (CanBiome2), they do not provide its results or confirm its completion within this text.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of FMT-LUMINate, a multicenter, open-label, phase 2 trial evaluating healthy donor fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) in patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) or melanoma. The trial enrolled 40 patients (20 NSCLC, 20 melanoma). In the NSCLC cohort receiving anti-PD-1 monotherapy, the objective response rate (ORR) was 80%. In the melanoma cohort receiving dual anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 therapy, the ORR was 75%. The study found that clinical response was associated with the loss of baseline deleterious bacterial species rather than the engraftment of donor-specific strains. Safety data indicated no grade 3+ adverse events in the NSCLC cohort, but a higher incidence of severe immune-related adverse events (including myocarditis) in the melanoma cohort, particularly among recipients of donors rich in Prevotella spp.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"tool_supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"allowed_use\": [],\n          \"conditions\": [],\n          \"decision\": \"stop\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"\",\n          \"limits\": [],\n          \"need_id\": \"Nbcf492500208-757e7573bb6bd28e\",\n          \"question\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验中 FMT 联合 ICI 的 ORR 数据是多少？供体菌群特征（如 Prevotella）与毒性（心肌炎）的关联机制是什么？\",\n          \"sources\": [\n            {\n              \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41591-025-04186-5\",\n              \"paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\",\n              \"source_handle\": \"P0583\",\n              \"title\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation plus immunotherapy in non-small cell lung cancer and melanoma: the phase 2 FMT-LUMINate trial\",\n              \"year\": \"2026\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"still_missing\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验中 FMT 联合 ICI 的 ORR 数据是多少？供体菌群特征（如 Prevotella）与毒性（心肌炎）的关联机制是什么？\",\n          \"usable_content\": \"{\\\"question_material\\\": [{\\\"question_id\\\": \\\"Q01\\\", \\\"examples\\\": [{\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"提供FMT联合ICI在NSCLC和黑色素瘤一线治疗中的客观缓解率（ORR）具体数据，以及安全性特征。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在FMT-LUMINate试验中，接受健康供体FMT联合抗PD-1单药治疗的非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者（n=20），其客观缓解率（ORR）为80%（16/20），超过了预设的64%主要终点阈值。在黑色素瘤队列中，接受FMT联合抗PD-1加抗CTLA-4双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的患者（n=20），ORR为75%（15/20）。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"NSCLC队列：一线治疗，无驱动基因突变，PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50%，使用帕博利珠单抗（pembrolizumab）。黑色素瘤队列：一线或既往辅助治疗后复发>6个月，使用纳武利尤单抗（nivolumab）联合伊匹木单抗（ipilimumab）。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": []}, {\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"揭示供体菌群特征（Prevotella富集）与特定毒性（心肌炎、3级及以上AEs）的关联机制，强调治疗背景（双免疫抑制 vs 单药）的关键调节作用。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"供体微生物组聚类显示，Cluster B（包含供体5和11）以高丰度的普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.，如Segatella copri, Prevotella sp. Marseille-P4119, P. stercorea）为特征。在黑色素瘤队列中，接受Cluster B供体（特别是供体5）FMT的患者发生了严重的免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），包括3例心肌炎（发生率15%），且发病时间显著提前（中位40天，而文献报道通常为60天）。其中2例心肌炎患者均接受了供体5的FMT，发病迅速（分别为第22天和第25天）。统计分析显示，Cluster B供体的粪便仅在双PD-1和CTLA-4阻断背景下导致毒性（P=0.051），而在抗PD-1单药治疗背景下未观察到此类毒性。此外，S. copri的定植与这些严重AEs患者外周血中CD4+ T细胞亚群增加相关。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"比较对象：接受不同供体FMT的患者；治疗背景对比：黑色素瘤队列（双ICI）vs NSCLC队列及既往MIMIC试验（均为抗PD-1单药）。供体5在MIMIC试验（抗PD-1单药）中提供的7次FMT均未导致3级及以上AEs，而在本试验黑色素瘤队列中导致的FMT均伴随严重AEs。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": []}], \\\"explanation\\\": \\\"该研究阐明了FMT增强ICI疗效的机制并非主要依赖于供体菌株的定植或供主微生物组的相似度，而是取决于患者基线有害菌群的清除。具体来说，响应者（Responders）在接受FMT后表现出比非响应者更显著的基线细菌物种丢失（SGB loss），特别是那些已知与ICI耐药相关的有害菌属（如Enterocloster, Clostridium, Streptococcus等）。这种有害菌的消除导致了色氨酸代谢途径的减弱，降低了血浆中喹啉酸（quinolinic acid）和犬尿氨酸（kynurenine）的水平，从而改善了免疫代谢微环境（增加效应CD8+ T细胞，减少调节性T细胞）。关于毒性机制，研究发现特定的供体菌群特征（富含Prevotella spp.）与双免疫检查点抑制剂（抗PD-1 + 抗CTLA-4）背景下的严重免疫相关不良事件（如心肌炎）存在情境依赖性关联。这种毒性风险在单药抗PD-1治疗中并未出现，提示供体选择需根据具体的ICI治疗方案进行优化。\\\", \\\"remaining_points\\\": [\\\"Prevotella spp. 诱导心肌炎的具体分子机制（如通过何种抗原或代谢物激活特定T细胞亚群）在本研究中仅表现为相关性，未深入解析其下游信号通路。\\\", \\\"虽然发现了基线有害菌丢失是疗效的关键预测因子，但哪些特定的丢失菌种组合对疗效贡献最大，以及是否可以通过定向清除而非全菌群移植来实现这一效果，仍需进一步研究。\\\"]}], \\\"plain_text\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"allowed_use\": [],\n          \"conditions\": [],\n          \"decision\": \"stop\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"\",\n          \"limits\": [],\n          \"need_id\": \"Nc9621a3d541b-757e7573bb6bd28e\",\n          \"question\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是 Ch5/Ch6 核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n          \"sources\": [\n            {\n              \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41591-025-04186-5\",\n              \"paper_id\": \"071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a\",\n              \"source_handle\": \"P0583\",\n              \"title\": \"Fecal microbiota transplantation plus immunotherapy in non-small cell lung cancer and melanoma: the phase 2 FMT-LUMINate trial\",\n              \"year\": \"2026\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"still_missing\": \"FMT-LUMINate 试验的详细安全性数据及供体聚类分析是 Ch5/Ch6 核心证据，需深入解读其统计方法及毒性事件的具体临床特征。\",\n          \"usable_content\": \"{\\\"question_material\\\": [{\\\"question_id\\\": \\\"Q01\\\", \\\"examples\\\": [{\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"提供FMT-LUMINate试验中安全性数据的详细统计结果、毒性事件的具体临床特征（如心肌炎发生率、起病时间）以及与供体微生物组相关的因果关联分析，作为核心证据支持关于FMT安全性的讨论。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在NSCLC队列中，接受FMT联合抗PD-1治疗的患者未出现3级或更高不良事件（AEs），仅15%患者因2级AE使用皮质类固醇；而在黑色素瘤队列中，65%患者经历3级或更高AEs，其中腹泻/结肠炎占20%，心肌炎发生率为15%（3例），显著高于文献报道的<1%。独立数据和安全监测委员会（DSMC）判定该组合总体安全，但指出黑色素瘤队列中的严重AEs起病更早（中位40天 vs 文献60天）。聚类分析显示，来自富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（特别是供体5）与3级及以上AEs的发生密切相关：所有由供体5提供的粪便导致黑色素瘤患者出现3级+ AEs，且2/3例心肌炎患者接受了供体5的FMT。这些毒性仅在双免疫检查点抑制剂（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）背景下观察到，而在单药抗PD-1治疗中未出现。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为FMT-LUMINate试验中的NSCLC（n=20）和黑色素瘤（n=20）患者。NSCLC组接受单药抗PD-1（pembrolizumab），黑色素瘤组接受双免疫检查点抑制剂（nivolumab + ipilimumab）。供体聚类基于 shotgun metagenomic sequencing 数据，使用 Bray-Curtis 指数进行无监督聚类，并通过 PERMANOVA 评估组间差异。AEs分级依据 CTCAE v5.0。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": []}, {\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"展示供体微生物组特征与免疫相关不良反应（irAEs）之间的机制性联系，特别是普雷沃菌属定植与特定T细胞亚群变化的关联，用于解释为何某些供体会增加毒性风险。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"通过无监督聚类分析，健康供体的肠道微生物组被分为两个 distinct clusters (P = 0.001)。Cluster B（包含供体5和11）以高丰度的普雷沃菌属（如 Segatella copri, Prevotella sp. Marseille-P4119, P. stercorea）为特征。在黑色素瘤患者中，接受 Cluster B 供体FMT后，出现3级+ AEs的患者中这些菌属显著富集（LDA score > 2, P < 0.05）。进一步分析显示，在接受双免疫检查点抑制剂治疗的患者中，S. copri 的定植与外周血中 CD4+ T细胞亚群比例增加相关（P = 0.026），而这种关联在单药抗PD-1治疗中不存在。这表明普雷沃菌属的定植是双免疫检查点抑制背景下毒性增加的驱动因素。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"分析对象包括FMT-LUMINate黑色素瘤队列及之前发表的MIMIC试验（Phase 1, melanoma, anti-PD-1 monotherapy）数据。统计方法包括 Fisher's exact test 比较组间差异，LEfSe 进行线性判别分析效应大小，以及 Spearman's r 相关性分析微生物定植与免疫细胞频率的关系。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": [\\\"R23\\\"]}], \\\"explanation\\\": \\\"本研究揭示了FMT联合ICI治疗的安全性与供体微生物组特征及ICI治疗方案之间的复杂相互作用。首先，FMT本身在NSCLC（单药抗PD-1）中表现出良好的耐受性，无3级以上AEs。然而，在黑色素瘤（双药抗PD-1/CTLA-4）中，虽然总体安全性被DSMC认可，但出现了加速发作的严重AEs，特别是心肌炎。这种毒性信号并非随机分布，而是与特定的供体微生物组特征紧密相关。通过供体聚类分析，研究发现富含普雷沃菌属（Prevotella spp.）的供体（Cluster B）与3级以上AEs的发生显著相关。机制上，这种关联依赖于ICI的治疗背景：普雷沃菌属的定植及其引发的CD4+ T细胞变化仅在双免疫检查点抑制（anti-PD-1 + anti-CTLA-4）条件下导致毒性，而在单药抗PD-1条件下不表现。这提示普雷沃菌属可能是双免疫检查点抑制背景下免疫相关不良反应的潜在驱动因子，强调了根据ICI方案选择合适供体的重要性。\\\", \\\"remaining_points\\\": [\\\"心肌炎的具体病理生理机制尚需进一步探索，尽管已将其列为特殊关注AE。\\\", \\\"供体筛选标准如何具体量化‘普雷沃菌属丰富度’阈值以排除高风险供体，文中未给出明确操作指南。\\\", \\\"长期随访中，这些早期AEs对最终肿瘤疗效是否有负面影响，文中提到数值上有关联但统计不显著，需更大样本验证。\\\"]}], \\\"plain_text\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0583\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a91f0ed643887e9f1b1d\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ef95a97dcd7bd8c151c4\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-501f35983c2f020a5c71\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-81ec1ef0c2a046851171\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-d46f32b2af56fa1c47e2\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-02757a568956f4b210b5\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-47775c7896730e2260bc\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b08d87be9985923039c9\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-4d651eaa044aee02c8ec\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-1294ef6fcf50c0be4a42\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-6e217865835c2f670e17\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b1f79ce65e003459f3ed\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-786845b1cd45dd24fdc8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-f0ebbdf389d42cf46d30\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementaryFiles_fetch_transport_TimeoutError\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_dcbe39b3d2\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0577\",\n      \"title\": \"Improved survival in advanced melanoma patients treated with fecal microbiota transplantation using healthy donor stool in combination with anti-PD1: final results of the MIMic phase 1 trial\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2025-012659\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"20c143d5373eab0bfea2067cf66b0e260dfeb1ef\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"Single-arm, multicenter phase I clinical trial. Patients received bowel preparation, followed by oral administration of capsules containing stool from one of three healthy male donors. Anti-PD1 therapy (nivolumab or pembrolizumab) started at least 7 days post-FMT. Outcomes were assessed using RECIST 1.1 criteria. Survival analysis used Kaplan-Meier methods. Metagenomic sequencing was performed on stool samples to analyze taxonomic composition (MetaPhlAn) and functional gene families (HUMAnN). Post hoc subgroup analyses compared responders vs. non-responders and patients with vs. without FMT-specific toxicity (FT) based on microbial and functional profiles.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides long-term survival data (median OS 52.8 months) for a small cohort of advanced melanoma patients treated with oral FMT plus anti-PD1, demonstrating high safety and promising efficacy signals compared to historical controls.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Small sample size (N=20), single-arm design without a control group, and lack of statistical power for definitive conclusions. Results are hypothesis-generating. Patient selection bias (excluded those appropriate for combination therapy).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies potential biomarkers for response, linking FMT-specific mild toxicity and long-term survival to specific microbial taxa (e.g., SCFA producers) and functional metabolic pathways (glycan modification, SCFA biosynthesis).\",\n            \"limits\": \"Associations are derived from post hoc analyses. Causality between specific microbes/enzymes and clinical benefit is not established. The 'donor effect' could not be clearly disentangled due to the small cohort and mixed donor usage.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Entire cohort (N=20), median follow-up 52.8 months\",\n            \"finding\": \"Median PFS was 29.6 months; median OS was 52.8 months. ORR was 65% (4 complete responses). Safety profile showed no increase in severe immune-related adverse events (irAEs); only grade 3 toxicities occurred.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Responders (R, N=13) vs. Non-responders (NR, N=7)\",\n            \"finding\": \"R patients had significantly longer mPFS (52.8 vs. 5.2 months, p=0.0016) and longer mOS (52.8 vs. 17.6 months, p=0.0628). R patients sustained donor-derived microbial similarity over time.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Patients with FMT-specific toxicity (FT, N=8) vs. Without FT (NFT, N=12)\",\n            \"finding\": \"All 8 FT patients were responders. FT patients had significantly improved mPFS (52.8 vs. 15.9 months, p=0.0364) and trend towards improved mOS (53.9 vs. 24.6 months, p=0.0610). FT was associated with enrichment of SCFA-producing taxa and glycan-modifying enzymes.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Long-term survivors (LTS) vs. Short-term survivors (STS)\",\n            \"finding\": \"LTS were enriched for commensal bacteria (Alistipes, Faecalibacterium) and enzymes involved in amino acid/SCFA biosynthesis. STS were enriched for stress-associated oxidative metabolism pathways and Hungatella hathewayi.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To evaluate whether modifying the gut microbiome via oral FMT from healthy donors can improve the safety, response rate, and survival outcomes of anti-PD1 therapy in patients with advanced melanoma who are naive to advanced-stage treatment, potentially overcoming primary resistance to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs).\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"Advanced cutaneous melanoma patients (N=20), treatment-naïve for advanced disease, receiving oral fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) from healthy donors followed by single-agent anti-PD1 therapy. Multicenter phase I trial conducted in Canada.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This study reports the final survival outcomes of the MIMic phase I trial, evaluating the safety and efficacy of combining oral FMT with healthy donor stool and anti-PD1 immunotherapy as first-line treatment for advanced melanoma. The trial enrolled 20 patients who received a single dose of FMT followed by nivolumab or pembrolizumab. The primary endpoint was safety, while secondary endpoints included objective response rate (ORR), progression-free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS). The study found that the combination was safe, with an ORR of 65%. Median PFS was 29.6 months and median OS was 52.8 months, figures reported as higher than historical randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and real-world data. Post hoc analyses linked clinical responses to specific microbial engraftment patterns and functional metabolic pathways, suggesting that successful microbiome modulation may enhance ICI efficacy.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This paper provides empirical evidence from a Phase I trial that oral FMT from healthy donors combined with anti-PD1 is safe and associated with improved survival in advanced melanoma. It offers specific mechanistic hypotheses linking clinical response to microbial engraftment success, characterized by the enrichment of short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-producing bacteria and specific metabolic pathways. The study highlights FMT-specific toxicity as a potential marker of successful microbial integration and immune priming.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Results are from a single-arm, small-sample Phase I trial. Cannot establish causal superiority over standard care due to lack of concurrent control arm. Patient population was restricted to those unsuitable for combination immunotherapy.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Reports clinical efficacy outcomes (ORR 65%, mPFS 29.6 mo, mOS 52.8 mo) for FMT+anti-PD1 in advanced melanoma, showing improvements over historical RCT benchmarks for anti-PD1 alone.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to illustrate the potential magnitude of benefit from microbiome manipulation in solid tumors, specifically melanoma. Provides baseline survival metrics for comparison with other microbiome interventions.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms are inferred from correlative post hoc analyses. The role of FMT-specific toxicity as a proxy for engraftment success is observational. Functional profiling relies on metagenomic prediction, which may not reflect actual protein activity or host interaction.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Links clinical response to specific microbial features: Responders and long-term survivors show enrichment of SCFA-producing taxa (Blautia, Roseburia, Faecalibacterium) and enzymes for glycan modification/amino acid biosynthesis. Non-responders/short-term survivors show enrichment of oxidative/stress pathways and Hungatella hathewayi.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides mechanistic insights into how microbiome composition might mediate ICI efficacy. Suggests that successful engraftment of metabolically fit, SCFA-producing communities enhances immune response. Identifies functional metabolic signatures (vs. just taxonomy) associated with outcome.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Helps categorize different microbiome intervention modalities in the review.\",\n            \"material\": \"Comparison of oral capsule-based FMT (this study) vs. invasive colonoscopic FMT (referenced in intro). This study supports the feasibility and safety of oral delivery for systemic immune modulation.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contextual background on FMT delivery methods\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Addresses the safety facet of microbiome interventions, countering concerns about increased autoimmune toxicity when combining FMT with ICIs.\",\n            \"material\": \"No increase in severe irAEs; FMT-specific toxicities were mild (Grade 1-2 GI).\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Safety profile of microbiome manipulation\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Useful for discussing the quality of evidence in the field and the need for larger, randomized Phase II/III trials (like the mentioned ME.17 trial).\",\n            \"material\": \"Acknowledges small sample size, lack of control arm, and hypothesis-generating nature of results.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Limitations of current evidence\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The study population is limited to advanced cutaneous melanoma patients ineligible for combination immunotherapy; results may not generalize to other solid tumors or earlier-stage disease.\",\n          \"Survival comparisons with historical RCTs are noted by authors but are confounded by patient selection biases and temporal changes in care; do not treat as definitive proof of superiority.\",\n          \"Microbial associations are correlational; causality between specific taxa/pathways and ICI response is not proven within this study design.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"oral fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) in solid tumors\",\n          \"anti-PD1 monotherapy in advanced melanoma\",\n          \"microbial engraftment dynamics (donor-recipient similarity)\",\n          \"FMT-specific gastrointestinal toxicity as a response biomarker\",\n          \"metabolic pathways: SCFA production, glycan degradation, oxidative stress\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material\": {\n        \"_progressive_task_signature\": \"b4053ba5060ea008\",\n        \"paper_title\": \"Improved survival in advanced melanoma patients treated with fecal microbiota transplantation using healthy donor stool in combination with anti-PD1: final results of the MIMic phase 1 trial\",\n        \"plain_text\": \"\",\n        \"question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为20名晚期黑色素瘤患者；供体为3名健康男性；给药方式为口服胶囊；预处理为PEG肠道准备；无抗生素使用。\",\n                \"finding\": \"MIMic试验使用了来自三位健康男性供体的粪便进行口服FMT。所有患者均接受单一供体的粪便（每位患者对应一位供体），未混合多供体粪便。供体经过“严格的选择标准”筛选（文中引用了之前的出版物Craven et al. 2017及Routy et al. 2023中的详细标准）。FMT产品以胶囊形式给药，每剂包含35-40粒胶囊，相当于80-100克粪便。在给予FMT前一天，患者使用聚乙二醇（PEG）基肠道准备液进行预处理，且治疗期间未使用抗生素。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R12\",\n                  \"R13\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为FMT干预标准化中供体筛选和制备流程的具体案例，展示如何通过严格筛选和健康供体来源来保证干预的一致性和安全性，同时指出单供体使用的局限性。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"MIMic试验在供体筛选和FMT制备方面采取了高度标准化的策略，旨在确保干预的安全性和微生物定植的可预测性。\\n\\n1. **供体筛选标准**：研究采用了“严格的选择标准”（stringent selection criteria）来筛选健康供体。虽然正文未列出具体排除指标的细节，但明确引用了关于代谢综合征疾病FMT供体筛选成本的研究（Craven et al., 2017, R13）以及该团队之前发表的I期试验方案（Routy et al., 2023, R12），暗示其遵循了当时临床前或早期临床试验中较为严格的卫生学、传染病及健康状况筛查流程。这种严格筛选是作为正面教材的，因为它最大限度地降低了传播病原体或引入致病微生物的风险，确保了“健康供体粪便”这一干预物的纯净度。\\n\\n2. **FMT制备与给药流程**：\\n   - **单一供源 vs 混合供源**：试验设计采用单一供体粪便（single healthy donor stool），每位患者仅接受来自三位指定男性供体中某一人的粪便。这种做法简化了批次管理，减少了不同供体间微生物组异质性带来的混杂因素，有利于评估特定微生物组合对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）的反应。然而，这也可能是一个反面教材或局限性，因为缺乏多供体混合可能无法覆盖更广泛的有益菌群多样性，且文中提到“没有明显的供体效应”，说明单个供体的效果在不同患者间可能存在差异，但未深入探讨混合供体是否会更优。\\n   - **制剂形式**：将粪便制成口服胶囊（35-40粒/剂，含80-100g粪便），而非传统的灌肠或鼻胃管给药。这提高了患者的依从性，减少了侵入性操作带来的感染风险，是FMT非侵入化、标准化的重要进展。\\n   - **预处理与辅助措施**：在FMT前一日使用PEG基肠道准备液，旨在清除原有肠道内容物，为新菌群定植腾出生态位。特别值得注意的是，研究中明确禁止使用抗生素，以避免抗生素对肠道菌群的破坏干扰FMT的效果或引起耐药性问题。这种“清洁但不杀菌”的准备策略是标准化流程中的关键控制点。\\n\\n总体而言，该试验通过严格的供体准入、单一的供体来源、标准化的胶囊制剂以及特定的肠道预处理，构建了一套可重复的FMT干预流程。其成功的关键在于确保了输入菌群的健康属性和定植潜力，为后续观察微生物组变化与临床疗效的关系提供了可靠的基础。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"文中引用的供体筛选具体指标（如具体的病原体检测列表、BMI限制、饮食要求等）未在本文正文中详细列出，需参考R12和R13获取完整细节。\",\n              \"单一供体与多供体混合在长期定植稳定性和临床获益上的比较数据不足，仅观察到无显著供体效应，未深入分析为何选择单一供体而非混合供体。\",\n              \"PEG肠道准备的具体配方浓度、体积及服用时间未详细说明。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"reading_mode\": \"focused_local_search\",\n        \"source_hash\": \"02a5690fbd7940bcc411f8b60e4aa551e71a8b476c67344b80a9b28911c5e444\"\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material_trimmed\": {\n        \"dropped_keys\": {\n          \"material_ready\": 4,\n          \"paper_id\": 42,\n          \"references\": 595,\n          \"reused_from\": 207,\n          \"review_id\": 23,\n          \"schema_version\": 46,\n          \"status\": 16,\n          \"task_id\": 34,\n          \"workflow\": 21\n        },\n        \"dropped_chars\": 988,\n        \"note\": \"深读材料里的记账字段与论文自带的参考文献表已省略；科学内容（问句材料/发现/条件）保留。需要时可用 locator 读原文。\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The paper provides mechanistic insights linking clinical response to microbial engraftment, noting that responders and long-term survivors showed enrichment of short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-produ taxa (e.g., Blautia, Roseburia, Faecalibacterium) and glycan-modifying enzymes. It also suggests that mild FMT-specific gastrointestinal toxicity may serve as a marker for successful microbial integration and immune priming. Additionally, the text contrasts these findings with historical RCT data (e.g., KEYNOTE-006, CheckMate 067) to contextualize the magnitude of observed benefits, though it acknowledges limitations regarding patient selection bias.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"large_scale_rct_results\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_8a825f7f53\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Search for completed Phase III randomized controlled trials involving FMT or defined microbiome consortia combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors. Verify completion status and primary outcome data for these specific high-level trials.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires confirmation of completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials. This study is a Phase I single-arm trial, not a Phase III RCT. While it mentions a planned Phase II randomized trial (ME.17), it does not provide data on any completed Phase III trials confirming efficacy.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports results from the MIMic trial, a multicenter, single-arm Phase I safety trial evaluating oral FMT combined with anti-PD1 therapy in 20 patients with advanced melanoma. The study found that the combination was safe and associated with an objective response rate (ORR) of 65%, median progression-free survival (mPFS) of 29.6 months, and median overall survival (mOS) of 52.8 months. These outcomes were compared favorably to historical randomized controlled trials (RCTs) of anti-PD1 monotherapy. The authors explicitly state that the sample size was small, there was no control arm, and the results are hypothesis-generating. The study identifies a subsequent randomized Phase II Canadian study (ME.17, NCT06623461) designed to evaluate the potential benefit of this intervention.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The paper provides mechanistic insights linking clinical response to microbial engraftment, noting that responders and long-term survivors showed enrichment of short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-produ taxa (e.g., Blautia, Roseburia, Faecalibacterium) and glycan-modifying enzymes. It also suggests that mild FMT-specific gastrointestinal toxicity may serve as a marker for successful microbial integration and immune priming. Additionally, the text contrasts these findings with historical RCT data (e.g., KEYNOTE-006, CheckMate 067) to contextualize the magnitude of observed benefits, though it acknowledges limitations regarding patient selection bias.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"large_scale_rct_results\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_8a825f7f53\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Search for completed Phase III randomized controlled trials involving FMT or defined microbiome consortia combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors. Verify completion status and primary outcome data for these specific high-level trials.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap requires confirmation of completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials. This study is a Phase I single-arm trial, not a Phase III RCT. While it mentions a planned Phase II randomized trial (ME.17), it does not provide data on any completed Phase III trials confirming efficacy.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports results from the MIMic trial, a multicenter, single-arm Phase I safety trial evaluating oral FMT combined with anti-PD1 therapy in 20 patients with advanced melanoma. The study found that the combination was safe and associated with an objective response rate (ORR) of 65%, median progression-free survival (mPFS) of 29.6 months, and median overall survival (mOS) of 52.8 months. These outcomes were compared favorably to historical randomized controlled trials (RCTs) of anti-PD1 monotherapy. The authors explicitly state that the sample size was small, there was no control arm, and the results are hypothesis-generating. The study identifies a subsequent randomized Phase II Canadian study (ME.17, NCT06623461) designed to evaluate the potential benefit of this intervention.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0577\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_8a825f7f53\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_8a825f7f53\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0215\",\n      \"title\": \"Microbiome Modulation for the Treatment of Solid Neoplasms\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1200/jco-25-00374\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"5b4da7a4eb5afd77f649e4aaa0b69c1db2666e97\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"As a review article, the approach involves a critical appraisal of published literature rather than primary data generation. The authors systematically discuss: 1) Preclinical models identifying key bacterial species; 2) Clinical correlations between stool microbiome composition and ICI outcomes; 3) The impact of antibiotics on survival; 4) Specific intervention trials for prebiotics (dietary fiber), probiotics (CBM588, SER-401), and FMT across different tumor types and lines of therapy. They compare results against historical benchmarks and note limitations such as small sample sizes and heterogeneity in trial designs.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive synthesis of current evidence linking gut microbiome composition to ICI efficacy in solid tumors, categorizing strategies into prebiotics, probiotics, and FMT.\",\n            \"limits\": \"The field lacks large, definitive randomized trials; most evidence comes from small phase I/II studies or retrospective analyses. The review acknowledges that widespread adoption is premature and mechanisms are not fully elucidated.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Highlights the complexity of microbial effects, noting that both low and excessively high levels of certain bacteria (e.g., Akkermansia) may be suboptimal, suggesting a need for balanced composition rather than single-species enrichment.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Does not provide causal mechanistic proof for all observed associations; many findings are correlative.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific ongoing trials and future directions, such as stratification based on baseline microbiome composition.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Cannot predict the success of future trials or resolve uncertainties regarding universal vs. personalized application of microbiome therapies.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Preclinical and observational clinical studies\",\n            \"finding\": \"Specific bacterial taxa (Ruminococcaceae, Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) correlate with favorable responses to anti-PD-1 antibodies, while antibiotic use before/during ICI initiation is associated with higher primary resistance and shorter overall survival.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Clinical trials of Probiotics (CBM588)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Small prospective trials in mRCC showed significantly improved progression-free survival and overall response rates when CBM588 was added to ICI-based regimens, though sample sizes were limited (n=30 per arm).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Clinical trials of FMT\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT combined with ICIs showed encouraging results in refractory melanoma (ORR 30%) and frontline settings for melanoma (ORR 65%), NSCLC (ORR 77%), and RCC (12-month PFS improved from 35% to 67%). However, benefits were not consistent in all contexts, such as microsatellite-stable colorectal cancer where outcomes were comparable to historical controls without FMT.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Biomarker development\",\n            \"finding\": \"The TOPOSCORE biomarker, integrating Akkermansia with other microbial groups, showed significant correlation with overall survival in cohorts of mNSCLC, mRCC, and urothelial carcinoma.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"The paper addresses the growing interest in microbiome modulation as a therapeutic strategy to overcome resistance to ICIs in oncology. It seeks to summarize current evidence regarding which microbial species are beneficial or detrimental, how disruption affects outcomes, and whether specific modulation strategies (prebiotics, probiotics, FMT) show clinical benefit.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the interplay between the gut microbiome and immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in patients with solid malignancies, specifically advanced melanoma, metastatic renal cell carcinoma (mRCC), and metastatic non–small cell lung cancer (mNSCLC). It covers preclinical evidence, clinical observations of microbial associations, and various modulation strategies including prebiotics, probiotics, and fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT).\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This article is a narrative review that critically appraises existing studies on how gut microbiome modulation can enhance the efficacy of ICIs in solid tumors. It synthesizes preclinical findings linking specific bacterial taxa to treatment response, discusses the negative impact of antibiotic-induced dysbiosis, and evaluates clinical data from trials involving dietary interventions, probiotic supplementation, and FMT. The authors highlight promising signals but emphasize the lack of large-scale randomized controlled trials needed to establish standard of care.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review serves as a foundational overview for understanding the current state of microbiome modulation in ICI-treated solid tumors. It provides concrete examples of successful and failed interventions, specific bacterial markers associated with response, and the methodological challenges in this field. It is particularly useful for establishing the background of why microbiome matters, summarizing the landscape of clinical trials, and highlighting the gap between preclinical promise and clinical validation.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Most clinical data are from small, non-randomized, or early-phase trials. Cannot definitively prove causality for all associations. The review itself notes that large RCTs are lacking.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Summarizes clinical evidence showing that specific gut microbes (Ruminococcaceae, Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) are associated with improved ICI outcomes, while antibiotic disruption correlates with poorer survival. Provides ORR and PFS data from FMT and probiotic trials in melanoma, RCC, and NSCLC.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to answer how the microbiome modulates efficacy by providing observational correlations and early-phase clinical trial results. Supports the argument that microbiome status is a determinant of ICI success.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"The review is descriptive and does not present new mechanistic experiments. Mechanisms are largely inferred from preclinical models or translational analyses of small cohorts. Does not provide detailed molecular pathway analysis.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Discusses potential mechanisms indirectly through observed effects: e.g., dietary fiber enhancing postbiotic production, vancomycin preconditioning disrupting immunity, and intratumoral Gammaproteobacteria hindering gemcitabine response. Mentions the role of short-chain fatty acids.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides context for mechanistic hypotheses. Can be used to illustrate proposed pathways (e.g., SCFA production, immune activation) that require further investigation.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Useful for introducing the topic and justifying the focus on microbiome in ICI resistance/sensitivity.\",\n            \"material\": \"The review establishes the rationale for microbiome modulation, citing preclinical discoveries (Sivan et al., Vétizou et al.) and the general interplay between gut microbiome and immunity.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Background and Context\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Illustrates that microbiome effects are context-dependent (tumor type, prior treatments), supporting nuanced discussions in the review about patient selection and disease specificity.\",\n            \"material\": \"Contrasting results: FMT worked well in melanoma/RCC/NSCLC but showed no added benefit over historical controls in microsatellite-stable colorectal cancer.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Example of Heterogeneity in Response\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Highlights limitations in current research design that the broader review should address when evaluating the strength of evidence.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion of small sample sizes, lack of standardization in FMT donors (responders vs. healthy volunteers), and conflicting data on single-biomarker utility (Akkermansia levels).\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Methodological Challenges\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review is a narrative summary, not a systematic meta-analysis. Do not treat its conclusions as definitive consensus.\",\n          \"Clinical trial results cited are primarily from Phase I/II studies with small sample sizes; avoid overstating efficacy as established fact.\",\n          \"Mechanistic claims are largely based on preclinical models or correlational clinical data; distinguish between observed association and proven mechanism.\",\n          \"The review explicitly states that large randomized trials are lacking; do not imply that these strategies are standard of care.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Gut microbiome composition and ICI response\",\n          \"Antibiotic-induced dysbiosis and resistance\",\n          \"Prebiotic interventions (dietary fiber, camu camu)\",\n          \"Probiotic interventions (CBM588, SER-401)\",\n          \"Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) protocols and outcomes\",\n          \"Microbial biomarkers (TOPOSCORE, Akkermansia abundance)\",\n          \"Tumor-specific intratumoral microbiome effects\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0215\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5b4da7a4eb5afd77f649e4aaa0b69c1db2666e97\\\\660270471cab72ffe5452e7ae7eaf376\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5b4da7a4eb5afd77f649e4aaa0b69c1db2666e97\\\\660270471cab72ffe5452e7ae7eaf376\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0343\",\n      \"title\": \"Integrative Oncology Approaches to Supporting Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Treatment of Solid Tumours\",\n      \"year\": \"2024\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/s11912-023-01492-4\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"93c5dd39ff702a807d9b564fd630d241e76ebcba\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"作为一篇叙述性综述，作者基于现有文献（包括观察性研究、临床试验、指南和机制研究），系统性地归纳了影响ICI疗效的非肿瘤内在因素（如炎症、微生物群、生活方式），并据此分类讨论了各类整合肿瘤学干预手段（营养、运动、微生物调节、补充剂、心理干预、草药等）的证据等级、潜在机制及临床建议。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了整合肿瘤学策略支持ICI治疗的全面概览，特别强调了从“预测”转向“响应行动”的转化医学思路（如利用炎症标志物指导干预时机）。\",\n            \"limits\": \"所引用的多数证据来自观察性研究或小样本试验，缺乏大规模随机对照试验（RCT）的确证；对于许多干预措施（如特定益生菌、中草药、身心疗法），其因果效应和最佳剂量/方案尚未明确。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"明确了医疗大麻在ICI治疗中的潜在负面信号，以及维生素D补充的具体获益阈值。\",\n            \"limits\": \"对某些新兴领域（如真菌疗法、具体中药方剂）仅提及预临床证据或正在进行的试验，缺乏直接的临床结果支持。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"成人实体瘤患者，基线或治疗期间\",\n            \"finding\": \"系统性炎症标志物（如NLR, CRP, LIPI）升高与较差的ICI预后相关；CRP flare（治疗后初期CRP升高后下降）可能与良好的免疫反应相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"饮食干预\",\n            \"finding\": \"摄入至少20g/天膳食纤维的地中海式饮食与晚期黑色素瘤患者的无进展生存期（PFS）延长相关；避免超加工食品和高糖饮料有助于维持健康的肠道微生物群。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"运动干预\",\n            \"finding\": \"回顾性研究显示，接受仑伐替尼联合抗PD-1治疗的肝细胞癌患者中，活跃运动组比久坐组具有更长的总生存期（OS）和无进展生存期（PFS）；但针对ICI的特异性RCT数据仍有限。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"肠道微生物群调节\",\n            \"finding\": \"粪菌移植（FMT）可使部分ICI耐药患者产生客观缓解；口服活体生物疗法（LBTs，如MRx0518, CBM588）显示出免疫调节和增强ICI疗效的潜力，且可能减轻胃肠道irAEs。益生菌效果存在争议，取决于菌株特异性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"维生素D补充\",\n            \"finding\": \"PROVIDENCE前瞻性研究表明，将血清25-羟基维生素D水平补充至≥75 nmol/l可显著延长晚期癌症患者的OS，降低疾病控制失败风险，并减少甲状腺irAEs的发生率。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"医疗大麻使用\",\n            \"finding\": \"一项前瞻性观察性研究发现，医疗大麻的使用与时间至肿瘤进展缩短及总生存期（OS）显著降低相关；尽管另一项小样本研究未达统计学显著差异，但鉴于潜在风险，建议谨慎使用。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"慢性压力与身心疗法\",\n            \"finding\": \"治疗前的情绪困扰和慢性心理压力被证实是黑色素瘤和非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者ICI疗效差的独立预后因素；身心疗法（瑜伽、太极等）在此领域尚需进一步临床研究。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"随着ICI在实体瘤中的广泛应用，如何识别并利用低成本、低毒性的整合肿瘤学策略来支持ICI治疗，以提高疗效、改善预后并最小化免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"成人实体瘤患者接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗期间，整合肿瘤学（Integrative Oncology, IO）策略的应用与潜力。涵盖营养、运动、微生物组调节、补充剂及心理干预等支持性措施。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇综述，旨在评估整合肿瘤学策略在支持成人实体瘤患者进行免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗中的作用和实际应用。文章指出，除了肿瘤内在因素外，宿主相关的系统性炎症、肠道微生物群、饮食和遗传背景显著影响ICI疗效。综述梳理了当前有前景的支持性干预措施，包括地中海式高纤维饮食、规律运动、活体生物疗法、减少质子泵抑制剂（PPI）和抗生素使用、维生素D补充等，并警告医疗大麻可能带来的生存期缩短风险。文章强调通过多模式术前康复（prehabilitation）和术中支持来优化患者状态，以改善治疗效果并最小化不良反应。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物群如何调节ICI疗效提供了重要的临床背景和机制线索。它汇总了关于微生物群组成、饮食纤维、活体生物疗法（LBTs）、粪菌移植（FMT）以及抗生素/PPI使用对ICI反应影响的证据。虽然它本身不提出新的分子机制实验数据，但它清晰地指出了微生物群作为关键中介变量的地位，并列出了具体的干预手段（如特定菌株、纤维摄入量）及其对应的临床结局变化，可直接用于回答“微生物群如何调节ICI疗效”及其背后的临床关联。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些发现主要基于观察性研究和小型试点研究，因果关系的确立程度不一；不同癌症类型（主要是黑色素瘤、NSCLC、HCC）的结果不能直接泛化到所有实体瘤。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了肠道微生物群调节ICI疗效的临床证据链：包括FMT诱导缓解的案例、LBTs（如MRx0518, CBM588）增强疗效和减轻irAEs的数据、以及饮食纤维（>20g/d）与PFS延长的关联。同时指出了益生菌效果的菌株特异性和矛盾性。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于描述肠道微生物群作为ICI疗效关键调节因子的临床表现；列举具体的微生物干预策略及其对肿瘤反应（ORR, PFS, OS）的影响方向。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制部分多为动物模型或体外研究推论（如L. reuteri的机制），临床人体内的确切分子通路细节在所给材料中未完全展开；压力机制主要基于动物研究和人类相关性观察，直接因果链条需结合其他文献验证。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"阐述了微生物群影响ICI疗效的部分机制路径：包括短链脂肪酸（SCFAs）驱动CD4/CD8 T细胞激活、促进IFN表达；特定菌株代谢物（如I3A）促进肿瘤内CD8+ T细胞浸润；以及系统性炎症（IL-6, IL-8）作为负向调节因子。同时提到了慢性压力通过HPA轴/SNS改变肿瘤微环境（TME）的间接机制。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释微生物群介导ICI疗效的生物学机制，特别是免疫细胞活化、细胞因子释放和肿瘤微环境重塑方面的通路。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"帮助界定综述的范围，说明除了微生物群外，还有哪些宿主因素（如炎症、压力、营养）共同作用影响ICI疗效。\",\n            \"material\": \"综述定义了整合肿瘤学，概述了术前康复（prehabilitation）的概念，以及除微生物群外的其他支持策略（运动、维生素D、大麻禁忌）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供背景信息：整合肿瘤学在ICI治疗中的整体定位和现状\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"通过对比积极干预（如LBTs、高纤维饮食）与潜在有害干预（如医疗大麻、滥用抗生素）的效果，强化微生物群/炎症调节的重要性。\",\n            \"material\": \"医疗大麻导致OS缩短的观察性数据；维生素D补充降低irAEs的数据；VAC（槲寄生提取物）的安全性数据。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供对比案例：不同干预措施的效果差异和安全警示\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述主要聚焦于成人实体瘤，特别是黑色素瘤、NSCLC、肾细胞癌和肝癌，结果可能不适用于血液肿瘤或其他罕见实体瘤。\",\n          \"关于肠道微生物群的许多结论来自观察性研究或小样本试验，需谨慎推断因果关系；例如，饮食纤维的益处可能与整体健康生活方式有关，而非单一成分作用。\",\n          \"益生菌的效果具有高度的菌株特异性，综述中提到的矛盾结果（Spencer vs Bender）提示不能一概而论地推荐或禁止所有益生菌。\",\n          \"医疗大麻的负面结果主要来自观察性研究，可能存在混杂因素（如疼痛严重程度、吸烟史），尽管综述建议谨慎，但不能视为绝对的禁忌证证据。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiome composition and diversity\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) efficacy in solid tumours\",\n          \"faecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"live biotherapeutics (LBTs)\",\n          \"dietary fibre intake\",\n          \"Mediterranean diet\",\n          \"proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and antibiotics impact\",\n          \"systemic inflammation markers (NLR, CRP)\",\n          \"vitamin D repletion\",\n          \"medical cannabis safety signals\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0343\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\93c5dd39ff702a807d9b564fd630d241e76ebcba\\\\62c866a3c83122fd1324635be4e5647e\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\93c5dd39ff702a807d9b564fd630d241e76ebcba\\\\62c866a3c83122fd1324635be4e5647e\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0585\",\n      \"title\": \"Gut Microbiota in Advanced Non-small-cell Lung Cancer Receiving Chemo-immunotherapy: An Ancillary Biomarker Study from the Phase III trial JCOG2007 (NIPPON).\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.jtho.2025.02.026\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"8016df1aa5a8160b7ccd412fa6851a6a508e2731\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"abstract\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"这是一项嵌入在随机对照III期临床试验（JCOG2007, NIPPON）中的辅助生物标志物研究。研究设计包括：1. 队列选择：从入组的295名患者中筛选出270名提供基线粪便样本的患者进行分析。2. 分组比较：比较两种一线治疗方案（PC vs NIC）。3. 微生物分析：对基线粪便样本进行16S rDNA测序，进行α/β多样性分析和LEfSe差异丰度分析。4. 临床关联分析：将微生物特征（多样性、特定菌属丰度）与主要临床终点（OS超过12或18个月的亚群区分、总体OS、≥Grade 4严重不良事件发生率）进行统计学关联分析，并进行了按治疗方案的亚组分析。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了在大型III期临床试验背景下，肠道微生物群与NSCLC化疗-免疫治疗预后及安全性关联的具体实证数据。识别了特定的细菌属（如Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus）作为潜在的预后和安全性标志物，并提示了方案特异性的微生物效应。\",\n            \"limits\": \"仅基于摘要信息，无法评估多变量校正模型的完整性、多重检验校正方法、16S测序的具体流程细节及批次效应处理。结果主要关联观察性，因果机制未阐明。NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31的结果需结合具体统计显著性和置信区间解读。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"整体队列（n=270），根据OS是否超过12或18个月划分的亚群\",\n            \"finding\": \"β多样性分析显示不同生存时间亚群具有不同的微生物结构。LEfSe分析发现，Fusicatenibacter、Butyricicoccus和Blautia在长生存患者中富集。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"整体队列，关注严重不良事件（≥ Grade 4 AEs）\",\n            \"finding\": \"较低的微生物α多样性及某些特定菌属的存在与较高的严重AE风险相关。相反，有益菌属（如Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus）的高丰度与较低的严重AE风险相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"按治疗方案分层（NIC臂 vs CP/PC臂）\",\n            \"finding\": \"在NIC组中，Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus的高丰度与更好的OS相关（HR分别为0.56和0.52），优于CP组。而在NIC组中，Prevotellaceae NK3B31的高丰度与更高的死亡风险相关（HR=2.33）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管免疫治疗改变了NSCLC的治疗格局，但缺乏可靠的生物标志物来指导治疗选择。肠道微生物群虽被视为潜在生物标志物，但其在NSCLC化疗-免疫联合治疗中的具体作用尚不清楚。本研究试图回答：肠道微生物群的组成和多样性是否能预测晚期NSCLC患者在接受不同化疗-免疫联合方案时的预后（OS）和治疗安全性（严重AEs）？\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"针对无驱动基因改变的晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，基于JCOG2007（NIPPON）III期临床试验的辅助生物标志物研究。研究对象为接受培美曲塞+铂类化疗联合帕博利珠单抗（PC组）或纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗联合铂类化疗（NIC组）的患者，分析基线粪便样本中的肠道微生物群。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究旨在探索肠道微生物群作为晚期NSCLC化疗-免疫治疗疗效和安全性生物标志物的潜力。研究利用JCOG2007试验中270名有基线粪便样本患者的16S rDNA测序数据，分析了微生物多样性及特定菌属丰度与总生存期（OS）及严重不良事件（AEs）之间的关联。结果显示，微生物结构随生存时间延长而变化，特定产丁酸菌属富集于长生存患者，且与较低的严重AE风险相关；此外，不同治疗方案下微生物对预后的影响存在差异。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于肠道微生物群如何调节实体瘤（特别是NSCLC）免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效及安全性的直接临床证据。其核心价值在于不仅关联了微生物与疗效（OS），还关联了与免疫治疗密切相关的毒性（严重AEs），并揭示了不同免疫检查点抑制剂组合（PD-1 vs PD-1+CTLA-4）下的微生物响应差异，有助于回答“哪些微生物有益/有害”以及“机制是否受药物类型影响”的问题。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"结果来自NSCLC特定人群，且为观察性关联。NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31显示负面关联，提示微生物效应具有复杂性和情境依赖性。不能直接外推至所有实体瘤或所有ICI单药治疗。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了肠道微生物群与ICI疗效关联的临床队列证据。具体发现：Fusicatenibacter和Butyricicoccus的高丰度与更长的OS相关，特别是在NIC（纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗）方案中效果显著（HR 0.56/0.52）。同时，这些菌属也与较低的严重AE风险相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持综述中关于“有益微生物促进ICI疗效”的论点。可用于举例说明特定菌属（产丁酸菌）在改善预后中的作用。可用于讨论微生物与免疫治疗毒性的关联（安全性维度）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"本研究未提供直接的机制验证（如动物模型、体外实验或代谢产物测量）。关联不等于因果。方案特异性结果的生物学解释需谨慎，可能涉及药物代谢、免疫微环境改变等多重因素。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了微生物群落结构与临床结局（生存期和毒性）的关联数据，暗示了微生物可能通过调节宿主免疫反应（进而影响疗效和毒性）发挥作用，但未直接测定分子机制。指出了不同免疫检查点阻断策略（PD-1 vs CTLA-4+PD-1）下微生物效应的差异性（方案特异性）。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中讨论“微生物效应的情境依赖性”或“药物-微生物相互作用”。虽然不直接揭示分子机制，但其发现的特定菌属（如产丁酸菌）可作为后续机制研究的候选靶点或生物标志物。可用于论证为何需要针对不同免疫方案定制微生物干预策略。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"为综述提供引言部分的背景支持，强调开发微生物生物标志物的临床需求和紧迫性。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究背景指出免疫治疗改变了NSCLC治疗格局，但缺乏可靠生物标志物，肠道微生物群被视为潜在标志物。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景与概念框架\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于展示微生物效应的异质性，避免过度简化“好菌/坏菌”的二元论。提示在整合微生物数据时需要考虑具体的菌群组成和可能的拮抗作用。\",\n            \"material\": \"NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31高丰度与高死亡风险（HR=2.33）相关，而Fusicatenibacter等则有益。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"实例与反例\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"反映领域内对于微生物指导临床决策的展望，可用于综述的讨论部分，探讨未来转化医学的方向和挑战。\",\n            \"material\": \"结论建议将肠道微生物谱分析整合到临床实践中以优化一线治疗策略。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"实践知识\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究为观察性附属研究，关联结果不能直接推断因果关系。\",\n          \"结果基于16S rDNA测序，分辨率限于属水平，且未提供功能基因组或代谢组数据，机制解释受限。\",\n          \"NIC组中Prevotellaceae NK3B31的负面结果需警惕，可能与样本量、统计效力或特定亚群特征有关，不宜简单概括为该类菌普遍有害。\",\n          \"方案特异性结果（NIC vs PC）的差异可能受药物相互作用、患者基线特征或其他混杂因素影响，需谨慎解读。\",\n          \"摘要未提供详细的统计校正方法（如多重检验校正、协变量调整），影响结果的确信度评估。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiota composition\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)\",\n          \"non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC)\",\n          \"chemo-immunotherapy\",\n          \"overall survival (OS)\",\n          \"adverse events (AEs)\",\n          \"16S rDNA sequencing\",\n          \"Fusicatenibacter\",\n          \"Butyricicoccus\",\n          \"Blautia\",\n          \"Prevotellaceae NK3B31\",\n          \"microbial diversity (alpha/beta)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study highlights regimen-specific effects, noting that microbial associations with outcomes differed between the PD-1 inhibitor-based regimen (PC) and the dual checkpoint blockade regimen (NIC). It identifies specific bacterial genera (Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus, Blautia) enriched in patients with longer OS (>12 or >18 months). It also notes that lower microbial alpha diversity and presence of certain taxa were linked to a higher risk of serious (≥ Grade 4) AEs. The authors conclude that gut microbiota may serve as a biomarker for chemo-immunotherapy in advanced NSCLC.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"No further action required; the gap is fulfilled by the existence of the JCOG2007 phase III RCT described in this study.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"fulfilled\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports on the JCOG2007 trial, a completed phase III randomized controlled trial comparing pembrolizumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (PC) and nivolumab-ipilimumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (NIC) in treatment-naïve advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients. The trial enrolled 295 patients, with an ancillary biomarker analysis performed on baseline fecal samples from 270 patients using 16S rDNA sequencing. The study found that gut microbiota composition was associated with overall survival (OS) and adverse events (AEs). Specifically, higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter and Butyricicoccus was linked to better OS in the NIC arm compared to the PC arm, and these genera were also associated with a lower risk of serious AEs. Conversely, higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31 was associated with higher mortality risk in the NIC arm.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study highlights regimen-specific effects, noting that microbial associations with outcomes differed between the PD-1 inhibitor-based regimen (PC) and the dual checkpoint blockade regimen (NIC). It identifies specific bacterial genera (Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus, Blautia) enriched in patients with longer OS (>12 or >18 months). It also notes that lower microbial alpha diversity and presence of certain taxa were linked to a higher risk of serious (≥ Grade 4) AEs. The authors conclude that gut microbiota may serve as a biomarker for chemo-immunotherapy in advanced NSCLC.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"No further action required; the gap is fulfilled by the existence of the JCOG2007 phase III RCT described in this study.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"fulfilled\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports on the JCOG2007 trial, a completed phase III randomized controlled trial comparing pembrolizumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (PC) and nivolumab-ipilimumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy (NIC) in treatment-naïve advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients. The trial enrolled 295 patients, with an ancillary biomarker analysis performed on baseline fecal samples from 270 patients using 16S rDNA sequencing. The study found that gut microbiota composition was associated with overall survival (OS) and adverse events (AEs). Specifically, higher abundance of Fusicatenibacter and Butyricicoccus was linked to better OS in the NIC arm compared to the PC arm, and these genera were also associated with a lower risk of serious AEs. Conversely, higher abundance of Prevotellaceae NK3B31 was associated with higher mortality risk in the NIC arm.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0585\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"input_attributed\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"no complete research body was indexed\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_empty\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"html\",\n            \"reason\": \"html_challenge_or_login\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"coverage\",\n            \"reason\": \"only abstract or non-title snippets were available\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"abstract_only\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_3636101ad7\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0297\",\n      \"title\": \"The gut microbiome in lung cancer: from pathogenesis to precision therapy\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3389/fmicb.2025.1606684\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"8211fc48bb00f738157c8859ad0748cc7d220f09\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"The authors conducted a narrative synthesis of literature from microbiology, immunology, and oncology. They organized the content into thematic sections: introduction to the gut-lung axis, alterations in microbiota composition and metabolism in lung cancer patients, microbial biomarkers for early detection, prediction of immunotherapy response, modulation of drug efficacy, microbiota-targeted therapeutic interventions, and mechanistic insights. The review integrates findings from clinical studies, animal models, and multi-omics analyses to construct a cohesive understanding of the gut-lung axis in lung cancer.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive overview of the gut-lung axis in lung cancer, linking specific microbial taxa and metabolites to clinical outcomes in diagnosis and immunotherapy.\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it does not present new primary data or perform a systematic meta-analysis. The causal relationships described are largely inferred from correlative clinical studies and preclinical models, which may vary across populations.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific microbial signatures (e.g., A. muciniphila, SCFA producers) and metabolic profiles as potential biomarkers for predicting ICI response.\",\n            \"limits\": \"The generalizability of these biomarkers depends on the heterogeneity of patient cohorts and sequencing methodologies used in the cited studies, which are not standardized in this review.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Discusses therapeutic interventions like FMT and probiotics as adjuncts to cancer therapy, highlighting their potential to overcome resistance.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Clinical translation faces challenges including interindividual variability, lack of standardized protocols for FMT/probiotics, and long-term safety concerns, which are noted but not resolved.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"In lung cancer patients compared to healthy controls\",\n            \"finding\": \"Patients exhibit dysbiosis characterized by increased abundance of Ruminococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, Lachnospiraceae, and decreased levels of Faecalibacterium, Bifidobacterium, and Veillonella. Metabolic profiles show reduced SCFA production and increased lactate.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"For early detection of lung cancer\",\n            \"finding\": \"A panel of 13 microbial biomarkers was identified with high diagnostic potential, achieving an AUC of 92.4% in discovery and 67.7% in validation cohorts using a Patient Discrimination Index (PDI).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Predicting response to Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Baseline presence of Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium spp., and Clostridiales correlates with improved ICI outcomes. Higher levels of acetate, propionate, and butyrate are associated with prolonged progression-free survival. A TOPOSCORE integrating species interaction groups and A. muciniphila abundance predicts individual responses.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Impact of antibiotics and probiotics on ICI efficacy\",\n            \"finding\": \"Antibiotic use prior to ICI therapy is associated with reduced overall survival and impaired efficacy. Probiotic supplementation (e.g., Bifidobacterium, Clostridium butyricum) may enhance ICI efficacy and improve survival outcomes in NSCLC patients.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Mechanisms of action\",\n            \"finding\": \"Gut microbes modulate lung immunity via metabolites (SCFAs), antigen presentation, and immune cell trafficking. Specific bacteria like A. muciniphila can translocate to tumor tissues, restructure the intratumoral ecosystem, and restore exhausted CD8+ T cells, enhancing anti-PD-1 therapy efficacy.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To synthesize existing evidence regarding how the gut microbiome influences lung cancer pathogenesis and treatment efficacy, particularly in the context of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), and to identify potential therapeutic strategies and biomarkers derived from this interaction.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the gut-lung axis in the context of lung cancer, specifically examining non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) and small cell lung carcinoma. It covers pathogenesis, early detection biomarkers, mechanisms of immune modulation, and therapeutic interventions including immunotherapy response prediction and microbiota-targeted treatments.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This is a narrative review synthesizing current knowledge on the role of the gut microbiome in lung cancer development, diagnosis, and treatment. It outlines the bidirectional communication between intestinal microbiota and pulmonary health via immune, neural, and metabolic pathways. The paper highlights microbial signatures predictive of disease and therapy response, discusses microbiota-targeted interventions such as probiotics and fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), and elucidates mechanistic insights into microbial-immune crosstalk. It aims to provide a comprehensive framework for leveraging the gut microbiome in personalized lung cancer management.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review serves as a foundational resource for understanding the gut-lung axis in lung cancer, particularly its impact on immunotherapy efficacy. It provides detailed descriptions of microbial signatures associated with ICI response, mechanistic pathways involving metabolites and immune cells, and emerging therapeutic strategies. It is highly relevant for facets concerning how the microbiome modulates ICI efficacy and the underlying mechanisms.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Focuses primarily on lung cancer; while it mentions other cancers briefly, the detailed evidence is lung-specific. Causal inference is limited to observational associations and preclinical models.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details the correlation between specific gut microbiota compositions (e.g., A. muciniphila, SCFA-producing bacteria) and improved clinical outcomes in solid tumors treated with ICIs. It also notes that antibiotic use negatively impacts ICI efficacy.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to support arguments about the positive or negative influence of specific microbial taxa on ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Provides examples of predictive biomarkers.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms are often derived from mouse models or inferred from human correlative data. The review acknowledges complexity and multiple interacting pathways rather than a single linear mechanism.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Elaborates on mechanisms including: 1) Metabolite-mediated regulation (SCFAs, acetate, butyrate influencing immune cell function); 2) Immune cell priming and trafficking (dendritic cells, T-cell activation, CD8+ T cell infiltration); 3) Direct translocation of bacteria to tumor sites; 4) Modulation of bile acid metabolism affecting Treg migration.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides specific mechanistic pathways to explain how gut microbes affect tumor immunity and drug response. Useful for detailing the biological basis of microbiome-immunotherapy interactions.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Establishes the theoretical framework for why gut microbiome matters in lung cancer, relevant to any discussion of extraintestinal effects of gut microbes.\",\n            \"material\": \"Description of the bidirectional network connecting intestinal microbiota with pulmonary health via immune, neural, and metabolic pathways.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Background context on the gut-lung axis concept\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Provides practical examples of how microbiome modulation is being explored clinically, useful for discussing future directions or intervention strategies.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion of probiotics, prebiotics, FMT, and dietary interventions as adjuncts to immunotherapy.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Examples of therapeutic interventions\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Allows for comparison of microbiome-Ici interactions across different solid tumors, though the primary focus remains lung cancer.\",\n            \"material\": \"Brief mentions of melanoma, renal, and bladder carcinomas regarding antibiotic impact on ICIs.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contrast with other cancer types\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review is a narrative synthesis, not a systematic review or meta-analysis; therefore, it does not provide pooled effect sizes or rigorous statistical aggregation of all available evidence.\",\n          \"Many mechanistic claims are based on preclinical (mouse) models or correlative clinical studies; causal relationships in humans are not definitively proven within this text.\",\n          \"The term 'solid tumours' in the user's plan is broader than the paper's primary focus on lung cancer; users must extrapolate carefully when applying lung-specific findings to other solid tumors.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut-lung axis\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)\",\n          \"non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC)\",\n          \"microbial biomarkers (Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium)\",\n          \"short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs)\",\n          \"fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"probiotics\",\n          \"antibiotic-induced dysbiosis\",\n          \"tumor microenvironment modulation\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0297\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\8211fc48bb00f738157c8859ad0748cc7d220f09\\\\5faefb771e9af4b8f1b5bd10a8bf9492\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\8211fc48bb00f738157c8859ad0748cc7d220f09\\\\5faefb771e9af4b8f1b5bd10a8bf9492\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0333\",\n      \"title\": \"Navigating the gut–metabolite–immune axis: enhancing efficacy and mitigating toxicity of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803970\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"8e25ec1d06aea0a73a479436c268bc6fab267259\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [\n        \"P0584\"\n      ],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"The authors conducted a comprehensive literature search across PubMed/MEDLINE, Web of Science Core Collection, and Embase up to May 1, 2026. They included original studies, RCTs, cohort studies, and preclinical investigations examining the effects of gut microbiota or metabolites on ICI efficacy or toxicities. Data extraction was guided by STORMS guidelines. As a narrative review, formal risk-of-bias assessment, quality grading, and statistical meta-analysis were not performed. The synthesis is organized thematically: first covering ICI mechanisms, then the heterogeneity of microbiome-ICI interactions, followed by specific metabolite classes, clinical heterogeneity of irAEs, and finally microbiome-based interventions.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive mechanistic synthesis linking specific gut microbes and metabolites to ICI efficacy and irAE mitigation, categorizing interventions by their molecular targets (STING, NOD2, AhR, etc.).\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it lacks formal risk-of-bias assessment and statistical meta-analysis. Findings are largely derived from preclinical models or small clinical cohorts with limited covariate adjustment. The review acknowledges significant methodological heterogeneity across studies and notes that many proposed biomarkers and interventions require validation in large-scale, standardized longitudinal studies.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Melanoma patients treated with anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab)\",\n            \"finding\": \"CTLA-4 blockade reshapes the microbiota, favoring Bacteroides species (e.g., B. fragilis, B. thetaiotaomicron), which are critical for antitumor efficacy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"NSCLC patients treated with anti-PD-1\",\n            \"finding\": \"High relative abundance of Akkermansia muciniphila (Akk-high) was associated with worse overall survival compared to Akk-low groups, challenging the binary 'good vs bad' bacteria concept and highlighting cohort/cancer-type dependency.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Various ICI regimens\",\n            \"finding\": \"Microbial influence is context-dependent; different ICIs rely on distinct microbial signatures (e.g., Faecalibacterium/Ruminococcaceae for anti-PD-1 vs. Bacteroides for anti-CTLA-4).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Tryptophan metabolites (3-IAld, IPA)\",\n            \"finding\": \"3-IAld alleviates colitis and enhances efficacy via AhR/IL-22; IPA provides cardioprotection via AhR/PI3K/AKT signaling.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"SCFAs (Butyrate, Propionate, Acetate)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Butyrate protects against cardiotoxicity via PPARa-CYP4X1 and enhances antigen presentation via HDAC inhibition; Propionate/Acetate promote Treg homeostasis and memory CD8+ T-cell differentiation.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"TMAO\",\n            \"finding\": \"Triggers immunogenic tumor cell death (pyroptosis) via PERK-GSDME pathway, recruiting CD8+ T cells.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Probiotics (LGG, BF839, Akk)\",\n            \"finding\": \"LGG and BF839 activate cGAS-STING axis in dendritic cells to enhance CD8+ T cell infiltration; Akk produces c-di-AMP which directly activates STING.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"FMT interventions\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT from long-term responders to PD-1 therapy showed higher efficacy than healthy donors; response correlated with loss of deleterious baseline species rather than just engraftment of donor strains.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"The clinical utility of ICIs is limited by interpatient variability in efficacy and the occurrence of systemic toxicities (irAEs). The review aims to systematically delineate the microbiome-metabolite-immune axis to understand these variations and optimize the balance between therapeutic gain and toxicity, ultimately guiding the development of precise microbial interventions.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the gut microbiome and its metabolic byproducts as systemic regulators of immune function, specifically in the context of cancer immunotherapy using immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). It covers mechanisms of ICI action (PD-1/PD-L1, CTLA-4, LAG-3, TIGIT, TIM-3), the heterogeneity of microbiome-ICI interactions across different cancer types and cohorts, microbial metabolites (tryptophan derivatives, SCFAs, TMAO, inosine, bile acids, polyamines) as mediators of efficacy and toxicity, and microbiome-based interventions including probiotics, FMT, postbiotics, and dietary fiber.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This narrative review synthesizes current evidence on how the gut microbiota and microbe-derived metabolites regulate the efficacy and immune-related adverse events (irAEs) of ICIs. It highlights specific taxa like Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, and Akkermansia muciniphila that potentiate therapeutic responses via pathways such as cGAS-STING and NOD2. The review also details how microbial metabolites protect organs like the heart and colon from inflammation while enhancing anti-tumor immunity. It evaluates emerging interventions like FMT and next-generation probiotics, noting that while preclinical and Phase I trials show feasibility, substantial hurdles remain regarding cohort heterogeneity, methodological standardization, and the need for large-scale longitudinal studies to establish reproducible predictive models.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review offers a structured framework for understanding the 'gut-metabolite-immune' axis in the context of solid tumor immunotherapy. It provides detailed mechanistic insights into how specific microbial taxa and metabolites modulate ICI efficacy and toxicity, serving as a valuable resource for explaining biological mechanisms and contextualizing clinical heterogeneity. It also summarizes the current state of microbiome-based interventions, highlighting both promising strategies and translational barriers.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Findings are based on a synthesis of various studies with differing methodologies; causal relationships are often inferred from preclinical models or observational data rather than definitive clinical trials.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details the context-dependent nature of microbiome-ICI interactions, showing that beneficial taxa vary by cancer type and ICI regimen (e.g., Bacteroides for CTLA-4, Faecalibacterium for PD-1, variable role of Akk). It also discusses the trade-off between efficacy and toxicity, noting that some microbes protective in one context may be detrimental in another.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to explain why microbiome signatures are not universal and depend on specific treatment protocols and patient populations. Helps address questions about heterogeneity in ICI response.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Many mechanisms are described from murine models or in vitro studies; human concentration thresholds and spatial dynamics within the tumor microenvironment are noted as undefined.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides specific molecular mechanisms linking microbes/metabolites to immune modulation. For example, it describes how LGG and BF839 activate the cGAS-STING pathway, how tryptophan metabolites act via AhR, and how SCFAs influence T-cell function and barrier integrity through epigenetic and receptor-mediated pathways.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Offers detailed mechanistic explanations for how gut microbes influence ICI efficacy and toxicity, suitable for sections discussing biological pathways and molecular targets.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Discusses the limitations of current microbiome research, including methodological heterogeneity, lack of standardized protocols, and the gap between preclinical findings and clinical translation.\",\n            \"material\": \"Critiques the reliance on single-taxa abundance, advocates for topology-based approaches, and highlights logistical barriers like metagenomic turnaround times for precision nutrition.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Provides background on the challenges and future directions in the field, useful for discussing the current state of evidence and areas needing further research.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Summarizes various microbiome-based interventions, including FMT, probiotics, postbiotics, and dietary fiber, detailing their potential benefits and risks.\",\n            \"material\": \"Describes FMT from responders vs. healthy donors, encapsulation technologies for probiotics, and the double-edged nature of antibiotic preconditioning and high-fiber diets.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Offers examples of clinical interventions and their outcomes, useful for comparing different strategies to modulate the microbiome for improved ICI outcomes.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review is a narrative synthesis and does not provide quantitative meta-analytic results; effect sizes and statistical significance should not be inferred from this text alone.\",\n          \"Many mechanistic insights are derived from preclinical models (mice) or small clinical cohorts; extrapolation to broader human populations requires caution.\",\n          \"The role of specific microbes (e.g., Akkermansia) can be context-dependent and even contradictory across studies; avoid treating any single taxon as universally beneficial or detrimental.\",\n          \"Claims about the efficacy of interventions like FMT or probiotics are primarily based on early-phase trials or preclinical data; robust clinical validation is still needed.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs): PD-1, PD-L1, CTLA-4, LAG-3, TIGIT, TIM-3\",\n          \"Gut Microbiota Taxa: Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium, Ruminococcaceae\",\n          \"Microbial Metabolites: Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), Tryptophan derivatives (3-IAld, IPA, ILA), TMAO, Inosine, Secondary bile acids, Polyamines\",\n          \"Signaling Pathways: cGAS-STING, NOD2, AhR, PPARa, HDAC, PERK-GSDME\",\n          \"Interventions: Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT), Probiotics (LGG, BF839), Postbiotics, Dietary Fiber\",\n          \"Clinical Outcomes: Objective Response Rate (ORR), Progression-Free Survival (PFS), Immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review provides detailed mechanistic pathways for microbial modulation of immunity, including cGAS-STING activation by Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG (LGG) and Bacteroides fragilis, NOD2 activation by Enterococcus, and AhR-mediated barrier protection by indole derivatives. It also outlines the context-dependent nature of microbial biomarkers, noting that associations with efficacy or toxicity vary by cancer type (e.g., melanoma vs. hepatobiliary) and ICI regimen. Specific trial data cited include a Phase Ib trial on antibiotic preconditioning (Glitza et al.), a Phase II FMT trial in NSCLC and melanoma (FMT-LUMINate), and a Phase I trial in gastric cancer (Zhang et al.). The authors emphasize that current evidence is constrained by small sample sizes, lack of standardization, and the predominance of preclinical data.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"State that no completed large randomised phase III trial of a microbiome intervention in ICI-treated patients was identified in this material. Note that the study is a narrative review summarizing preclinical and early-phase (I/II) evidence. Highlight that the authors explicitly call for future large-scale randomized controlled trials to validate findings, confirming the absence of such completed Phase III data in the current literature reviewed.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap question asks for a completed large randomised phase III trial of a microbiome intervention in cancer patients receiving ICIs. This study is a narrative review and does not report any completed Phase III trials. It explicitly states that 'substantial hurdles remain' and calls for 'large-scale, multicenter, standardized, longitudinal studies,' indicating that such trials were not completed or reported at the time of writing. Therefore, the success criterion of identifying a completed large Phase III trial is not met.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"The study is a narrative review that synthesizes evidence from preclinical models and early-phase clinical trials (Phase I/II) regarding microbiome interventions in cancer patients receiving immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). It details mechanisms by which specific taxa (e.g., Bacteroides, Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) and metabolites (SCFAs, indoles, TMAO) modulate ICI efficacy and immune-related adverse events (irAEs). The review discusses the feasibility of interventions such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotics, and postbiotics, citing Phase Ib and Phase II trials (e.g., FMT-LUMINate, Glitza et al.) but explicitly notes that large-scale, multicenter, standardized longitudinal studies are needed. It highlights significant heterogeneity in study cohorts and methodologies, preventing the establishment of reproducible predictive models or definitive clinical intervention strategies.\"\n      },\n      \"local_passages\": {\n        \"external_calls\": 0,\n        \"passages\": [\n          {\n            \"best_sentence\": \"The review discusses the feasibility of interventions such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotics, and postbiotics, citing Phase Ib and Phase II trials (e.g., FMT-LUMINate, Glitza et al.) but explicitly notes that large-scale, multicenter, standardized longitudinal studies are needed.\",\n            \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\",\n            \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803970\",\n            \"limits\": [],\n            \"match_terms\": [\n              \"粪菌移植\",\n              \"益生菌\",\n              \"机制\",\n              \"gut microbiome modulate efficacy immune checkpoint inhibitors solid tumours\",\n              \"mechanisms mediate effect\",\n              \"ici\",\n              \"i\",\n              \"ii\",\n              \"iii\",\n              \"nsclc\",\n              \"rct\"\n            ],\n            \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n            \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:290154100\",\n            \"pool_action\": \"already_in_pool\",\n            \"reading_path\": \"\",\n            \"score\": 120.720283,\n            \"section_path\": [\n              \"Supplementary question reading\"\n            ],\n            \"segment_kind\": \"directed_content\",\n            \"source_handle\": \"P0584\",\n            \"text\": \"{\\\"auxiliary_material\\\": \\\"The review provides detailed mechanistic pathways for microbial modulation of immunity, including cGAS-STING activation by Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG (LGG) and Bacteroides fragilis, NOD2 activation by Enterococcus, and AhR-mediated barrier protection by indole derivatives. It also outlines the context-dependent nature of microbial biomarkers, noting that associations with efficacy or toxicity vary by cancer type (e.g., melanoma vs. hepatobiliary) and ICI regimen. Specific trial data cited include a Phase Ib trial on antibiotic preconditioning (Glitza et al.), a Phase II FMT trial in NSCLC and melanoma (FMT-LUMINate), and a Phase I trial in gastric cancer (Zhang et al.). The authors emphasize that current evidence is constrained by small sample sizes, lack of standardization, and the predominance of preclinical data.\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": [], \\\"gap_id\\\": \\\"case_E_phase3_rct\\\", \\\"judgment_path\\\": \\\"F:\\\\\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\\\\\outputs\\\\\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\\\\\03_external_closure\\\\\\\\runs\\\\\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\\\\\source_units\\\\\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\\\", \\\"limitations\\\": [], \\\"material_ready\\\": true, \\\"material_usefulness\\\": \\\"suppor…\",\n            \"title\": \"Navigating the gut–metabolite–immune axis: enhancing efficacy and mitigating toxicity of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n            \"usable_fields\": [],\n            \"year\": \"2026\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"best_sentence\": \"The paper also evaluates emerging microbiome-targeted interventions, such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), next-generation probiotics with encapsulation technologies, postbiotics, and precision nutrition, highlighting the context-dependent nature of these interactions and the need for standardized, multi-omics approaches to develop precise microbial interventions.\",\n            \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260926_adaptive_retrieval\\\\03_external_closure\\\\runs\\\\case_E_phase3_rct\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_93c14070cc\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\",\n            \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803970\",\n            \"limits\": [],\n            \"match_terms\": [\n              \"粪菌移植\",\n              \"益生菌\",\n              \"gut microbiome modulate efficacy immune checkpoint inhibitors solid tumours\"\n            ],\n            \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n            \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:290154100\",\n            \"pool_action\": \"already_in_pool\",\n            \"reading_path\": \"\",\n            \"score\": 17.613751,\n            \"section_path\": [\n              \"general understanding\",\n              \"work summary\"\n            ],\n            \"segment_kind\": \"card_work_summary\",\n            \"source_handle\": \"P0584\",\n            \"text\": \"taxa (e.g., Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia) and metabolites (SCFAs, indole derivatives, TMAO) that modulate antitumor immunity and organ protection. m, Akkermansia) and metabolites (SCFAs, indole derivatives, TMAO) that modulate antitumor immunity and organ protection. The paper also evaluates emerging microbiome-targeted interventions, such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), next-generation probiotics with encapsulation technologies, postbiotics, and precision nutrition, highlighting the context-dependent nature of these interactions and the need for standardized, multi-omics approaches to develop precise microbial interventions.\",\n            \"title\": \"Navigating the gut–metabolite–immune axis: enhancing efficacy and mitigating toxicity of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n            \"usable_fields\": [],\n            \"year\": \"2026\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"question\": \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect? 微生物干预策略的临床转化现状 系统评估粪菌移植（FMT）、活体生物药（LBPs）、益生菌及饮食干预在实体瘤 ICI 治疗中的临床试验数据，区分初治与难治人群的疗效差异，明确证据等级（I/II 期 vs III 期），并分析供体选择、安全性及标准化挑战。 涵盖人体临床试验（I-III 期），聚焦实体瘤（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、RCC、CRC 等）。包括 FMT（供体来源、给药途径）、定义菌群（如 CBM588、MRx0518）、益生菌及饮食干预。明确排除纯临床前研究（除非用于解释机制背景）及非 ICI 背景的微生物干预。严格区分观察性关联与干预性因果证据，标注试验设计类型（单臂 vs RCT）及人群特征（初治 vs 经治）。\",\n        \"reading_mode\": \"focused_local_search\"\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0333\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\8e25ec1d06aea0a73a479436c268bc6fab267259\\\\ffe6b04dd05271a83854d9bf81f87394\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-403bce67afb0fdb07c1f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\8e25ec1d06aea0a73a479436c268bc6fab267259\\\\ffe6b04dd05271a83854d9bf81f87394\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0593\",\n      \"title\": \"Single-cell-based identification of drug synergy with immunotherapy via tumor microenvironment remodeling\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2025-014132\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:286644045\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"研究采用多步骤计算与实验结合的策略：1) 利用TCGA bulk RNA-seq数据和ImmuCellAI去卷积算法，鉴定与免疫浸润负相关或正相关的TIIMGs；2) 使用L1000高通量筛选平台，针对TIIMGs筛选739种化合物的转录组扰动效应，并通过drug-to-cell recall验证；3) 在CT26小鼠模型中进行体内给药，收集肿瘤组织进行scRNA-seq，利用NMF算法从肿瘤细胞中提取异质性程序并聚类为12个MPs；4) 开发MP评分算法（结合Cluster 1激活与Cluster 2抑制的加权）量化TME重塑程度；5) 基于MP评分优先选择候选药物（oxypurinol及其前体allopurinol），通过流式细胞术、LC-MS/MS检测肌苷水平、体外共培养及体内协同实验验证机制；6) 回顾性分析人类膀胱癌患者队列中抗痛风药与抗PD-1治疗的生存关联。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"建立了首个基于单细胞分辨率的药物诱导TME重塑量化框架（resTMe），定义了12个TME元程序（MPs）和MP评分算法，为预测药物-免疫疗法协同作用提供了新工具。\",\n            \"limits\": \"核心scRNA-seq数据仅来源于小鼠模型（CT26），缺乏人源类器官或PDX模型的直接验证，限制了框架在人类复杂TME中的直接外推性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"发现了别嘌醇（Allopurinol）作为抗PD-1疗法的潜在增效剂，阐明了其通过嘌呤补救途径增加肌苷分泌、增强肿瘤免疫原性和T细胞功能的分子机制。\",\n            \"limits\": \"机制验证主要在体外细胞系和小鼠模型中进行；人类临床证据仅来自小样本（n=50）、单中心的回顾性观察性研究，存在混杂因素风险，需前瞻性临床试验确认。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"证实了MDSCs的耗竭是响应性TME的关键标志物，并揭示了低剂量药物可能通过非细胞毒性机制重塑TME以促进免疫浸润。\",\n            \"limits\": \"对于其他候选药物（如epirubicin, vemurafenib）的具体分子机制阐述不如AP深入；不同药物对特定免疫亚群的影响差异较大，尚未形成统一的普适性规则。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"8种癌症类型TCGA数据分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"鉴定出肿瘤细胞特异性免疫浸润调节基因（TIIMGs），其中COAD拥有最多的dpGenes（792个），KIRC最少，揭示了不同癌症间TME调控网络的异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"L1000平台筛选739种化合物\",\n            \"finding\": \"识别出54种在多癌种中重复出现的候选药物，主要靶向CDKs、HDAC1、PI3K等通路。低剂量处理更倾向于产生非细胞毒性的免疫调节效应，而高剂量常伴随免疫抑制。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"CT26小鼠模型scRNA-seq分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"药物处理导致肿瘤细胞比例下降，免疫细胞（特别是巨噬细胞、DCs）浸润增加，但MDSCs显著减少。oxypurinol表现出最强的TME重塑能力，显著降低MDSCs并增加抗原呈递相关细胞。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"MP评分算法应用\",\n            \"finding\": \"定义了12个MPs，其中Cluster 2（含MPs 1,3,6,8,10,12）与抗原加工和呈递通路高度相关。oxypurinol获得最高MP评分，提示其具有最强的TME重塑潜力。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Allopurinol (AP) 联合抗PD-1治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"AP通过抑制黄嘌呤脱氢酶（XDH）增加肌苷合成并分泌至胞外，肌苷上调肿瘤细胞MHC I类和PD-L1表达，增强CD8+ T细胞介导的细胞毒性。在CT26模型中，AP联合抗PD-1实现4/6小鼠完全治愈。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"人类膀胱癌回顾性队列（n=50）\",\n            \"finding\": \"在接受抗PD-1治疗的膀胱癌患者中，同时服用抗痛风药物（如AP或febuxostat）的患者组（n=7）的总生存期（OS）显著优于未服用组（n=43）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"如何在复杂的肿瘤微环境中系统性地识别能够增强免疫疗法疗效的药物组合？目前缺乏量化药物诱导TME重塑的系统框架，且难以预测哪些非抗癌药物可作为免疫治疗的增效剂。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究聚焦于实体瘤（以小鼠结肠癌CT26模型和人类膀胱癌队列为主）的肿瘤微环境（TME），探讨药物诱导的TME重塑如何影响免疫检查点抑制剂（如抗PD-1/PD-L1）的疗效。研究对象包括8种癌症类型的TCGA数据、739种免疫调节化合物、小鼠体内的单细胞转录组数据以及临床回顾性队列。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该研究旨在解决缺乏系统性框架来量化药物诱导的TME重塑的问题。作者首先通过TCGA数据和ImmuCellAI算法鉴定了肿瘤细胞特异性免疫浸润调节基因（TIIMGs），并利用L1000平台筛选出能显著调控这些基因的化合物。随后，在小鼠CT26肿瘤模型中，通过单细胞RNA测序（scRNA-seq）分析了多种药物处理后的TME变化，识别出12个与TME重塑相关的元程序（Meta-programs, MPs）。基于MP富集情况开发了MP评分算法，用于量化TME对药物的响应程度。该算法成功鉴定出别嘌醇（Allopurinol, AP），一种痛风药物，发现其能通过促进肌苷（inosine）的合成与分泌，上调肿瘤细胞的MHC I类和PD-L1表达，从而增强CD8+ T细胞的杀伤活性。在体内实验中，低剂量AP联合抗PD-1治疗实现了4/6的小鼠完全肿瘤清除。此外，一项包含50名膀胱癌患者的回顾性研究显示，接受抗痛风药物治疗的患者在抗PD-1治疗下的总生存期显著优于未使用者。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述提供了关于‘非传统免疫调节药物’（老药新用）如何通过代谢重编程和TME重塑增强ICI疗效的具体案例和机制解释。它不直接涉及肠道菌群，但提供了一个重要的对比维度：即宿主代谢状态（如痛风药物干预）和肿瘤局部代谢产物（肌苷）如何独立于或协同于微生物组影响ICI疗效。其建立的MP评分框架可用于归类不同药物对TME细胞组成的影响模式。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"该研究未涉及肠道菌群，因此不能直接回答菌群如何调制ICI疗效的问题，只能作为平行机制或交互作用的潜在背景。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了非微生物来源的代谢物（肌苷）增强ICI疗效的证据。研究发现别嘌醇通过增加肌苷分泌，增强CD8+ T细胞功能，这与某些肠道菌群代谢产物（如丁酸盐、次级胆汁酸）调节免疫微环境的机制形成互补或对比视角。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于讨论除了微生物组之外，宿主自身代谢干预和肿瘤局部代谢环境如何调节ICI疗效。可作为‘代谢微环境’章节的重要素材。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制仅限于别嘌醇-肌苷轴，不能推广为所有ICI增效剂的通用机制。人类证据仅为相关性，因果链条在人体中尚待确证。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"详细阐述了别嘌醇增强ICI疗效的具体分子机制：抑制XDH -> 增加肌苷 -> 上调MHC I/PD-L1 -> 增强T细胞杀伤。同时指出MDSCs减少是响应性TME的标志。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释‘代谢产物介导的免疫增强’这一具体机制路径。可与菌群代谢产物（如短链脂肪酸）的作用机制进行比较，区分内源性代谢物与外源性（菌群）代谢物的不同作用靶点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"基于小鼠模型数据，人类组织中MDSCs的功能异质性更大，需谨慎外推。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了MDSCs耗竭作为ICI响应生物标志物的实证支持。在scRNA-seq分析中，响应性TME显著特征为MDSCs比例降低。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于综述中关于‘免疫抑制细胞群体在ICI耐药中的作用’或‘预测ICI响应的生物标志物’部分。可与其他研究对比不同免疫抑制细胞（如Tregs, TAMs）在ICI疗效中的权重。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供药物重定位（Drug Repurposing）在免疫肿瘤学中的应用实例。\",\n            \"material\": \"别嘌醇（痛风药）被鉴定为抗PD-1增效剂，并在回顾性临床队列中显示生存获益。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论现有非抗癌药物如何被重新评估用于增强ICI疗效，拓展‘哪些因素调制ICI疗效’的范围 beyond 微生物组和宿主遗传背景。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供TME细胞组成变化的定量分析框架。\",\n            \"material\": \"MP评分算法和12个元程序的定义，量化了抗原呈递、盐应激反应等生物学过程在药物处理后的变化。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为方法论参考，说明如何从单细胞数据中提取TME重塑的特征，用于比较不同干预手段（包括可能的菌群移植或饮食干预）对TME细胞景观的影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供代谢物（肌苷）直接调节T细胞功能的体外和体内证据。\",\n            \"material\": \"AP处理导致肌苷大量分泌，增强OT-I T细胞对B16-OVA肿瘤的杀伤力。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于构建‘代谢-免疫’互作网络，可能与某些产生类似代谢物的菌群菌株的作用机制形成对照或协同讨论。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究的核心对象是小鼠模型和人类膀胱癌回顾性队列，未直接研究肠道菌群。在将其用于回答‘菌群如何调制ICI’问题时，必须明确其作为‘非菌群代谢机制’的对比或补充角色，而非直接证据。\",\n          \"人类临床数据仅为小样本回顾性观察（n=50），存在选择偏倚和混杂因素，不能作为因果关系的确凿证据，仅能作为假设生成的线索。\",\n          \"别嘌醇的机制特异性强（肌苷轴），不能将其效果泛化为所有代谢调节药物或所有ICI增效剂的共同机制。\",\n          \"MP评分和元程序是基于小鼠CT26模型定义的，在不同癌症类型或人源模型中的适用性需进一步验证。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Tumor Microenvironment (TME) Remodeling\",\n          \"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs) Synergy\",\n          \"Metabolic Reprogramming (Inosine/Purine Salvage Pathway)\",\n          \"Myeloid-Derived Suppressor Cells (MDSCs) Depletion\",\n          \"Drug Repurposing for Oncology\",\n          \"Single-cell Transcriptomics in Drug Response\",\n          \"Antigen Presentation (MHC Class I)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study developed a meta-program (MP) scoring algorithm based on scRNA-seq data from mouse tumors to quantify TME remodeling, identifying myeloid-derived suppressor cell (MDSC) depletion as a hallmark of responsive TMEs. It established a resource (scIMD) and web tool (resTMe) for screening immunomodulatory drugs. The analysis covered eight cancer types for gene identification but performed in vivo validation primarily in the CT26 colon carcinoma model.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"case_C2_inosine_tme\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Use this study to support the mechanistic plausibility of inosine-mediated ICI enhancement and to cite indirect human clinical evidence linking xanthine oxidase inhibition to improved survival. However, explicitly state that direct human TME inosine quantification and concentration-response correlation data remain unavailable in this source. Future search strategies should target metabolomics studies profiling human tumor interstitial fluid or tissue specifically for purine metabolites in ICI-treated cohorts.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"This material does not provide the absolute physiological concentration of inosine within the human tumor microenvironment (TME); the concentration data reported is from mouse cell culture supernatants. Furthermore, it does not report a direct statistical correlation between measured human TME inosine levels and ICI efficacy (e.g., ORR, PFS, OS). The human clinical evidence provided is an association between gout medication usage (as a proxy for inosine elevation) and survival, not a correlation based on quantified inosine metabolite levels in patient tissues or fluids. The study explicitly notes its limitation that drug scRNA-seq data were derived from mouse models.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study investigated the synergistic effect of allopurinol (AP) with anti-PD-1 therapy. In mouse CT26 cell cultures, LC-MS/MS analysis showed AP treatment increased extracellular inosine levels by approximately 500 to 1000-fold in supernatants, while cell lysate levels remained unchanged, indicating active export. Mechanistically, AP inhibits xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), elevating hypoxanthine which is converted to inosine via the salvage pathway. In vivo, AP combined with anti-PD-1 achieved tumor-free outcomes in 4/6 mice. In a retrospective human cohort of 50 bladder cancer patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy, the 7 patients concurrently taking gout medications (AP or febuxostat) showed significantly better overall survival compared to the 43 patients who did not. The authors propose that elevated inosine enhances tumor immunogenicity (upregulating MHC class I and PD-L1) and supports T-cell function.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study developed a meta-program (MP) scoring algorithm based on scRNA-seq data from mouse tumors to quantify TME remodeling, identifying myeloid-derived suppressor cell (MDSC) depletion as a hallmark of responsive TMEs. It established a resource (scIMD) and web tool (resTMe) for screening immunomodulatory drugs. The analysis covered eight cancer types for gene identification but performed in vivo validation primarily in the CT26 colon carcinoma model.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"case_C2_inosine_tme\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Use this study to support the mechanistic plausibility of inosine-mediated ICI enhancement and to cite indirect human clinical evidence linking xanthine oxidase inhibition to improved survival. However, explicitly state that direct human TME inosine quantification and concentration-response correlation data remain unavailable in this source. Future search strategies should target metabolomics studies profiling human tumor interstitial fluid or tissue specifically for purine metabolites in ICI-treated cohorts.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"This material does not provide the absolute physiological concentration of inosine within the human tumor microenvironment (TME); the concentration data reported is from mouse cell culture supernatants. Furthermore, it does not report a direct statistical correlation between measured human TME inosine levels and ICI efficacy (e.g., ORR, PFS, OS). The human clinical evidence provided is an association between gout medication usage (as a proxy for inosine elevation) and survival, not a correlation based on quantified inosine metabolite levels in patient tissues or fluids. The study explicitly notes its limitation that drug scRNA-seq data were derived from mouse models.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study investigated the synergistic effect of allopurinol (AP) with anti-PD-1 therapy. In mouse CT26 cell cultures, LC-MS/MS analysis showed AP treatment increased extracellular inosine levels by approximately 500 to 1000-fold in supernatants, while cell lysate levels remained unchanged, indicating active export. Mechanistically, AP inhibits xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH), elevating hypoxanthine which is converted to inosine via the salvage pathway. In vivo, AP combined with anti-PD-1 achieved tumor-free outcomes in 4/6 mice. In a retrospective human cohort of 50 bladder cancer patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy, the 7 patients concurrently taking gout medications (AP or febuxostat) showed significantly better overall survival compared to the 43 patients who did not. The authors propose that elevated inosine enhances tumor immunogenicity (upregulating MHC class I and PD-L1) and supports T-cell function.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0593\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-d48cd64bd12b302ad2ba\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-95bd9a77b14edf06222c\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a7194a689f46f19f0fc9\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-5f82ba52a99d38e52a55\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-5bcddf895da96806b16c\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-c44cb221036f78d05573\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"missing\": [\n              \"jitc-14-3-g001.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g002.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g003.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g004.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g005.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-g006.jpg\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s001.docx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s002.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s003.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s004.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s005.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s006.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s007.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s008.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s009.xlsx\",\n              \"jitc-14-3-s010.tif\"\n            ],\n            \"reason\": \"graphic_members_unmatched\",\n            \"status\": \"partial\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementary_bundle_had_no_safe_matching_images\",\n            \"status\": \"unparsed\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\Nf2f13ca37a1cba78c9d1-acf4314fb7d520c6\\\\round_2\\\\supplements\\\\case_C2_inosine_tme\\\\attempt-h4sdftel\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_7df1939f2d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0578\",\n      \"title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2026-015122\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"d9577b3a29bd8aec60c9e27c6a56a63faf3f4a27\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用单臂、开放标签的IIa期篮子试验设计。纳入标准包括ICI治疗后疾病进展、ECOG评分0-1及可测量病灶。供体需为ICI长期响应者（>12个月响应）。干预措施包括基线后第1周和第5周通过结肠镜进行两次FMT，随后在第14、30、78周可通过灌肠进行最多三次补充FMT。ICI治疗保持不变或重新引入。主要终点评估安全性和ORR（基于iRECIST）。次要终点包括可行性、临床获益率、无进展生存期（PFS）、总生存期（OS）、微生物定植（16S rRNA测序）、免疫反应（质谱流式细胞术分析PBMCs）及生物标志物（血清细胞因子）。统计分析采用描述性统计、Kaplan-Meier生存分析及Cox比例风险模型探索免疫特征与预后的关联，未进行多重检验校正。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"证实了在ICI耐药的晚期实体瘤患者中，使用ICI响应者供体的FMT是安全和可行的。提供了关于FMT后微生物定植动力学（短暂性）和系统性免疫景观（髓系抑制与T细胞活化）的详细生物标志物数据。\",\n            \"limits\": \"样本量小（n=12），试验因入组缓慢提前终止。单臂设计无法确立因果关系，缺乏对照组。未能实现预期的临床疗效（无ORR），限制了对其机制解释的确定性。结果可能受到快速进展性疾病背景的影响，未能在足够长的时间内观察FMT的潜在延迟效应。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"12名ICI耐药晚期实体瘤患者，接受来自ICI响应者的FMT联合ICI治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT安全性良好，无严重FMT相关不良事件导致停药，但50%患者出现免疫相关不良事件（irAEs），多数为轻度。客观缓解率（ORR）为0%，中位PFS为1.5个月，中位OS为10.1个月。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"微生物组分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"首次FMT后一周，大多数受体的微生物组向供体方向偏移（定植成功），但这种相似性在后续时间点大多下降，表明定植多为暂时性。仅1名获得临床获益的患者实现了持久的供体-受体微生物相似性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"免疫微环境分析\",\n            \"finding\": \"基线时高度分化的T细胞环境（CD8+效应记忆T细胞和TEMRA）与改善的PFS和OS相关；而髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型预后较差。CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平随时间增加，高水平的CD14+单核细胞与较短的PFS和OS相关。这些免疫特征与预后的关联在使用ICI治疗起始日期重新计算时依然成立，提示其反映的是宿主对免疫治疗的固有反应能力，而非FMT的直接因果效应。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"早期研究表明FMT可能逆转ICI耐药，但其适用性在其他肿瘤类型中尚不明确，且关于患者选择、供体选择及给药方案的问题仍未解决。本研究试图回答：在ICI耐药的晚期实体瘤患者中，重复FMT是否安全可行，以及是否能通过微生物定植和免疫重塑来恢复ICI的疗效。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"针对晚期实体瘤（黑色素瘤、头颈部鳞癌、肾细胞癌、微卫星不稳定性高胰腺癌）中免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药或进展的患者，评估来自ICI长期响应者的粪菌移植（FMT）联合ICI治疗的安全性和有效性。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究是一项单臂IIa期篮子试验（MITRIC），旨在验证通过FMT调节肠道微生物群能否克服已建立的ICI耐药性。研究纳入了12名在转移性阶段对ICI治疗进展的患者，使用ICI长期响应者作为供体，给予患者最多五次FMT（前两次通过结肠镜，后续通过灌肠），并继续原有ICI治疗。主要终点为FMT相关不良事件和客观缓解率（ORR）。结果显示FMT安全性良好，但未能观察到客观肿瘤反应（ORR为0%），仅5名患者达到疾病稳定。尽管缺乏总体疗效证据，研究发现FMT诱导了短暂的微生物定植，且外周血中的免疫细胞特征（如分化T细胞与髓系细胞的平衡）与生存期显著相关，但这些免疫特征似乎更多反映疾病本身状态而非FMT的特异性干预效果。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文提供了关于FMT在ICI耐药实体瘤中应用的阴性疗效结果及详细的转化医学发现。对于综述而言，其核心价值在于反驳了“FMT能普遍逆转ICI耐药”的简单假设，并指出了患者选择（如疾病进展速度、既往免疫状态）和供体匹配的重要性。同时，它提供了关于肠道微生物定植持久性与临床获益之间潜在联系的生物学见解，以及系统性免疫景观（特别是髓系细胞和T细胞亚群）作为预后生物标志物的证据。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅适用于ICI耐药/进展人群，不能外推至一线治疗场景。样本量极小，结果可能存在偶然性，但趋势明确。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了FMT在广泛ICI耐药实体瘤队列中的真实世界疗效数据：ORR为0%，中位PFS仅为1.5个月。这表明在非选择性、快速进展的耐药人群中，FMT联合ICI并非有效的挽救疗法。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持综述中关于“FMT疗效具有高度情境依赖性”或“需严格筛选患者群体”的论点。对比其他阳性研究，说明患者基线状态（如疾病负荷、进展速度）对FMT结局的关键影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"免疫关联分析为探索性，未校正多重检验，且缺乏对照组，无法证明FMT改变了这些免疫特征从而改善了预后。仅描述了相关性，未证明因果机制。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"揭示了FMT干预后系统性免疫景观的变化及其与预后的关联：(1) FMT诱导的微生物定植多为暂时性，仅持久定植者与临床获益相关；(2) 基线高分化T细胞表型预示更好预后，而高髓系细胞（CD14+）和高IL-8水平预示不良预后；(3) 这些免疫特征似乎反映了宿主固有的免疫反应能力，而非FMT直接重塑的结果。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释FMT起效的潜在机制（需要持久定植和特定的宿主免疫背景）。提供生物标志物候选项（如CD14+单核细胞、IL-8、T细胞分化状态）以预测哪些患者可能从微生物调节中受益。帮助区分“药物直接效应”与“宿主背景效应”。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供FMT在临床转化中的实际挑战案例，特别是供体选择和患者异质性问题。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究讨论了使用ICI响应者作为供体的局限性（微生物组成差异大），以及不同研究间供体数量（单供体vs多供体）对结果的潜在影响。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论“供体选择标准”对FMT疗效的影响，指出单一供体或多供体混合策略的优劣尚无共识，且供体与受体的匹配度可能是关键因素。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于FMT给药途径和频率的可行性数据。\",\n            \"material\": \"比较了结肠镜给药（前两次）和灌肠给药（后续）的可行性及耐受性。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述中关于“FMT最佳给药方案”的部分，说明多次、重复给药在技术上可行，但在临床实践中受限于患者状况和疾病进展。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究的阴性结果主要归因于患者群体的快速进展特性和小样本量，不应将其解读为FMT在所有情况下均无效，而应视为特定情境下的失败尝试。\",\n          \"免疫生物标志物的发现是探索性的，且可能与ICI治疗本身的效应混淆，需谨慎解读其作为FMT特异性机制的作用。\",\n          \"微生物定植的暂时性并不必然意味着临床无效，因为唯一获益患者的持久定植提示了两者间的潜在联系，但这仅是个案观察。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) resistance\",\n          \"solid tumors (melanoma, HNSCC, RCC, pancreatic cancer)\",\n          \"microbiota engraftment kinetics\",\n          \"peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs)\",\n          \"myeloid-derived suppressor cells / CD14+ monocytes\",\n          \"T-cell differentiation phenotypes\",\n          \"serum cytokines (IL-8)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material\": {\n        \"_progressive_task_requirements\": {\n          \"knowledge_gaps\": [\n            \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\"\n          ],\n          \"questions\": [\n            {\n              \"gap_key\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n              \"purpose\": \"Supply a concrete, attributed case for the coordinated review.\",\n              \"question\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n              \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n              \"required_output_ids\": [\n                \"O01\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ],\n          \"required_outputs\": [\n            {\n              \"description\": \"Source-based findings, conditions, methods and limits useful to the review.\",\n              \"output_id\": \"O01\",\n              \"output_type\": \"practical_material\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        \"_progressive_task_signature\": \"1826ef4c29abfe31\",\n        \"current_question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究 (MITRIC trial)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为12名接受过多种治疗的晚期实体瘤患者（9例黑色素瘤，1例HNSCC，1例ccRCC，1例MSI-H胰腺癌），均处于ICI治疗后的疾病进展期。供体为3名长期ICI反应者。干预措施包括结肠镜给药的初始FMT和灌肠后续FMT。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在12名ICI耐药实体瘤患者中，FMT联合ICI治疗未观察到客观缓解（ORR 0%）。尽管FMT安全且可行，但临床获益有限。机制分析显示，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差预后相关；治疗后CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关，表明FMT未能逆转这种不利的免疫微环境，甚至可能伴随髓系驱动的免疫抑制增加。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供阴性结果的详细解释，特别是关于患者选择（快速进展、晚期）、供体匹配（多供体 vs 单供体）及免疫微环境限制（髓系主导、IL-8升高），用于平衡Ch5中的阳性结果。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Baruch et al. (2021)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。所有反应者均接受来自单一供体的粪便。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的概念验证试验中，使用粪便微生物移植（FMT）实现了3/10患者的客观缓解，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R19\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中使用的单一供体策略和特定患者群体，以突显MITRIC试验中多供体和不同患者选择带来的差异。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Davar et al. (2021)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的试验中，FMT使3/15患者产生反应，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。响应者在FMT/抗PD-1治疗后表现出外周血中CD56+CD8+ T细胞水平升高，且这些细胞表达更高的激活标记物；同时，响应者体内循环IL-8降低，肿瘤内产生IL-8的髓系细胞频率减少。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R18\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中观察到的免疫学变化（如CD56+CD8+ T细胞增加、IL-8降低），与MITRIC试验中的阴性结果形成机制上的对比。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Kim et al. (2024)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者，主要为上消化道癌症。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者中，FMT改善了1/13名上消化道癌症患者的抗PD-1抑制剂疗效。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R20\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示在其他实体瘤（上消化道癌）中FMT的潜在疗效，说明癌症类型和疾病状态对结果的影响。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"MITRIC试验的阴性结果提示，FMT在克服ICI耐药方面并非普遍有效，其效果受到患者选择、供体匹配和免疫微环境状态的严格限制。\\n\\n首先，患者选择是关键因素。与之前报告阳性结果的试验（如Baruch等和Davar等）相比，MITRIC试验中的患者大多患有扩散迅速、生长快速的疾病，这可能没有给予FMT足够的时间来发挥有意义的生物学效应。此外，MITRIC试验纳入了原发性和继发性ICI耐药患者，而之前的研究主要关注继发性耐药或特定类型的耐药，这可能导致了结果的异质性。\\n\\n其次，供体匹配和多样性可能影响疗效。MITRIC试验使用了三名不同的供体，而Baruch等人的研究中所有反应者均来自单一供体。这表明，使用多名供体可能导致供体-受体匹配次优，或者缺乏对特定获益至关重要的微生物群落。供体的微生物组成存在显著差异，且目前尚不清楚使用ICI反应者作为供体是否优于健康供体。\\n\\n最后，免疫微环境的限制是重要的机制解释。MITRIC试验发现，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差的预后相关。治疗后，CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关。相比之下，Davar等人的研究发现响应者体内IL-8降低，且髓系细胞产生的IL-8减少。这表明，在MITRIC试验的患者群体中，FMT未能逆转由髓系驱动的免疫抑制微环境，甚至可能伴随着这种抑制的增加，从而限制了临床获益。因此，FMT可能仅在具有特定免疫特征（如高分化T细胞环境、低髓系浸润）的选定人群中有效，而可靠的生物标志物尚未建立。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"MITRIC试验样本量小，且为单臂研究，无法进行因果推断或多重检验校正，因此关于免疫微环境和生物标志物的发现应谨慎解读。\",\n              \"长期定植的效果难以评估，因为大多数患者在疾病进展后无法收集后续的粪便样本。\",\n              \"需要进一步的研究来确定FMT的最佳给药方案、供体选择标准以及能够预测获益的患者亚群。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"fulfilled\": false,\n        \"paper_identity\": {\n          \"canonical_paper_id\": \"d9577b3a29bd8aec60c9e27c6a56a63faf3f4a27\",\n          \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2026-015122\",\n          \"title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\"\n        },\n        \"paper_title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\",\n        \"plain_text\": \"\",\n        \"prior_question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为 ICI 治疗期间或治疗后进展的晚期实体瘤患者；供体为长期 ICI 响应者；干预措施为结肠镜和灌肠给予的重复 FMT 联合持续或重新引入的 ICI 治疗；评估标准采用 iRECIST。\",\n                \"finding\": \"MITRIC 试验为一项单臂 IIa 期篮子试验，纳入 12 名对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药的晚期实体瘤患者（黑色素瘤 n=9, ccRCC n=1, HNSCC n=1, MSI-H 胰腺癌 n=1）。主要终点客观缓解率（ORR）为 0/12（0%），未观察到任何客观缓解（部分或完全缓解）。5 名患者达到最佳稳定疾病（SD），7 名患者进展（PD）。中位无进展生存期（PFS）为 1.5 个月，中位总生存期（OS）为 10.1 个月。仅 1 名黑色素瘤患者获得协议定义的临床获益（SD >6 个月），另 2 名患者出现混合反应（部分病灶消退）。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供 FMT 在 ICI 耐药/难治性实体瘤患者中的具体疗效数据（ORR, PFS, OS），并展示阴性结果的具体表现，作为该人群疗效局限性的直接证据。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"作者对 MITRIC 试验结果为阴性的解释主要集中在患者选择、疾病特征及供体匹配等方面：\\n1. **患者选择与疾病进展速度**：既往研究（如 Baruch et al., Davar et al.）中的响应往往发生较晚，且有时 preceded by pseudo-progression（假性进展）。而 MITRIC 中的大多数患者表现为弥漫性、快速生长的疾病，可能没有足够的时间让 FMT 发挥有意义的生物学效应。\\n2. **供体匹配与异质性**：虽然既往研究中所有响应者均接受单一供体的粪便，而本试验使用了三名供体，可能存在次优的供体-受体匹配或缺乏产生获益所需的特定微生物群落。此外，ICI 响应者之间的微生物组成差异很大，使用健康供体是否更优尚不清楚。\\n3. **免疫微环境限制**：基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚 T 细胞表型与较差预后相关，且 FMT 未能逆转这种不利的髓系主导免疫环境（事实上 CD14+ 单核细胞和 IL-8 随时间增加，这与较短的 PFS 和 OS 相关）。这表明 FMT 可能仅在选定的具有特定免疫特征的人群中有效。\\n4. **样本量与异质性**：小样本量是重要局限性，且不同研究在供体选择、FMT 制备、给药途径和治疗方案上的异质性也可能影响结果。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"FMT 在 ICI 耐药人群中的最佳生物标志物筛选标准尚未确立。\",\n              \"供体选择（ICI 响应者 vs 健康供体）及个体化匹配策略对疗效的具体影响仍需进一步研究。\",\n              \"FMT 是否能通过改变外周血免疫表型（如减少髓系抑制）来增强疗效，目前数据不支持其诱导了显著的全身免疫改变。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"question_material\": [\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为 ICI 治疗期间或治疗后进展的晚期实体瘤患者；供体为长期 ICI 响应者；干预措施为结肠镜和灌肠给予的重复 FMT 联合持续或重新引入的 ICI 治疗；评估标准采用 iRECIST。\",\n                \"finding\": \"MITRIC 试验为一项单臂 IIa 期篮子试验，纳入 12 名对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药的晚期实体瘤患者（黑色素瘤 n=9, ccRCC n=1, HNSCC n=1, MSI-H 胰腺癌 n=1）。主要终点客观缓解率（ORR）为 0/12（0%），未观察到任何客观缓解（部分或完全缓解）。5 名患者达到最佳稳定疾病（SD），7 名患者进展（PD）。中位无进展生存期（PFS）为 1.5 个月，中位总生存期（OS）为 10.1 个月。仅 1 名黑色素瘤患者获得协议定义的临床获益（SD >6 个月），另 2 名患者出现混合反应（部分病灶消退）。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供 FMT 在 ICI 耐药/难治性实体瘤患者中的具体疗效数据（ORR, PFS, OS），并展示阴性结果的具体表现，作为该人群疗效局限性的直接证据。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"作者对 MITRIC 试验结果为阴性的解释主要集中在患者选择、疾病特征及供体匹配等方面：\\n1. **患者选择与疾病进展速度**：既往研究（如 Baruch et al., Davar et al.）中的响应往往发生较晚，且有时 preceded by pseudo-progression（假性进展）。而 MITRIC 中的大多数患者表现为弥漫性、快速生长的疾病，可能没有足够的时间让 FMT 发挥有意义的生物学效应。\\n2. **供体匹配与异质性**：虽然既往研究中所有响应者均接受单一供体的粪便，而本试验使用了三名供体，可能存在次优的供体-受体匹配或缺乏产生获益所需的特定微生物群落。此外，ICI 响应者之间的微生物组成差异很大，使用健康供体是否更优尚不清楚。\\n3. **免疫微环境限制**：基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚 T 细胞表型与较差预后相关，且 FMT 未能逆转这种不利的髓系主导免疫环境（事实上 CD14+ 单核细胞和 IL-8 随时间增加，这与较短的 PFS 和 OS 相关）。这表明 FMT 可能仅在选定的具有特定免疫特征的人群中有效。\\n4. **样本量与异质性**：小样本量是重要局限性，且不同研究在供体选择、FMT 制备、给药途径和治疗方案上的异质性也可能影响结果。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"FMT 在 ICI 耐药人群中的最佳生物标志物筛选标准尚未确立。\",\n              \"供体选择（ICI 响应者 vs 健康供体）及个体化匹配策略对疗效的具体影响仍需进一步研究。\",\n              \"FMT 是否能通过改变外周血免疫表型（如减少髓系抑制）来增强疗效，目前数据不支持其诱导了显著的全身免疫改变。\"\n            ]\n          },\n          {\n            \"examples\": [\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"本研究 (MITRIC trial)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为12名接受过多种治疗的晚期实体瘤患者（9例黑色素瘤，1例HNSCC，1例ccRCC，1例MSI-H胰腺癌），均处于ICI治疗后的疾病进展期。供体为3名长期ICI反应者。干预措施包括结肠镜给药的初始FMT和灌肠后续FMT。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在12名ICI耐药实体瘤患者中，FMT联合ICI治疗未观察到客观缓解（ORR 0%）。尽管FMT安全且可行，但临床获益有限。机制分析显示，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差预后相关；治疗后CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关，表明FMT未能逆转这种不利的免疫微环境，甚至可能伴随髓系驱动的免疫抑制增加。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"提供阴性结果的详细解释，特别是关于患者选择（快速进展、晚期）、供体匹配（多供体 vs 单供体）及免疫微环境限制（髓系主导、IL-8升高），用于平衡Ch5中的阳性结果。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Baruch et al. (2021)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。所有反应者均接受来自单一供体的粪便。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的概念验证试验中，使用粪便微生物移植（FMT）实现了3/10患者的客观缓解，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R19\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中使用的单一供体策略和特定患者群体，以突显MITRIC试验中多供体和不同患者选择带来的差异。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Davar et al. (2021)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的试验中，FMT使3/15患者产生反应，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。响应者在FMT/抗PD-1治疗后表现出外周血中CD56+CD8+ T细胞水平升高，且这些细胞表达更高的激活标记物；同时，响应者体内循环IL-8降低，肿瘤内产生IL-8的髓系细胞频率减少。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R18\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中观察到的免疫学变化（如CD56+CD8+ T细胞增加、IL-8降低），与MITRIC试验中的阴性结果形成机制上的对比。\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"attribution\": \"Kim et al. (2024)\",\n                \"conditions\": \"研究对象为抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者，主要为上消化道癌症。\",\n                \"finding\": \"在抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者中，FMT改善了1/13名上消化道癌症患者的抗PD-1抑制剂疗效。\",\n                \"reference_ids\": [\n                  \"R20\"\n                ],\n                \"use_in_review\": \"作为对比背景，展示在其他实体瘤（上消化道癌）中FMT的潜在疗效，说明癌症类型和疾病状态对结果的影响。\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"explanation\": \"MITRIC试验的阴性结果提示，FMT在克服ICI耐药方面并非普遍有效，其效果受到患者选择、供体匹配和免疫微环境状态的严格限制。\\n\\n首先，患者选择是关键因素。与之前报告阳性结果的试验（如Baruch等和Davar等）相比，MITRIC试验中的患者大多患有扩散迅速、生长快速的疾病，这可能没有给予FMT足够的时间来发挥有意义的生物学效应。此外，MITRIC试验纳入了原发性和继发性ICI耐药患者，而之前的研究主要关注继发性耐药或特定类型的耐药，这可能导致了结果的异质性。\\n\\n其次，供体匹配和多样性可能影响疗效。MITRIC试验使用了三名不同的供体，而Baruch等人的研究中所有反应者均来自单一供体。这表明，使用多名供体可能导致供体-受体匹配次优，或者缺乏对特定获益至关重要的微生物群落。供体的微生物组成存在显著差异，且目前尚不清楚使用ICI反应者作为供体是否优于健康供体。\\n\\n最后，免疫微环境的限制是重要的机制解释。MITRIC试验发现，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差的预后相关。治疗后，CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关。相比之下，Davar等人的研究发现响应者体内IL-8降低，且髓系细胞产生的IL-8减少。这表明，在MITRIC试验的患者群体中，FMT未能逆转由髓系驱动的免疫抑制微环境，甚至可能伴随着这种抑制的增加，从而限制了临床获益。因此，FMT可能仅在具有特定免疫特征（如高分化T细胞环境、低髓系浸润）的选定人群中有效，而可靠的生物标志物尚未建立。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"remaining_points\": [\n              \"MITRIC试验样本量小，且为单臂研究，无法进行因果推断或多重检验校正，因此关于免疫微环境和生物标志物的发现应谨慎解读。\",\n              \"长期定植的效果难以评估，因为大多数患者在疾病进展后无法收集后续的粪便样本。\",\n              \"需要进一步的研究来确定FMT的最佳给药方案、供体选择标准以及能够预测获益的患者亚群。\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"questions\": [\n          {\n            \"gap_key\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Supply a concrete, attributed case for the coordinated review.\",\n            \"question\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n            \"question_id\": \"Q01\",\n            \"required_output_ids\": [\n              \"O01\"\n            ]\n          }\n        ],\n        \"reading_mode\": \"focused_local_search\",\n        \"required_outputs\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Source-based findings, conditions, methods and limits useful to the review.\",\n            \"output_id\": \"O01\",\n            \"output_type\": \"practical_material\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"source_hash\": \"163af5f925ccdf303a5e1ea759ee09e197370c5f96ebb2b13c0ffe191f601130\",\n        \"task_hash\": \"d5ed1ee74fa77146cbbbb8902e443d73f4d0b6bf5e2f2fa86fc5fd776eb04965\"\n      },\n      \"deep_read_material_trimmed\": {\n        \"dropped_keys\": {\n          \"material_ready\": 4,\n          \"paper_id\": 42,\n          \"references\": 834,\n          \"review_id\": 23,\n          \"schema_version\": 46,\n          \"status\": 9,\n          \"task_id\": 34,\n          \"workflow\": 21\n        },\n        \"dropped_chars\": 1013,\n        \"note\": \"深读材料里的记账字段与论文自带的参考文献表已省略；科学内容（问句材料/发现/条件）保留。需要时可用 locator 读原文。\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study provides mechanistic insights into why efficacy may be limited in certain contexts. It found that microbiota engraftment from donors was transient in most recipients, with durable engraftment observed only in the single patient who achieved clinical benefit. Mass cytometry analysis revealed that a baseline myeloid-dominant environment (high CD14+ monocytes) and high serum IL-8 were associated with poorer outcomes, while an activated T-cell signature was favorable. These immune features appeared to reflect the host's underlying disease state rather than being directly modified by FMT to improve outcomes. Additionally, the review planning material notes that this study highlights challenges in donor selection (using ICI responders vs healthy donors) and patient heterogeneity (rapidly progressive disease vs stable disease), suggesting that FMT efficacy is highly context-dependent.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"large_scale_rct_results\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_fc8af41423\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Identify and retrieve full-text or registry data for actual Phase III randomized controlled trials of FMT or defined microbial consortia in oncology (e.g., TACITO, though noted as randomized, verify if Phase III; other ongoing/completed Phase III trials like those in melanoma or RCC). If no such trials are found in the literature base, explicitly state that no Phase III RCTs have yet confirmed efficacy, using this Phase IIa negative result to illustrate the current stage of evidence (pre-registration/early-phase focus).\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap question asks for confirmation of completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials confirming microbiome intervention efficacy. This study is explicitly identified as a 'single-arm phase IIa' trial. It is neither Phase III nor randomized. Therefore, it does not fulfill the success criterion of providing data from a completed Phase III RCT.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of the MITRIC trial (NCT05286294), a single-arm, phase IIa basket trial evaluating Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) from immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) responders in patients with advanced cancers progressing on ICIs. The trial enrolled 12 patients across melanoma, head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and microsatellite instability-high pancreatic cancer. The co-primary endpoints were safety and objective response rate (ORR). The study found that FMT was safe and feasible but demonstrated limited clinical activity: no objective responses were observed (ORR 0%), median progression-free survival was 1.5 months, and median overall survival was 10.1 months. Clinical benefit (stable disease >6 months) was achieved in only one patient. The authors conclude that FMT does not support efficacy as a salvage therapy in this non-biomarker-driven, heavily pretreated population.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study provides mechanistic insights into why efficacy may be limited in certain contexts. It found that microbiota engraftment from donors was transient in most recipients, with durable engraftment observed only in the single patient who achieved clinical benefit. Mass cytometry analysis revealed that a baseline myeloid-dominant environment (high CD14+ monocytes) and high serum IL-8 were associated with poorer outcomes, while an activated T-cell signature was favorable. These immune features appeared to reflect the host's underlying disease state rather than being directly modified by FMT to improve outcomes. Additionally, the review planning material notes that this study highlights challenges in donor selection (using ICI responders vs healthy donors) and patient heterogeneity (rapidly progressive disease vs stable disease), suggesting that FMT efficacy is highly context-dependent.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"large_scale_rct_results\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_fc8af41423\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Identify and retrieve full-text or registry data for actual Phase III randomized controlled trials of FMT or defined microbial consortia in oncology (e.g., TACITO, though noted as randomized, verify if Phase III; other ongoing/completed Phase III trials like those in melanoma or RCC). If no such trials are found in the literature base, explicitly state that no Phase III RCTs have yet confirmed efficacy, using this Phase IIa negative result to illustrate the current stage of evidence (pre-registration/early-phase focus).\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The gap question asks for confirmation of completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials confirming microbiome intervention efficacy. This study is explicitly identified as a 'single-arm phase IIa' trial. It is neither Phase III nor randomized. Therefore, it does not fulfill the success criterion of providing data from a completed Phase III RCT.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the results of the MITRIC trial (NCT05286294), a single-arm, phase IIa basket trial evaluating Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) from immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) responders in patients with advanced cancers progressing on ICIs. The trial enrolled 12 patients across melanoma, head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and microsatellite instability-high pancreatic cancer. The co-primary endpoints were safety and objective response rate (ORR). The study found that FMT was safe and feasible but demonstrated limited clinical activity: no objective responses were observed (ORR 0%), median progression-free survival was 1.5 months, and median overall survival was 10.1 months. Clinical benefit (stable disease >6 months) was achieved in only one patient. The authors conclude that FMT does not support efficacy as a salvage therapy in this non-biomarker-driven, heavily pretreated population.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"tool_supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"allowed_use\": [],\n          \"conditions\": [],\n          \"decision\": \"stop\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"\",\n          \"limits\": [],\n          \"need_id\": \"N55287a996d28-7390b583b8224c16\",\n          \"question\": \"MITRIC 试验中 FMT 在 ICI 耐药患者中的 ORR 及 PFS 数据是多少？为何结果为阴性？\",\n          \"sources\": [\n            {\n              \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2026-015122\",\n              \"paper_id\": \"d9577b3a29bd8aec60c9e27c6a56a63faf3f4a27\",\n              \"source_handle\": \"P0578\",\n              \"title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\",\n              \"year\": \"2026\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"still_missing\": \"MITRIC 试验中 FMT 在 ICI 耐药患者中的 ORR 及 PFS 数据是多少？为何结果为阴性？\",\n          \"usable_content\": \"{\\\"question_material\\\": [{\\\"question_id\\\": \\\"Q01\\\", \\\"examples\\\": [{\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"提供 FMT 在 ICI 耐药/难治性实体瘤患者中的具体疗效数据（ORR, PFS, OS），并展示阴性结果的具体表现，作为该人群疗效局限性的直接证据。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"MITRIC 试验为一项单臂 IIa 期篮子试验，纳入 12 名对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药的晚期实体瘤患者（黑色素瘤 n=9, ccRCC n=1, HNSCC n=1, MSI-H 胰腺癌 n=1）。主要终点客观缓解率（ORR）为 0/12（0%），未观察到任何客观缓解（部分或完全缓解）。5 名患者达到最佳稳定疾病（SD），7 名患者进展（PD）。中位无进展生存期（PFS）为 1.5 个月，中位总生存期（OS）为 10.1 个月。仅 1 名黑色素瘤患者获得协议定义的临床获益（SD >6 个月），另 2 名患者出现混合反应（部分病灶消退）。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为 ICI 治疗期间或治疗后进展的晚期实体瘤患者；供体为长期 ICI 响应者；干预措施为结肠镜和灌肠给予的重复 FMT 联合持续或重新引入的 ICI 治疗；评估标准采用 iRECIST。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": []}], \\\"explanation\\\": \\\"作者对 MITRIC 试验结果为阴性的解释主要集中在患者选择、疾病特征及供体匹配等方面：\\\\n1. **患者选择与疾病进展速度**：既往研究（如 Baruch et al., Davar et al.）中的响应往往发生较晚，且有时 preceded by pseudo-progression（假性进展）。而 MITRIC 中的大多数患者表现为弥漫性、快速生长的疾病，可能没有足够的时间让 FMT 发挥有意义的生物学效应。\\\\n2. **供体匹配与异质性**：虽然既往研究中所有响应者均接受单一供体的粪便，而本试验使用了三名供体，可能存在次优的供体-受体匹配或缺乏产生获益所需的特定微生物群落。此外，ICI 响应者之间的微生物组成差异很大，使用健康供体是否更优尚不清楚。\\\\n3. **免疫微环境限制**：基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚 T 细胞表型与较差预后相关，且 FMT 未能逆转这种不利的髓系主导免疫环境（事实上 CD14+ 单核细胞和 IL-8 随时间增加，这与较短的 PFS 和 OS 相关）。这表明 FMT 可能仅在选定的具有特定免疫特征的人群中有效。\\\\n4. **样本量与异质性**：小样本量是重要局限性，且不同研究在供体选择、FMT 制备、给药途径和治疗方案上的异质性也可能影响结果。\\\", \\\"remaining_points\\\": [\\\"FMT 在 ICI 耐药人群中的最佳生物标志物筛选标准尚未确立。\\\", \\\"供体选择（ICI 响应者 vs 健康供体）及个体化匹配策略对疗效的具体影响仍需进一步研究。\\\", \\\"FMT 是否能通过改变外周血免疫表型（如减少髓系抑制）来增强疗效，目前数据不支持其诱导了显著的全身免疫改变。\\\"]}], \\\"plain_text\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"allowed_use\": [],\n          \"conditions\": [],\n          \"decision\": \"stop\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"\",\n          \"limits\": [],\n          \"need_id\": \"Nf74b4d86ffb7-7390b583b8224c16\",\n          \"question\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n          \"sources\": [\n            {\n              \"doi\": \"10.1136/jitc-2026-015122\",\n              \"paper_id\": \"d9577b3a29bd8aec60c9e27c6a56a63faf3f4a27\",\n              \"source_handle\": \"P0578\",\n              \"title\": \"Safety and efficacy of fecal microbiota transplantation in solid cancers resistant to immune checkpoint inhibitors: results of the MITRIC trial\",\n              \"year\": \"2026\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"still_missing\": \"MITRIC 试验阴性结果需详细提取其关于患者选择、供体匹配及免疫微环境限制的解释，以平衡 Ch5 中的阳性结果。\",\n          \"usable_content\": \"{\\\"question_material\\\": [{\\\"question_id\\\": \\\"Q01\\\", \\\"examples\\\": [{\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"本研究 (MITRIC trial)\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"提供阴性结果的详细解释，特别是关于患者选择（快速进展、晚期）、供体匹配（多供体 vs 单供体）及免疫微环境限制（髓系主导、IL-8升高），用于平衡Ch5中的阳性结果。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在12名ICI耐药实体瘤患者中，FMT联合ICI治疗未观察到客观缓解（ORR 0%）。尽管FMT安全且可行，但临床获益有限。机制分析显示，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差预后相关；治疗后CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关，表明FMT未能逆转这种不利的免疫微环境，甚至可能伴随髓系驱动的免疫抑制增加。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为12名接受过多种治疗的晚期实体瘤患者（9例黑色素瘤，1例HNSCC，1例ccRCC，1例MSI-H胰腺癌），均处于ICI治疗后的疾病进展期。供体为3名长期ICI反应者。干预措施包括结肠镜给药的初始FMT和灌肠后续FMT。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": []}, {\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"Baruch et al. (2021)\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中使用的单一供体策略和特定患者群体，以突显MITRIC试验中多供体和不同患者选择带来的差异。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的概念验证试验中，使用粪便微生物移植（FMT）实现了3/10患者的客观缓解，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。所有反应者均接受来自单一供体的粪便。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": [\\\"R19\\\"]}, {\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"Davar et al. (2021)\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"作为对比背景，展示阳性结果中观察到的免疫学变化（如CD56+CD8+ T细胞增加、IL-8降低），与MITRIC试验中的阴性结果形成机制上的对比。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在一项针对抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者的试验中，FMT使3/15患者产生反应，另有3名患者获得持久的疾病稳定。响应者在FMT/抗PD-1治疗后表现出外周血中CD56+CD8+ T细胞水平升高，且这些细胞表达更高的激活标记物；同时，响应者体内循环IL-8降低，肿瘤内产生IL-8的髓系细胞频率减少。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为抗PD-1耐受的转移性黑色素瘤患者。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": [\\\"R18\\\"]}, {\\\"attribution\\\": \\\"Kim et al. (2024)\\\", \\\"use_in_review\\\": \\\"作为对比背景，展示在其他实体瘤（上消化道癌）中FMT的潜在疗效，说明癌症类型和疾病状态对结果的影响。\\\", \\\"finding\\\": \\\"在抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者中，FMT改善了1/13名上消化道癌症患者的抗PD-1抑制剂疗效。\\\", \\\"conditions\\\": \\\"研究对象为抗PD-1抑制剂耐药的不可切除或转移性实体瘤患者，主要为上消化道癌症。\\\", \\\"reference_ids\\\": [\\\"R20\\\"]}], \\\"explanation\\\": \\\"MITRIC试验的阴性结果提示，FMT在克服ICI耐药方面并非普遍有效，其效果受到患者选择、供体匹配和免疫微环境状态的严格限制。\\\\n\\\\n首先，患者选择是关键因素。与之前报告阳性结果的试验（如Baruch等和Davar等）相比，MITRIC试验中的患者大多患有扩散迅速、生长快速的疾病，这可能没有给予FMT足够的时间来发挥有意义的生物学效应。此外，MITRIC试验纳入了原发性和继发性ICI耐药患者，而之前的研究主要关注继发性耐药或特定类型的耐药，这可能导致了结果的异质性。\\\\n\\\\n其次，供体匹配和多样性可能影响疗效。MITRIC试验使用了三名不同的供体，而Baruch等人的研究中所有反应者均来自单一供体。这表明，使用多名供体可能导致供体-受体匹配次优，或者缺乏对特定获益至关重要的微生物群落。供体的微生物组成存在显著差异，且目前尚不清楚使用ICI反应者作为供体是否优于健康供体。\\\\n\\\\n最后，免疫微环境的限制是重要的机制解释。MITRIC试验发现，基线时髓系偏倚环境和幼稚T细胞表型与较差的预后相关。治疗后，CD14+单核细胞和血清IL-8水平升高，这与较短的PFS和OS相关。相比之下，Davar等人的研究发现响应者体内IL-8降低，且髓系细胞产生的IL-8减少。这表明，在MITRIC试验的患者群体中，FMT未能逆转由髓系驱动的免疫抑制微环境，甚至可能伴随着这种抑制的增加，从而限制了临床获益。因此，FMT可能仅在具有特定免疫特征（如高分化T细胞环境、低髓系浸润）的选定人群中有效，而可靠的生物标志物尚未建立。\\\", \\\"remaining_points\\\": [\\\"MITRIC试验样本量小，且为单臂研究，无法进行因果推断或多重检验校正，因此关于免疫微环境和生物标志物的发现应谨慎解读。\\\", \\\"长期定植的效果难以评估，因为大多数患者在疾病进展后无法收集后续的粪便样本。\\\", \\\"需要进一步的研究来确定FMT的最佳给药方案、供体选择标准以及能够预测获益的患者亚群。\\\"]}], \\\"plain_text\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0578\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_fc8af41423\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\progressive_review_plan\\\\20260924_microbiome\\\\run01\\\\level1\\\\supplements\\\\large_scale_rct_results\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_fc8af41423\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0212\",\n      \"title\": \"Microbiota boost immunotherapy? A meta-analysis dives into fecal microbiota transplantation and immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1186/s12916-025-04183-y\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"5af3aee4ca5f3778325d44c8432c8b71b95ca531\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用PRISMA指南指导的系统综述方法。检索策略覆盖PubMed、ClinicalTrials.gov及主要肿瘤学会议摘要。纳入标准限定为接受ICI联合FMT治疗的实体瘤患者，排除仅关注免疫微环境变化或无具体数据的回顾性研究。使用Cochrane ROB 2.0和ROBINS-I工具评估偏倚风险。统计分析使用Stata 18和R语言，以ORR为主要终点，CR、PR、PFS、OS及不良事件为次要终点。对于生存数据，因多数研究未报告风险比（HR），采用加权平均中位数进行初步评估。亚组分析基于ICI策略、供体来源、给药途径和肿瘤类型进行。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了FMT联合ICI治疗实体瘤的汇总疗效数据（ORR 43%），并量化了不同ICI策略（特别是双药联合）对疗效的显著影响。\",\n            \"limits\": \"所给材料未交代具体的微生物组测序深度、特定菌株定植率的长期追踪数据，以及FMT对ICI耐药性的逆转机制的直接实验证据。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"明确了FMT联合治疗的安全性特征，指出其不显著增加严重不良事件风险，甚至可能在某些情况下降低irAEs。\",\n            \"limits\": \"安全性数据主要来自短期随访，缺乏长期安全性监测，无法评估迟发性不良反应或微生物组改变的持久性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"识别了关键的方法学异质性来源，如供体选择（ICI反应者vs健康人）和给药途径对结果的潜在影响。\",\n            \"limits\": \"纳入研究多为单臂或小样本RCT，缺乏大规模多中心对照，导致效应估计可能存在选择偏倚，且无法确立因果关系。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"所有纳入的10项研究（n=164），混合ICI方案\",\n            \"finding\": \"总体客观缓解率（ORR）为43% (95% CI: 0.35–0.51)，存在中度异质性 (I² = 59%)。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"亚组：抗PD-1联合抗CTLA-4双药治疗 vs 抗PD-1单药\",\n            \"finding\": \"双药治疗ORR显著更高（60% vs 37%, P=0.01），且双药组内异质性低 (I² = 0)。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"亚组：供体来源为ICI反应者 vs 健康人或商业制剂\",\n            \"finding\": \"ICI反应者供体的ORR较高（46%），但组间差异无统计学意义；该亚组内存在显著异质性 (I² = 66%)。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"亚组：口服粪菌胶囊 vs 内镜移植\",\n            \"finding\": \"口服胶囊ORR较高（49% vs 34%），但组间差异无统计学意义；该亚组内存在显著异质性 (I² = 68%)。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"安全性评估：所有研究\",\n            \"finding\": \"1-2级不良事件发生率42% (95% CI: 0.32–0.52)，3-4级为37% (95% CI: 0.28–0.46)。双药治疗组的3-4级AE发生率高于单药组。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"生存分析：部分研究提供的中位数据\",\n            \"finding\": \"加权平均中位无进展生存期（mPFS）为8.32个月，中位总生存期（mOS）为12.67个月。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"解决FMT在增强ICI疗效方面的临床证据碎片化问题，明确其有效性、安全性以及不同治疗策略（如ICI组合、供体来源、给药途径）对疗效的影响，为未来临床试验设计提供依据。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"针对晚期或难治性实体瘤患者，评估粪便微生物群移植（FMT）联合免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗的临床疗效与安全性。纳入的研究包括随机对照试验（RCT）、单臂干预研究和前瞻性队列研究，涵盖黑色素瘤、肾细胞癌（RCC）、微卫星稳定型结直肠癌（MSS-CRC）等多种肿瘤类型。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"这是一篇系统综述与荟萃分析，旨在综合现有证据以评估FMT作为增强ICI疗效辅助策略的临床价值。研究通过系统检索PubMed及临床试验注册库，筛选出10项涉及164名患者的研究进行定量合成。主要发现显示，FMT联合ICI的总体客观缓解率（ORR）为43%，其中抗PD-1联合抗CTLA-4双药治疗的ORR显著高于单药治疗（60% vs 37%）。安全性方面，1-2级不良事件发生率为42%，3-4级为37%。尽管结果呈现初步积极信号，但作者指出由于纳入研究多为早期小样本试验且缺乏长期随访数据，结论尚需大规模RCT进一步验证。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文为综述“肠道微生物群如何调节实体瘤中ICI疗效”提供了关键的临床关联证据。它证实了通过FMT重塑微生物群确实能提升ICI的客观缓解率，特别是在双药联合背景下。同时，它指出了当前临床转化的瓶颈：异质性高、缺乏标准化协议、以及机制不明。对于机制部分，它提供了理论假设框架（如增加有益菌、调节TME），但未提供直接的分子机制验证。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅反映临床终点（ORR/PFS/OS），未直接测量微生物群的具体组成变化与疗效之间的因果链条。异质性较高，不能代表所有患者群体。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了FMT联合ICI在实体瘤中的汇总临床疗效数据（ORR 43%），证实了微生物干预对ICI疗效的正向调节作用。特别指出抗PD-1/CTLA-4双药联合时疗效显著提升（ORR 60%），提示微生物群可能增强双重阻断的协同效应。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物群是否以及如何调节ICI疗效”的问题，提供临床层面的有效性证据。可用于支持“微生物群是ICI疗效的关键决定因素”这一论点，并展示不同治疗策略下的疗效差异。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些是作者基于已有文献提出的假设，并非本荟萃分析直接验证的机制。缺乏直接的分子生物学证据（如单细胞测序、代谢组学数据）来证实这些机制在FMT患者中的实际运作路径。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提出了FMT增强ICI疗效的潜在机制假说：(1) 增加有益菌（如Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium）；(2) 提高肠道微生物α多样性；(3) 调节肿瘤微环境（TME），促进效应T细胞浸润并抑制调节性T细胞。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导该效应的机制是什么”的问题，提供基于文献的理论解释框架。可作为后续深入探讨具体菌株功能或代谢产物作用的背景知识。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"亚组分析样本量小，异质性大，结果仅为趋势性观察，不能作为确定性的指导原则。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"揭示了供体来源（ICI反应者 vs 健康人）和给药途径（口服胶囊 vs 内镜）对疗效的潜在影响趋势，尽管统计上不显著，但提示了个性化FMT策略的重要性。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于讨论“哪些因素可能影响微生物群调节ICI疗效的效果”，为制定个性化治疗方案提供依据。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供FMT在ICI治疗中的安全性背景，特别是关于免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的发生率。\",\n            \"material\": \"1-2级AE发生率42%，3-4级为37%。双药治疗组3-4级AE更高。FMT可能具有减轻某些irAEs（如结肠炎）的潜力。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于评估微生物干预的安全边界，讨论其在联合治疗中的风险收益比。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供当前FMT临床研究的局限性，如缺乏长期随访、标准化协议缺失等。\",\n            \"material\": \"纳入研究多为早期小样本试验，缺乏大规模RCT，长期疗效和安全性未知。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于指出领域内的知识空白，强调未来研究需要标准化的微生物组管理和长期监测。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究为荟萃分析，其结论依赖于纳入的10项研究的质量和设计。大部分研究为单臂或小样本RCT，存在较高的选择偏倚风险，因此其报告的疗效提升不能完全归因于FMT，也可能受患者选择等因素影响。\",\n          \"亚组分析（如供体来源、给药途径）的结果虽显示趋势，但因样本量小和异质性高，不具备统计显著性，解读时应谨慎，避免过度推断。\",\n          \"机制部分仅为作者基于文献的推测，非本研究直接验证，引用时需明确区分观察性关联与因果机制。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)\",\n          \"objective response rate (ORR)\",\n          \"solid tumors\",\n          \"anti-PD-1 / anti-CTLA-4 combination therapy\",\n          \"gut microbiome modulation\",\n          \"adverse events (AEs) / immune-related adverse events (irAEs)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study provides detailed safety data (42% grade 1–2 adverse events, 37% grade 3–4 adverse events) and mechanistic hypotheses (e.g., increasing beneficial bacteria like Akkermansia muciniphila, modulating tumor microenvironment) which, while not validating the Phase III gap, offer context on why such trials are being pursued. It also notes heterogeneity in FMT protocols (donor sources, administration routes) as a barrier that large-scale trials need to standardize.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"phase_iii_rct_evidence\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU01_search_candidate_d1c4b32e8c\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Use this meta-analysis to substantiate the claim that current evidence is limited to early-phase (I/II) and small-scale RCTs. Cite the authors' conclusion regarding the necessity of large-scale Phase III trials to justify the gap. Do not present the 3 included RCTs as fulfilling the 'large-scale Phase III' criterion, as the study itself classifies them as limited and calls for further validation.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific evidence requested—completed large-scale randomized controlled Phase III clinical trials confirming efficacy—is not present in the literature covered by this study. The 164 patients across 10 studies do not constitute a large-scale Phase III dataset, and the authors explicitly state the need for such trials to validate efficacy and safety.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study is a systematic review and meta-analysis (search up to September 30, 2024) that included 10 studies involving 164 patients with solid tumors treated with FMT combined with ICIs. The authors report that the included studies comprised 5 phase I trials, 4 phase II–III trials, and 3 randomized controlled trials (RCTs), but note that most were early-phase clinical trials with small sample sizes. The pooled objective response rate (ORR) was 43%, with subgroup analysis showing higher ORR (60%) for anti-PD-1 combined with anti-CTLA-4 therapy compared to monotherapy (37%). Crucially, the authors conclude in both the Abstract and Discussion that 'larger-scale randomized controlled trials with long-term follow-up are required to confirm and optimize treatment protocols' and highlight the 'absence of randomized controlled trials' (referring to large-scale/definitive ones) as a limitation. This material directly addresses the gap question by confirming that while preliminary RCT data exists, completed large-scale Phase III evidence is currently lacking and identified as a necessary future step.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0212\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5af3aee4ca5f3778325d44c8432c8b71b95ca531\\\\8c1809a73adaf485fed884559e8e0215\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\5af3aee4ca5f3778325d44c8432c8b71b95ca531\\\\8c1809a73adaf485fed884559e8e0215\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0590\",\n      \"title\": \"Gut microbiome in colorectal cancer: recent advances and clinical implications\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3393/ac.2026.00010.0001\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"CorpusId:286044272\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性综述方法，按主题组织材料：1) 病理机制：分类介绍主要致病菌株的分子通路；2) 保护机制：阐述有益菌群及代谢物的稳态维持功能；3) 诊断应用：汇总近期宏基因组学筛查研究的性能指标（AUC等）及局限性分析；4) 治疗干预：对比不同微生物调控策略（饮食、益生菌、FMT、工程菌、抗生素）的临床前与临床证据。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了结直肠癌相关微生物致病机制的系统性分类框架，明确了病原体与宿主免疫/代谢通路的交互细节。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，其结论依赖于所引用的原始研究，未提供新的实验数据；对临床转化的建议基于现有证据等级的综合判断，而非独立验证。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"指出了微生物标志物研究中混杂因素（如BMI、粪便特征）的重要性，强调了定量分析和去混杂的必要性。\",\n            \"limits\": \"未提供具体的去混杂统计模型代码或通用阈值，仅引用了相关研究案例。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"区分了不同微生物治疗策略的证据层级，明确将FMT和工程菌定位为实验性干预，纠正了过度乐观的临床预期。\",\n            \"limits\": \"对于特定菌株在人类患者中的长期定植效果和安全性数据，材料指出尚不充分。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"针对具核梭杆菌 (Fn)、pks+大肠杆菌、ETBF等主要病原体\",\n            \"finding\": \"这些菌株通过激活Wnt/β-catenin、NF-κB等信号通路，诱导DNA损伤、慢性炎症及免疫抑制，直接促进肿瘤发生和进展。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"针对基于粪便宏基因组学的CRC筛查模型\",\n            \"finding\": \"多队列荟萃分析显示宏基因组面板具有高诊断效能（AUC 0.85-0.99），但部分关联信号受肠道转运时间、炎症状态等宿主因素混杂，去混杂后某些标志物（如Fn）的关联性减弱。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"针对FMT及其他微生物疗法\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT在黑色素瘤等实体瘤中改善免疫检查点抑制剂疗效有初步证据，但在CRC中仅限小规模无对照试验，存在安全性和生态稳定性风险；益生菌和工程菌主要处于临床前阶段；抗生素暴露与CRC风险增加相关，不建议作为预防或治疗手段。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"旨在澄清肠道微生物组在结直肠癌中从“旁观者”到“主动驱动者”的角色转变，评估当前基于微生物组的诊断 biomarkers 和治疗手段的临床证据水平、主要障碍及未来转化方向。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"结直肠癌（CRC）与肠道微生物组的关系，涵盖致癌机制、保护性菌群功能、基于微生物组的生物标志物开发及临床治疗策略。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇综述，系统梳理了肠道微生物组在结直肠癌发生发展中的主动驱动作用及其临床转化潜力。文章首先详细阐述了多种病原体（如具核梭杆菌、pks+大肠杆菌等）通过炎症、代谢紊乱、基因毒性和免疫逃逸促进肿瘤发生的分子机制，并介绍了有益菌群及其代谢产物（如短链脂肪酸）的保护作用。随后，文章评估了基于粪便宏基因组学的生物标志物研究进展，指出其在早期检测中的高准确性但面临标准化和混杂因素控制的挑战。最后，综述了饮食调节、益生菌、粪菌移植（FMT）及工程菌等治疗策略的证据等级，强调目前多数疗法仍处于实验或早期临床阶段，且抗生素使用可能增加风险。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物组如何影响实体瘤（特别是结直肠癌）免疫治疗响应提供了关键的背景机制和现状评估。它详细列出了可能增强或削弱免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效的微生物特征（如Fn富集导致耐药，特定益生菌增强疗效），并指出了FMT联合ICI治疗的潜在前景及当前证据局限。这对于回答微生物组调节ICI疗效的机制和现状至关重要。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"主要证据来自结直肠癌的体外/体内模型或小规模观察性研究；FMT在CRC中的有效性尚未在随机对照试验中证实；不同肿瘤类型（如黑色素瘤 vs CRC）的微生物效应可能存在差异，不可直接外推。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了微生物组调节免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效的具体实例和证据等级。明确指出Fn和Pa与抗PD-1治疗抵抗相关，而某些益生菌（如Bifidobacterium）在动物模型中增强ICI疗效。FMT在黑色素瘤中恢复ICI响应的临床证据被引用，但在CRC中仅为探索性试验。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持‘微生物组成分影响ICI疗效’这一观点，列举具体正负向影响的菌种，并说明当前临床转化的阶段（实验性）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制描述多为细胞或动物模型层面的因果推断；人类体内确切的因果链条仍需更多验证；不同菌株亚型（如Fn的不同clades）可能有不同效应。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"详细阐述了微生物影响免疫微环境的分子机制。包括：1) Fn通过Fap2结合TIGIT抑制T/NK细胞毒性；2) Pa通过TLR2/4激活MDSC抑制CD8+/CD4+ T细胞；3) ETBF诱导Th17/IL-17炎症反应；4) 有益菌代谢物（SCFAs）调节树突状细胞/T细胞反应。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释微生物组调节ICI疗效的具体生物学通路，特别是免疫抑制性微环境的形成机制（TIGIT, MDSC, Th17等）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供结直肠癌中微生物组与宿主互作的整体背景，区分致癌病原体与保护性菌群。\",\n            \"material\": \"概述了CRC相关病原体的致病途径（炎症、基因毒性、屏障破坏）及有益菌群的稳态维持作用。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为综述的背景部分，界定研究对象（CRC微生物组）的功能谱系，帮助读者理解为何特定微生物会影响癌症进程及治疗响应。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于微生物组检测技术现状和挑战的信息。\",\n            \"material\": \"讨论了宏基因组学筛查的性能、混杂因素（如粪便特征、BMI）的影响及标准化的必要性。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论在评估微生物组与免疫治疗关系时，需要控制的技术变量和混杂因素，强调定量分析和去混杂的重要性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"作者将FMT在CRC中的应用严格限定为‘实验性/临床试验阶段’，不得将其解读为已确立的标准治疗。\",\n          \"文中提到的抗生素与CRC风险增加的相关性是流行病学观察结果，不能直接推导为因果关系用于个体临床决策，且明确反对将抗生素作为CRC治疗策略。\",\n          \"不同研究中对微生物标志物的定义和检测方法差异巨大，综述中引用的AUC值仅在各自研究背景下有效，跨研究比较需谨慎。\",\n          \"机制描述多基于小鼠模型或细胞实验，人类体内的确切效力和剂量效应尚不明确。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Fusobacterium nucleatum (Fn) and immune evasion\",\n          \"Peptostreptococcus anaerobius (Pa) and anti-PD-1 resistance\",\n          \"Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) in oncology\",\n          \"Microbiome modulation of immune checkpoint inhibitors\",\n          \"Strain-level SNVs in CRC detection\",\n          \"Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) and immune homeostasis\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_material\": {\n        \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review details mechanistic pathways linking specific gut microbes to immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) response, which provides biological rationale for ongoing trials. For example, Fusobacterium nucleatum (Fn) is described as promoting immune evasion via Fap2-TIGIT binding and chemotherapy resistance, while Peptostreptococcus anaerobius (Pa) is linked to anti–PD-1 resistance via MDSC activation. Conversely, beneficial bacteria like Bifidobacterium are noted in preclinical models to enhance antitumor efficacy of PD-1 blockade. The authors also discuss confounding factors in microbiome research (e.g., stool transit time, BMI) that complicate biomarker and intervention standardization, relevant for designing future rigorous trials.\",\n        \"evidence\": [],\n        \"gap_id\": \"phase_iii_rct_evidence\",\n        \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_a86301e06d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n        \"limitations\": [],\n        \"material_ready\": true,\n        \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n        \"outline_action\": \"Use this material to substantiate the claim that Phase III evidence is currently lacking, citing the authors' summary of existing Phase 1/2 data and their explicit call for large-scale RCTs. Incorporate the mechanistic details (e.g., Fn-TIGIT, Pa-MDSC pathways) to explain the biological basis for why microbiome interventions are being pursued despite the lack of Phase III confirmation. Clearly distinguish between the melanoma Phase 1 findings and the CRC uncontrolled Phase 2 data as reported by the authors to avoid overgeneralizing efficacy claims.\",\n        \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific evidence requested—a completed large randomized controlled Phase III clinical trial confirming microbiome intervention efficacy in ICI-treated solid tumors—is not present in this material. The authors explicitly state that such well-controlled prospective studies are still needed and that current human evidence is limited to Phase 1 (melanoma) or uncontrolled Phase 2 (CRC) settings. Therefore, the gap regarding confirmed Phase III efficacy remains unresolved based on this text.\",\n        \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n        \"status\": \"unmet\",\n        \"useful_material\": \"This review explicitly addresses the current clinical evidence level for microbiome interventions in cancer immunotherapy. The authors report that for Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT), the most developed clinical evidence comes from Phase 1 studies in melanoma patients showing recovery of anti–PD-1 response in some cases. For colorectal cancer (CRC), the authors cite a Phase 2 study evaluating FMT with immunotherapy and targeted therapy that reported improvement signals but was designed without a control group, making effectiveness determination difficult. The authors conclude that large-scale prospective studies and well-designed randomized controlled trials are still needed before routine clinical use, and frame FMT in CRC as an investigational intervention rather than an established treatment.\"\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review details mechanistic pathways linking specific gut microbes to immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) response, which provides biological rationale for ongoing trials. For example, Fusobacterium nucleatum (Fn) is described as promoting immune evasion via Fap2-TIGIT binding and chemotherapy resistance, while Peptostreptococcus anaerobius (Pa) is linked to anti–PD-1 resistance via MDSC activation. Conversely, beneficial bacteria like Bifidobacterium are noted in preclinical models to enhance antitumor efficacy of PD-1 blockade. The authors also discuss confounding factors in microbiome research (e.g., stool transit time, BMI) that complicate biomarker and intervention standardization, relevant for designing future rigorous trials.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"phase_iii_rct_evidence\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_a86301e06d\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Use this material to substantiate the claim that Phase III evidence is currently lacking, citing the authors' summary of existing Phase 1/2 data and their explicit call for large-scale RCTs. Incorporate the mechanistic details (e.g., Fn-TIGIT, Pa-MDSC pathways) to explain the biological basis for why microbiome interventions are being pursued despite the lack of Phase III confirmation. Clearly distinguish between the melanoma Phase 1 findings and the CRC uncontrolled Phase 2 data as reported by the authors to avoid overgeneralizing efficacy claims.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific evidence requested—a completed large randomized controlled Phase III clinical trial confirming microbiome intervention efficacy in ICI-treated solid tumors—is not present in this material. The authors explicitly state that such well-controlled prospective studies are still needed and that current human evidence is limited to Phase 1 (melanoma) or uncontrolled Phase 2 (CRC) settings. Therefore, the gap regarding confirmed Phase III efficacy remains unresolved based on this text.\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This review explicitly addresses the current clinical evidence level for microbiome interventions in cancer immunotherapy. The authors report that for Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT), the most developed clinical evidence comes from Phase 1 studies in melanoma patients showing recovery of anti–PD-1 response in some cases. For colorectal cancer (CRC), the authors cite a Phase 2 study evaluating FMT with immunotherapy and targeted therapy that reported improvement signals but was designed without a control group, making effectiveness determination difficult. The authors conclude that large-scale prospective studies and well-designed randomized controlled trials are still needed before routine clinical use, and frame FMT in CRC as an investigational intervention rather than an established treatment.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0590\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_a86301e06d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU02_search_candidate_a86301e06d\\\\card\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0152\",\n      \"title\": \"Natural polysaccharides as immune adjuvants to overcome immune checkpoint inhibitor resistance: mechanistic insights, clinical evidence, and translational challenges\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3389/fimmu.2026.1914914\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"43db4adf4e2779233c627e8cd307e2c0ac2ad9c1\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0152\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\format_recovered_reading\\\\43db4adf4e2779233c627e8cd307e2c0ac2ad9c1\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"The authors conduct a narrative review synthesizing literature from structural biology, immunology, oncology, and biomanufacturing. They organize the content by: 1) Structural determinants of bioactivity; 2) Four mechanistic axes of ICI sensitization; 3) Clinical and preclinical evidence; 4) Translational bottlenecks (heterogeneity, PK/PD, trial design); 5) Future strategies (AI, synthetic biology, nanodelivery). They compare polysaccharides with mainstream immunostimulants and contextualize findings within Technology Readiness Levels (TRLs).\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive framework linking polysaccharide structure (ESAR) to immune function and ICI synergy, categorizing mechanisms into four distinct axes.\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a review, it synthesizes existing data rather than generating new experimental evidence; clinical validation is limited to early-phase/small-sample studies.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific translational bottlenecks (structural heterogeneity, low bioavailability) and proposes advanced technical solutions (AI glycomics, synthetic biology, nanodelivery).\",\n            \"limits\": \"Proposed solutions like Industry 6.0 integration and AI models are largely theoretical or in early development stages; practical implementation details are not yet established.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Contextualizes polysaccharides against other adjuvants (CpG, STING agonists, FMT) and defines their niche in terms of safety and tolerability.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Direct head-to-head comparative clinical data between polysaccharides and these other agents are sparse; comparisons are often based on separate study contexts.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Fungal beta-glucans\",\n            \"finding\": \"Intact triple-helix conformation is essential for Dectin-1 binding and immune activation; extraction-induced disruption abolishes activity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Plant/Marine polysaccharides\",\n            \"finding\": \"C-2/C-3 acetylation or sulfation degree are key determinants of TLR4/MyD88 signaling intensity and ROS scavenging.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Gut microbiota modulation\",\n            \"finding\": \"Polysaccharides act as prebiotics to enrich beneficial taxa (e.g., Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) and increase SCFA production, which synergizes with anti-PD-1 therapy; effects are microbiota-dependent.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Clinical evidence\",\n            \"finding\": \"Early-phase trials show promise (e.g., ORR 21.7% in refractory NSCLC with beta-glucan + envafolimab), but large-scale Phase III RCTs are absent; most strategies remain at TRL 3-5.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Safety considerations\",\n            \"finding\": \"Excessive intake of fermentable fibers in dysbiotic models may induce cholestatic liver cancer via SCFA accumulation, highlighting a dose-dependent safety boundary.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"How can natural polysaccharides be rationally designed and utilized to overcome primary and acquired resistance to ICIs in solid tumors, given their complex structures and variable clinical outcomes?\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"Natural polysaccharides (from fungi, plants, marine organisms) as immune adjuvants to overcome resistance to Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors (ICIs) in solid tumors. Covers structural biology, molecular mechanisms, preclinical/clinical evidence, translational challenges, and future technological directions.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This review systematically analyzes the potential of natural polysaccharides to enhance the efficacy of ICIs by reversing tumor immune resistance. It dissects the structure-activity relationships (SAR) governing immunomodulation, elaborates on four core mechanisms (TAM repolarization, CD8+ T cell rejuvenation, gut microbiota remodeling, and suppression of tumor-intrinsic escape), and evaluates current clinical translation bottlenecks such as structural heterogeneity and low bioavailability. The paper positions polysaccharides within the broader landscape of combination therapies, compares them with other adjuvants, and proposes a roadmap for precision medicine using AI-driven glycomics, synthetic biology, and multi-layer biomarkers.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Sets the context for why microbiome modulation is a critical strategy in overcoming ICI resistance.\",\n            \"material\": \"Introduction sections detailing primary/acquired resistance rates, limitations of CAR-T and kinase inhibitors, and the need for novel adjuvants.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Background on ICI resistance and current treatment landscape\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Helps position polysaccharide-based microbiome modulation within the broader spectrum of immunotherapy combinations.\",\n            \"material\": \"Section 5.1 comparing polysaccharides with dual checkpoint blockade, TIM-3 targeting, and oncolytic viruses in terms of efficacy and safety profiles.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Comparison of adjuvant strategies\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Provides forward-looking perspectives on how technology might overcome current limitations in standardizing microbiome-modulating agents.\",\n            \"material\": \"Sections 6.3-6.6 discussing AI-driven QSAR, graph neural networks, synthetic biology, and large-scale N-glycomics.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Technological innovations in glycomics and biomanufacturing\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Most evidence is preclinical (mouse models) or from small early-phase human trials; causal links in humans are inferred from FMT experiments and observational cohorts.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Details the role of gut microbiota in mediating ICI response, specifically highlighting how polysaccharides remodel microbial composition (enriching Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) and metabolite profiles (increasing SCFAs like butyrate and propionate) to enhance anti-tumor immunity.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used to explain the mechanism by which dietary or supplemental polysaccharides sensitize cold tumors to ICIs via microbiota-dependent pathways.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms are often derived from in vitro or syngeneic mouse models; direct translation to human clinical outcomes is not fully validated.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Elaborates on the molecular mechanisms of polysaccharide-mediated immunomodulation, including TAM repolarization (M2 to M1), CD8+ T cell exhaustion reversal, and suppression of tumor-intrinsic escape (PD-L1 downregulation). Links these to specific structural motifs (triple helix, acetylation).\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides mechanistic depth for how adjuvants reverse ICI resistance, offering specific pathways (e.g., STAT3/galectin-3, Dectin-1/Syk/NF-kB) to cite when discussing non-microbiota mechanisms.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Based on specific dysbiotic mouse models; human relevance requires further validation.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Discusses the safety boundaries and risks associated with polysaccharide/fiber supplementation, noting that excessive fermentation in dysbiotic guts may lead to adverse outcomes like cholestatic liver cancer.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Useful for discussing the limitations and risks of microbiota-targeted therapies, providing a balanced view beyond just efficacy.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review serves as a critical resource for understanding how natural polysaccharides modulate the gut microbiome to influence ICI efficacy in solid tumors. It provides detailed mechanistic insights into the bidirectional crosstalk between polysaccharide-derived metabolites (SCFAs, indole derivatives) and host immunity, along with a structured analysis of translational challenges and future technological interventions.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review focuses on natural polysaccharides; do not generalize findings to all prebiotics or dietary fibers without specifying the polysaccharide type.\",\n          \"Clinical evidence is primarily from early-phase trials or real-world retrospective studies; avoid overstating efficacy as if confirmed by Phase III RCTs.\",\n          \"Mechanistic claims are largely supported by preclinical models; distinguish between observed associations in mice and validated causal mechanisms in humans.\",\n          \"Safety warnings (e.g., liver cancer risk) are based on specific dysbiotic conditions; do not assume universal toxicity.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiota-immune axis\",\n          \"short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs)\",\n          \"Akkermansia muciniphila\",\n          \"polysaccharide structural heterogeneity\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitor resistance\",\n          \"solid tumor microenvironment\",\n          \"nanodelivery systems\",\n          \"AI-assisted glycomics\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-edcd953058d1d5fa5081\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-847338f921b68453cb16\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"assets\",\n            \"reason\": \"supplementaryFiles_fetch_transport_TimeoutError\",\n            \"status\": \"missing\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\format_recovered_reading\\\\43db4adf4e2779233c627e8cd307e2c0ac2ad9c1\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0273\",\n      \"title\": \"The Gut Microbiome and Cancer Immunotherapy: Can We Use the Gut Microbiome as a Predictive Biomarker for Clinical Response in Cancer Immunotherapy?\",\n      \"year\": \"2021\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3390/cancers13194824\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙述性综述方法。电子数据库搜索策略包括关键词“cancer”、“immunotherapy/immune checkpoint inhibitor”和“microbiome/microbiota”。纳入标准限定为：成人（>18岁）、英文全文、同行评审期刊发表的研究。排除动物模型研究以专注于临床应用证据。对纳入的10项研究进行定性综合，按癌症类型（黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC等）和治疗结果（疗效、irAEs、FMT干预）分类整理发现。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"首次专门针对临床研究的综述，汇总了肠道微生物组作为ICIs疗效预测生物标志物和irAEs风险因子的证据，强调了FMT在逆转耐药性方面的初步临床可行性。\",\n            \"limits\": \"所纳入的研究样本量普遍较小（多数n≤42），存在异质性（不同癌症类型、不同ICI药物、不同测序方法如16S rRNA与宏基因组）。观察性研究无法确立因果关系。未对纳入的非随机研究进行严格的质量偏倚风险评估。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"接受ICIs治疗的晚期癌症患者（黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC等）\",\n            \"finding\": \"基线肠道微生物组的多样性和组成与ICIs疗效显著相关。高α-多样性通常与较长的PFS和OS相关。特定菌属富集与响应正相关（如Fecalibacterium prausnitzii, Bifidobacterium longum, Akkermansia muciniphila），而某些拟杆菌目（Bacteroidales）丰度高则与非响应相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"接受CTLA-4抑制剂治疗的黑色素瘤患者\",\n            \"finding\": \"低丰度的Bacteroidetes门细菌与免疫检查点抑制剂诱导的结肠炎（GI毒性）相关；相反，较高的Bacteroidetes丰度与抵抗结肠炎发生相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"抗PD-1难治性转移性黑色素瘤患者接受来自响应者的FMT\",\n            \"finding\": \"两项突破性临床试验显示，将来自免疫治疗响应者的粪菌移植给难治性患者，结合抗PD-1治疗，可使部分患者重新获得临床响应（Baruch et al. 3/10响应；Davar et al. 6/15响应），且安全性良好。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管免疫疗法提高了部分晚期癌症患者的生存率，但仅少数患者受益且常伴随严重免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）。目前尚缺乏基于临床证据的指导方针来利用肠道微生物组预测治疗反应或管理副作用。本研究试图厘清肠道微生物组作为预测生物标志物的潜力及其在增强疗效和减少副作用中的作用。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"临床癌症患者（晚期转移性黑色素瘤、肝细胞癌、非小细胞肺癌及肾细胞癌），聚焦于免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）治疗前后的肠道微生物组特征及其与疗效和不良事件的关系。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"该综述旨在评估肠道微生物组对癌症患者免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）临床结局及相关不良事件（AEs）的影响。作者检索了截至2021年3月的PubMed、Medline和ScienceDirect数据库，筛选出10项涉及成年人的临床研究。研究综合分析了基线微生物组的多样性（α/β多样性）和组成成分与ICIs疗效（无进展生存期PFS、总生存期OS）及胃肠道毒性（如结肠炎）之间的关联，并探讨了粪菌移植（FMT）在难治性患者中的潜在调节作用。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该论文提供了关于肠道微生物组如何影响实体瘤免疫治疗疗效的临床证据汇总，特别指出了特定菌群（如Faecalibacterium, Akkermansia）与疗效的正向关联，以及抗生素使用或特定菌群缺失与疗效不佳或毒性的关联。此外，它提供了FMT作为克服免疫耐药的临床干预案例，是讨论微生物组调控机制和临床转化潜力的重要素材。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些关联主要基于观察性研究，存在癌症类型异质性和样本量小的限制，不能直接推断因果机制。不同研究中具体差异菌种存在不一致性。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了多项临床研究数据，表明基线肠道微生物组的特定组成（如Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, Bifidobacterium longum, Akkermansia muciniphila的高丰度）和较高多样性与ICIs治疗的良好临床响应（延长PFS/OS）相关。同时指出Bacteroidales的高丰度与非响应相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于支持“肠道微生物组特征可预测免疫治疗疗效”的观点，列举具体有益菌和有害菌作为证据。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅两项研究涉及此方面，且主要针对CTLA-4抑制剂，推广至其他ICI需谨慎。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"揭示了肠道微生物组与免疫治疗不良反应（特别是结肠炎）的联系。低Bacteroidetes丰度与CTLA-4诱导的结肠炎风险增加相关。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于说明微生物组不仅影响疗效，还影响安全性/耐受性，可作为全面评估免疫治疗预后的维度。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"样本量极小，仅为概念验证性质。供体和受体间的个体差异、预处理抗生素的使用等因素可能影响结果。尚未阐明具体的分子机制或关键菌株。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"通过FMT临床试验（Baruch et al., Davar et al.）展示了从响应者向难治者移植微生物组可恢复部分患者的治疗响应，并观察到CD8+ T细胞激活增加和IL-8表达髓系细胞减少。这为微生物组介导免疫治疗效应的因果假设提供了最强的临床间接证据。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于论证微生物组在介导免疫治疗效应中的潜在因果作用，以及通过微生物组调节（如FMT）改善预后的可能性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"主要是相关性观察，未详细区分抗生素种类、使用时机及具体影响的菌群通路。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"指出抗生素使用（尤其是ICIs期间）与较差的PFS和OS相关，提示抗生素引起的菌群失调可能是导致免疫治疗耐药的一个因素。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释环境因素（如抗生素）如何通过改变微生物组进而影响治疗机制，补充机制部分的背景。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于阐述当前领域从基础机制研究向临床转化过渡的现状与挑战，强调本综述关注临床数据的必要性。\",\n            \"material\": \"综述指出目前尚无基于临床证据的指南来利用微生物组指导免疫治疗，且既往综述多包含动物模型，导致临床应用存在差距。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景与现状概述\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"用于讨论当前证据等级的局限性，并为未来需要标准化、大规模RCT研究提供依据。\",\n            \"material\": \"提到现有研究在测序方法（16S vs 宏基因组）、样本采集时间点上的异质性，以及缺乏标准化的偏倚评估工具。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"方法论局限性与未来方向\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"可用于分析微生物组-宿主互作是否具有癌症类型特异性，还是存在通用的“响应型”菌群特征。\",\n            \"material\": \"汇总了黑色素瘤、HCC、NSCLC中不同的优势菌种（如黑色素瘤中Faecalibacterium，HCC中Akkermansia/Ruminococcaceae，NSCLC中Akkermansia/Alistipes）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"对比不同癌症类型的微生物组特征\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述仅纳入临床研究，排除了动物实验，因此其关于机制的解释（如T细胞激活）主要基于人类临床样本的相关性或FMT后的表型变化，而非直接的分子通路解析。\",\n          \"发现的菌群与疗效的关联多为观察性结果，存在混杂因素（如饮食、生活方式、合并用药），不能直接视为因果证据，除非有FMT等干预性研究的支持。\",\n          \"不同研究间使用的测序区域（V3-V4, V4, V4-V5）和分析方法不同，导致具体菌种鉴定可能存在偏差，比较时需考虑技术异质性。\",\n          \"FMT研究样本量极小（n=10, n=15），结果仅代表初步安全性和可行性，不能代表普遍疗效。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"肠道微生物组多样性（Alpha/Beta diversity）\",\n          \"特定菌属丰度（Fecalibacterium, Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia, Bacteroides）\",\n          \"免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）疗效（PFS, OS, Response Rate）\",\n          \"免疫相关不良事件（irAEs, Colitis）\",\n          \"粪菌移植（FMT）干预\",\n          \"抗生素使用对微生物组及疗效的影响\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"supplement_materials\": [\n        {\n          \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review details methodological heterogeneity across the included studies, such as variations in sequencing regions (V3-V4, V4, V4-V5), sample collection times, and cancer types, which complicates direct comparison. It also notes that only two studies examined gastrointestinal toxicity (colitis), finding low Bacteroidetes abundance associated with higher risk. The authors mention that while FMT response rates in these small studies are comparable to RCTs for Clostridioides difficile infection, the cancer FMT studies themselves are described as 'breakthrough clinical studies' rather than large-scale Phase III trials. The review excludes animal models to focus on clinical applicability but acknowledges that most included studies are observational (retrospective or prospective cohorts) rather than randomized interventions.\",\n          \"evidence\": [],\n          \"gap_id\": \"phase_iii_rct_evidence\",\n          \"judgment_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\level2\\\\external\\\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\\\round_1\\\\supplements\\\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\\\source_units\\\\SU03_search_candidate_2d56a11919\\\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json\",\n          \"limitations\": [],\n          \"material_ready\": true,\n          \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n          \"outline_action\": \"Use this material to characterize the current evidence landscape as primarily observational with emerging but limited interventional data. Cite the authors' explicit statement that well-designed RCTs are still required to establish causality and clinical guidelines. Use the details of the Baruch and Davar studies to illustrate the proof-of-concept stage of FMT in oncology, contrasting their small sample sizes and specific indication (refractory melanoma) against the gap's requirement for large-scale Phase III trials in solid tumors. This supports the conclusion that the gap remains unresolved due to the immaturity of interventional trial data.\",\n          \"outline_action_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific evidence requested—completed large-scale randomized controlled Phase III clinical trials confirming microbiome intervention (FMT) efficacy in ICI-treated solid tumors—is not present in this material. The available FMT data is limited to small-sample (n=10, n=15), early-phase studies restricted to refractory melanoma, not broad solid tumors. The authors' call for future robust RCTs and multicentre trials indicates that such Phase III evidence does not yet exist or was not captured in this review (search up to March 2021).\",\n          \"remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy\": false,\n          \"status\": \"unmet\",\n          \"useful_material\": \"This study is a clinical review analyzing 10 studies (total n=467 patients) regarding the gut microbiome and immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) outcomes in solid tumors (melanoma, HCC, NSCLC, RCC). It reports that baseline microbiome diversity and composition (e.g., enrichment of Faecalibacterium, Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) are associated with improved progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS), while Bacteroidales abundance often correlates with non-response. Crucially for the gap, the authors highlight two FMT clinical studies (Baruch et al., n=10; Davar et al., n=15) in anti-PD-1 refractory metastatic melanoma. These studies demonstrated safety and potential efficacy (30-40% response rate) when combining FMT from responders with ICI re-challenge. However, the authors explicitly state that 'further well-designed RCTs are required to explore the causal effects' and that 'future international multicentre trials will be required to provide comprehensive and reliable data.' They note that up-to-date practice guidelines based on clinical evidence are not yet available.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0273\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\\\\c3429e894a9546214bd063ce941ed466\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-403bce67afb0fdb07c1f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\759b176490dcac135bff0b645dc106c3df8f4059\\\\c3429e894a9546214bd063ce941ed466\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0417\",\n      \"title\": \"Impact of the gut microbiome on immune checkpoint inhibitor efficacy-a systematic review.\",\n      \"year\": \"2019\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3747/co.26.5177\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"b6efedbc1ffd0d54c8ed8ce3eb0a38c148e45450\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0417\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\b6efedbc1ffd0d54c8ed8ce3eb0a38c148e45450\\\\aab60b62006cb6c9d5403e0993070595\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"遵循PRISMA指南进行系统综述。在Medline数据库中搜索“cancer immunotherapy”或“immune checkpoint inhibitors”与“microbiome”、“probiotic”、“antibiotic”、“dysbiosis”的组合词。纳入标准为英语或法语出版物，描述可量化的微生物组组成/修饰与ICI反应或irAEs之间的联系。排除综述、评论和专家意见（但筛查其参考文献）。数据提取变量包括患者数量、ICI类型、癌症类型、微生物组评估方法、干预措施及量化效应。未进行定量合成（Meta分析），而是进行定性综合。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了截至2018年底关于肠道微生物组如何影响ICI疗效和毒性的临床证据的综合视图。确认了抗生素使用的负面影响，并指出了特定微生物特征（如多样性、特定菌属比例）与疗效的潜在联系。\",\n            \"limits\": \"仅检索Medline数据库，可能遗漏其他来源的研究。纳入的研究多为回顾性、样本量小且异质性高，缺乏前瞻性试验数据。未能确定具体的因果机制，且无法精确定义抗生素暴露的最佳时间窗口或优先避免的抗生素类别。所给摘要未交代具体的统计学检验细节及偏倚风险评估过程。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"实体瘤患者（NSCLC, RCC为主），接受ICI治疗前后使用抗生素（ABt组 vs ABn组）\",\n            \"finding\": \"抗生素使用与较低的ICI疗效相关。在RCC患者中，抗生素显著缩短PFS和OS，降低ORR；在NSCLC患者中，抗生素显著降低OS，但对PFS和ORR的影响不一致或无显著差异。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"不同癌症类型（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、RCC、SCCHN），基线粪便或唾液样本\",\n            \"finding\": \"微生物组组成与ICI疗效存在显著关联，但具体细菌物种在不同研究中不一致（如Bifidobacterium longum, Akkermansia muciniphila, Ruminococcaceae等）。高细菌多样性和“响应者相关”细菌与“非响应者相关”细菌的良好比例似乎与更好的患者预后相关。口腔微生物组与ICI疗效无明显关联。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"黑色素瘤患者，接受Ipilimumab治疗\",\n            \"finding\": \"微生物组组成与irAEs（特别是结肠炎）的发生相关。某些细菌（如Faecalibacterium prausnitzii）的高丰度与结肠炎风险增加相关，而其他细菌（如Bacteroidetes）的高丰度则与低发生率相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"ICI诱导的难治性结肠炎患者\",\n            \"finding\": \"在两例个案报告中，粪便微生物群移植（FMT）成功缓解了ICI相关的结肠炎症状。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管前体模型显示肠道微生物组显著影响ICI疗效，但在临床实践中这种相互作用尚不清楚。本研究试图确定：(1)抗生素是否影响ICI疗效；(2)基线微生物组组成是否与ICI疗效相关；(3)基线微生物组组成是否与irAEs发生相关；(4)微生物组操纵是否能减轻irAEs。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"人类实体瘤患者（主要为非小细胞肺癌、肾细胞癌、黑色素瘤等）接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs，包括抗CTLA-4、抗PD-1和抗PD-L1抗体）治疗期间，肠道微生物组（GM）及其修饰（如抗生素使用、粪便微生物群移植FMT）对ICI疗效及免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的影响。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"这是一篇2019年发表的系统综述，旨在评估临床实践中肠道微生物组与免疫检查点抑制剂疗效之间的关联。作者通过Medline检索，筛选出13项符合纳入标准的人类研究（包括全文文章和摘要），分别分析了抗生素使用对ICI疗效的影响、基线微生物组组成与ICI疗效的相关性、以及微生物组组成与irAEs（特别是结肠炎）的相关性。研究发现抗生素使用普遍与ICI疗效降低相关，而特定的微生物组成或多样性与更好的临床结局有关；此外，FMT在个别案例中显示出缓解ICI诱导的结肠炎的潜力。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供临床实践中的管理建议背景：避免在使用ICI期间使用抗生素。\",\n            \"material\": \"综述结论建议尽可能避免在ICI治疗前或使用期间使用抗生素，因为其对ICI抗癌疗效有负面影响。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为临床干预策略的背景信息，说明当前临床决策中考虑微生物组因素的紧迫性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"介绍FMT作为潜在治疗手段的现状。\",\n            \"material\": \"FMT在动物模型中恢复ICI疗效并减少结肠炎，在人类个案中成功缓解难治性结肠炎，但尚无改善ICI疗效的人体数据。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为未来研究方向和潜在干预措施的例子，展示从微生物组调控角度改善治疗结果的潜力。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"区分口腔与肠道微生物组的作用。\",\n            \"material\": \"口腔微生物组与ICI疗效无明显关联，而肠道微生物组有显著关联。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"明确研究对象范围，强调肠道微生物组在ICI疗效中的特异性作用，排除口腔微生物组的干扰。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"相关性不等于因果性；具体菌种的预测价值在不同研究中存在冲突，不能直接确立单一通用生物标志物。样本量较小，多为回顾性研究。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了抗生素使用与ICI疗效降低之间关联的多项临床研究汇总，包括ORR、PFS和OS的具体变化趋势。同时列举了不同癌症类型中微生物组组成与疗效关联的具体菌种（如Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium等），尽管这些菌种在不同研究中不一致。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节ICI疗效”中的现象学部分，即哪些因素（抗生素、特定菌群）与疗效正负相关。可用于对比不同研究中预测标志物的异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些主要是基于动物模型或观察性数据的推测，文中明确指出人类患者中尚未进行类似的机制验证研究。无法直接证明具体的分子机制路径。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"讨论了抗生素可能通过改变微生物组选择不利菌种、或通过抗生素本身的抗炎特性（如降低促炎细胞因子、减少T细胞反应）来拮抗ICI疗效的潜在机制。指出微生物组与irAEs（结肠炎）的关联可能反映了ICI疗效与免疫介导毒性之间的正相关（表型伴随现象）。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"为解释“机制”提供理论假设和间接证据。特别是关于抗生素非特异性抗炎作用的讨论，以及微生物组介导的免疫激活导致疗效和毒性并存的假说。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物组对ICI疗效的双向调节作用提供了关键的临床背景素材。它汇总了抗生素作为负面调节因子的证据，以及特定微生物特征作为疗效预测指标的初步发现。对于综述而言，此文有助于构建“微生物组-免疫疗法相互作用”的临床现状图景，特别是关于抗生素干扰和微生物组成预测价值的讨论，可作为机制探讨前的现象学基础。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述主要涵盖实体瘤，特别是NSCLC、RCC和黑色素瘤，结果可能不适用于血液肿瘤或其他类型癌症。\",\n          \"关于特定细菌物种与疗效的关联在不同研究中存在不一致，这可能与检测方法、人群特征和医疗史差异有关，需谨慎解读单一菌种的预测价值。\",\n          \"抗生素的影响可能是多方面的（微生物组改变+直接抗炎作用），综述未区分具体抗生素种类的影响差异，仅指出广谱抗生素影响更显著。\",\n          \"FMT在缓解irAEs中的效果仅基于个案报告，不能推广为常规治疗建议。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"抗生素对ICI疗效的负面影响\",\n          \"基线微生物组组成与ICI疗效的相关性\",\n          \"微生物组多样性与临床预后的关系\",\n          \"微生物组组成与irAEs（结肠炎）的关联\",\n          \"粪便微生物群移植（FMT）在缓解irAEs中的应用\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"identity\",\n            \"reason\": \"source identity was not independently verified\",\n            \"status\": \"input_attributed\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"pdf\",\n            \"reason\": \"full-page text extracted; page positions retained\",\n            \"status\": \"fitz\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"kind\": \"fitz\",\n            \"reason\": \"layout, table relations, formulas, figure pixels, and scanned text may be lost\",\n            \"status\": \"known_gap\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\b6efedbc1ffd0d54c8ed8ce3eb0a38c148e45450\\\\aab60b62006cb6c9d5403e0993070595\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0096\",\n      \"title\": \"The Gut Microbiome from a Biomarker to a Novel Therapeutic Strategy for Immunotherapy Response in Patients with Lung Cancer\",\n      \"year\": \"2023\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3390/curroncol30110681\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"261e0a3618ae7acf6e831646379929eff88857d5\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0096\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\261e0a3618ae7acf6e831646379929eff88857d5\\\\f2397cb158bb42a6eda954d2993a8748\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性综述方法，综合分析了近年来关于NSCLC患者微生物组与ICI反应关联的临床研究、回顾性队列分析以及动物模型实验。重点比较了响应者与无响应者的微生物组成差异，分析了抗生素使用对预后的影响机制，并汇总了正在进行的或已完成的涉及FMT、益生菌、饮食等干预措施的临床试验结果。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC患者微生物组特征与ICI疗效关联的最新证据汇总，明确了关键的保护性和风险性菌属。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，其结论依赖于所引用的原始研究，这些研究多为回顾性或单中心小规模试验，存在地理偏倚（主要来自中国和日本）和方法学异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"详细阐述了抗生素和PPI对ICI疗效的负面干扰机制，为临床合理用药提供依据。\",\n            \"limits\": \"对于微生物组调节的具体分子机制（如抗原特异性与非特异性机制的细节）尚未完全阐明，文中指出“大部分机制仍未知”。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"评估了FMT、MET、益生菌、饮食等多种干预策略的临床转化潜力。\",\n            \"limits\": \"大多数微生物组干预措施仍处于早期临床试验阶段，缺乏针对NSCLC的大规模随机对照试验结果，长期安全性和有效性尚不确定。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"接受抗PD-1/PD-L1治疗的NSCLC患者\",\n            \"finding\": \"特定细菌丰度与疗效相关：A. muciniphila、Ruminococcus、Faecalibacterium、Blautia等通常与良好预后相关；而Parabacteroides、Fusobacterium、Hungatella等则与耐药相关。值得注意的是，A. muciniphila丰度过高（超过第77百分位）可能与较短的总生存期相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"ICI治疗前近期使用抗生素（特别是β-内酰胺酶抑制剂、氟喹诺酮类）\",\n            \"finding\": \"抗生素使用导致肠道菌群失调，显著缩短NSCLC患者的中位总生存期（OS）和无进展生存期（PFS），且这种负面影响在ICI治疗前30天内最为明显。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"动物模型及初步临床试验\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT从ICI响应者转移至小鼠可恢复ICI疗效；口服A. muciniphila可逆转耐药。部分临床试验显示FMT联合ICI在黑色素瘤和NSCLC中具有安全性和初步疗效，但针对NSCLC的大规模数据仍有限。益生菌的效果在不同研究中存在不一致性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管ICI在NSCLC中取得了显著疗效，但许多患者出现原发或继发耐药及免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）。目前尚缺乏有效的预测生物标志物和克服耐药的策略。本研究旨在阐明微生物组如何影响ICI疗效，并探索基于微生物组的新型治疗途径。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，聚焦于肠道微生物组、肺部微生物组及其代谢产物与免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效及毒性的关系。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇综述，系统梳理了肠道和肺部微生物组作为预测生物标志物及辅助治疗策略在NSCLC免疫治疗中的应用现状。文章首先回顾了抗生素（ATB）和质子泵抑制剂（PPI）等合并用药对微生物组的破坏作用及其对ICI疗效的负面影响；其次，总结了多项临床研究中确定的与ICI响应相关的特定细菌特征（如Akkermansia muciniphila、Faecalibacterium等）；最后，评估了通过粪便微生物移植（FMT）、益生菌、益生元、饮食干预及噬菌体疗法等调节微生物组的潜在治疗手段及其临床试验进展。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供NSCLC免疫治疗背景下微生物组研究的整体图景和最新进展。\",\n            \"material\": \"综述了从传统化疗到ICI治疗的转变，以及微生物组作为“癌症新标志物”的地位确立过程。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为综述的背景介绍，说明为什么关注微生物组是当前的热点和未满足的临床需求。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于合并用药（抗生素、PPI）对免疫治疗影响的详细数据。\",\n            \"material\": \"引用了多个大型队列研究，显示ATB和PPI使用显著降低OS和PFS，并讨论了时间窗和药物类别的影响。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于讨论外部因素（药物暴露）如何干扰微生物组介导的治疗效果，强调临床管理的重要性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于肺部微生物组与肠道微生物组关系的初步见解。\",\n            \"material\": \"指出肺部微生物组（如Veillonella, Streptococcus）与肿瘤进展相关，并提示其与肠道微生物组的联系有待探索。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"扩展综述的范围，不仅限于肠道，还涵盖肺部局部微环境，体现多部位微生物互作的复杂性。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些关联主要基于观察性研究和回顾性队列，因果关系需结合机制研究确认。不同研究间的菌属定义可能存在技术差异（16S vs WGS）。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC患者中与ICI响应和非响应相关的特定微生物特征列表。例如，响应者富集Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, Bifidobacterium spp., Blautia等；非响应者富集Parabacteroides, Fusobacterium, Hungatella等。同时指出了A. muciniphila丰度过高的潜在风险。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节ICI疗效”中的相关性部分，即确定哪些微生物群落结构与疗效改善或恶化有关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"许多机制细节来自小鼠模型或体外实验，直接外推到人类NSCLC患者需谨慎。作者明确指出大部分机制“仍 largely unknown”，现有解释多为假说或初步证据。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提出了几种可能的机制路径：1) 抗生素通过下调MAdCAM-1表达，促进Enterocloster增殖，进而抑制Th17/Treg细胞向肿瘤迁移；2) 某些细菌（如E. hirae）携带的噬菌体蛋白可与肿瘤抗原发生交叉反应，激活CD8+ T细胞；3) 短链脂肪酸（如丁酸）由有益菌产生，可能增强抗肿瘤免疫反应；4) 肺部微生物（如Veillonella）通过上调PI3K/Akt和ERK/MAPK通路促进肿瘤进展。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导该效应的机制是什么”，提供具体的分子和细胞生物学解释框架。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"多数试验处于I/II期，样本量小，且部分结果来自其他癌种（如黑色素瘤、肾细胞癌），不能直接等同于NSCLC的最终结论。益生菌效果在不同研究中存在矛盾。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"总结了当前针对NSCLC的微生物组干预临床试验（NCT编号），包括FMT、MET、益生菌（如C. butyricum, Bifidobacterium）和饮食补充剂（如Castalagin, MicrSoy-20）的进展和初步结果。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于展示将微生物组发现转化为临床实践的现状和挑战，特别是在讨论治疗策略时提供具体案例。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物组如何调节固体肿瘤（特别是NSCLC）中的ICI疗效提供了全面的背景素材。它涵盖了从生物标志物识别（哪些菌有益/有害）到干预策略（FMT、益生菌、饮食）的完整链条，特别强调了抗生素使用的危害和特定菌属（如Akkermansia）的双刃剑效应。可用于构建综述中关于“微生物组作为预测因子”、“环境因素（药物/饮食）的影响”以及“微生物组靶向治疗”的核心章节。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述主要聚焦于NSCLC，虽然提及黑色素瘤和肾细胞癌的FMT数据，但不能直接将其结果推广到其他实体瘤或所有肺癌亚型。\",\n          \"文中提到的微生物组与疗效的关联多为相关性，因果机制大多仍在假设阶段，特别是关于具体菌株的作用机制。\",\n          \"抗生素对ICI疗效的负面影响在大多数研究中得到证实，但也存在少数相反结果的报道，需注意这一争议性。\",\n          \"益生菌的效果在不同研究中不一致，部分研究显示有益，部分显示无效甚至有害，需谨慎解读其作为常规辅助治疗的潜力。\",\n          \"微生物组检测技术（16S rRNA vs WGS）和分析方法的差异可能导致不同研究间结果的不可比性。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"肠道微生物组组成与ICI疗效的相关性\",\n          \"肺部微生物组在肺癌中的作用\",\n          \"抗生素诱导的菌群失调对免疫治疗的影响\",\n          \"粪便微生物移植（FMT）在实体瘤中的应用\",\n          \"益生菌、益生元及饮食干预对免疫微环境的调节\",\n          \"短链脂肪酸（SCFA）在抗肿瘤免疫中的作用\",\n          \"噬菌体疗法与微生物抗原交叉反应\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-403bce67afb0fdb07c1f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\261e0a3618ae7acf6e831646379929eff88857d5\\\\f2397cb158bb42a6eda954d2993a8748\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0148\",\n      \"title\": \"Response to immune checkpoint inhibition is associated with the gut microbiome in advanced KRAS-mutated non-small cell lung cancer\",\n      \"year\": \"2023\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1101/2023.10.30.23297712\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0148\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\\\\51a215242479c34d622b6d11c5525875\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"Observational cohort study. 1. Participant selection: 40 recruited, 33 eligible (KRAS-mutated NSCLC, baseline stool collected). 2. Sequencing: Shotgun metagenomics (Illumina HiSeq 2000). 3. Processing: KneadData for host removal, HUMAnN3 for functional profiles, MetaPhlAn3 for taxonomy. Normalization via arcsine square-root (taxa) and log (pathways). 4. Statistical Analysis: Shannon index for alpha-diversity; PCA on clr-transformed data for beta-diversity; PERMANOVA for variance explanation; Bayesian logistic-normal linear regression (Pibble model) to identify differential abundance of species/pathways associated with DCR, PFS12, and irAEs, adjusting for covariates. Credible intervals used for significance (75% and 90%).\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"First study to profile the gut microbiome exclusively in KRAS-mutated NSCLC patients treated with ICI. Identifies specific microbial signatures (SCFA-producers, Akkermansia) overlapping with findings in other tumor types (melanoma, RCC), suggesting potential tumor-independent mechanisms.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Small sample size (n=33), single time-point (baseline only), limited statistical power (wide credible intervals for some associations like Akkermansia). Cannot establish causality. Regional/methodological differences may limit generalizability compared to larger multi-center cohorts.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Responders (DCR/PFS12) vs Non-responders\",\n            \"finding\": \"No significant difference in alpha or beta diversity. Responders enriched in SCFA-producing species (Agathobaculum butyriciproducens, Clostridium leptum, Bifidobacterium longum, Eubacterium ramulus, Fusicatenibacter saccharivorans) and Akkermansia muciniphila (wider CI). Responders also showed higher abundance of pro-inflammatory pathways (LPS, heme synthesis). Non-responders enriched in Bacteroides spp. and Blautia genus.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Patients with irAEs vs Patients without irAEs\",\n            \"finding\": \"No diversity differences. Patients unaffected by irAEs enriched in biotin and SCFA synthesis pathways. Patients developing irAEs enriched in Alistipes finegoldii, Bifidobacterium longum, and Bacteroides uniformis, and amino acid synthesis pathways.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Covariate analysis (PERMANOVA)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Metastatic disease stage and sex explained most variation in species composition. Response, ICI regimen, stage, and age explained 4-6% of pathway variation, but none reached statistical significance.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"To determine if the gut microbiome composition and function are associated with response to anti-PD-1 immunotherapy and the development of irAEs specifically in patients with KRAS-mutated NSCLC, a population where such associations have not been previously established.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"Advanced KRAS-mutated non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients treated with anti-PD-1 immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI). Cohort: 33 patients, baseline stool samples only.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This study investigates the association between the gut microbiome and clinical outcomes in a homogeneous cohort of advanced KRAS-mutated NSCLC patients receiving ICI therapy. The authors performed shotgun metagenomic sequencing on baseline stool samples to analyze microbial composition and functional pathways. They compared responders versus non-responders (defined by DCR and PFS12) and patients with vs. without immune-related adverse events (irAEs), adjusting for confounders like ICI regimen, metastatic stage, age, sex, and BMI. The study found no differences in overall alpha or beta diversity but identified specific species and metabolic pathways differentially abundant between groups.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Useful for a section discussing why microbiome biomarkers may vary across populations and the need for standardized methodologies or large multi-center validation.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion compares findings with studies from China, Japan, and other European cohorts, noting differences in associated species (e.g., Bacteroides massiliensis in Chinese cohort vs. Bacteroides in this cohort). Attributes differences to diet, geography, and methodological factors.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Contextualize regional/methodological heterogeneity in microbiome studies.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Supports the argument that microbiome effects might be modified by host genetics or tumor genotype, necessitating stratified analyses in future reviews.\",\n            \"material\": \"Focuses exclusively on KRAS-mutated NSCLC, a subgroup with poor prognosis and heterogeneous response to standard therapies.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"Highlight the importance of genetic subgroups in NSCLC.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Study is limited to KRAS-mutated NSCLC. Sample size is small (n=33). Associations are observational. Some findings (e.g., Akkermansia) did not reach high statistical confidence (90% BCL) due to sample size.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Identifies specific microbial features associated with ICI response in KRAS-mutated NSCLC: enrichment of SCFA-producing species (e.g., Agathobaculum, Bifidobacterium) and Akkermansia in responders; enrichment of Bacteroides/Blautia in non-responders.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Can be used as evidence for the association between beneficial microbes (SCFA producers) and improved ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Supports the hypothesis that certain microbial signatures predict response across different cancer types.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Pathway-level inference from metagenomics. Causality of these pathways in driving response is not established.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Reports that responders also had higher abundance of pro-inflammatory pathways (LPS, heme synthesis), challenging the notion that only 'healthy' microbiomes predict response.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Useful for discussing the complexity of microbial mechanisms in ICI response, indicating that inflammatory signals may also be part of the effective anti-tumor immune activation.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Mechanisms are inferred from correlation. Direct causal links between specific bacterial metabolites and immune checkpoint blockade efficacy are not demonstrated in this study.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Suggests potential mechanisms involving fiber fermentation (SCFA production) and vitamin synthesis (biotin) as mediators of ICI efficacy and protection from irAEs.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Provides mechanistic hypotheses (dietary fiber -> SCFA -> enhanced immunity) that can be discussed in the context of how the microbiome modulates ICI efficacy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Cross-sectional association at baseline. Does not prove these microbes cause irAEs.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Associates irAE development with specific microbes (Alistipes, Bifidobacterium, Bacteroides uniformis) and amino acid synthesis pathways.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Helps explore the link between microbiome and immune toxicity (irAEs), which is a key aspect of ICI modulation. May inform strategies to manage side effects.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"Provides empirical evidence linking specific gut microbial taxa (SCFA producers, Akkermansia) and pathways to ICI response in KRAS-mutated NSCLC. Offers a comparative perspective on whether microbial biomarkers are tumor-type specific or universal, and highlights the complexity of 'pro-inflammatory' vs 'health-associated' signatures in responders.\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The study is observational; associations do not imply causation.\",\n          \"Small sample size limits statistical power and generalizability.\",\n          \"Single time-point sampling cannot capture dynamic changes in the microbiome during treatment.\",\n          \"Findings are specific to KRAS-mutated NSCLC; extrapolation to other NSCLC subtypes or cancers requires caution, although overlaps with other tumor types are noted.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"KRAS-mutated NSCLC\",\n          \"Anti-PD-1 Immunotherapy\",\n          \"Gut Microbiome Composition\",\n          \"Short Chain Fatty Acid (SCFA) Producers\",\n          \"Akkermansia muciniphila\",\n          \"Bacteroides/Blautia Genus\",\n          \"Immune-related Adverse Events (irAEs)\",\n          \"Metabolic Pathways (Biotin, LPS, Heme)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-8a2eafa351c193b6e72f\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\42b8755a18f7ff2ebb90765b502a37b6f6e868e5\\\\51a215242479c34d622b6d11c5525875\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0474\",\n      \"title\": \"Tumor microenvironment-mediated resistance to immune checkpoint inhibitors in non-small cell lung cancer: Mechanisms, combination strategies and clinical perspectives (Review)\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3892/mco.2026.2971\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0474\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\\\\627d038304660198900f99576a1ce6c8\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性文献回顾方法。检索PubMed索引的英文出版物（1946-2026年）、主要肿瘤学指南、关键II/III期临床试验、荟萃分析及机制研究。检索词涵盖NSCLC、ICI、TME、耐药及相关分子靶点。对临床证据按试验阶段、人群相关性、终点成熟度及监管意义进行加权；对临床前和假设生成性研究主要用于支持机制概念。通过分类归纳TME组成、分子通路、物理化学抑制因素及系统性因素，构建耐药机制框架，并据此评估不同组合疗法的临床转化潜力。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC中TME介导耐药的整合性框架，强调从孤立因素转向相互连接的生物学过程视角；明确了不同联合疗法（免疫-免疫、免疫-抗血管、免疫-放化疗、免疫-靶向）的临床证据层级和适用情境。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为叙事性综述，其结论依赖于作者对纳入文献的选择和解释；未提供新的原始实验数据；部分机制（如微生物组干预）仅基于初步研究，缺乏大规模临床验证；特定生物标志物（如ctDNA阈值）尚待标准化。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"TME细胞成分异质性\",\n            \"finding\": \"巨噬细胞并非简单的M1/M2二元模型，而是存在激活状态的连续谱系；MDSCs亚型特异性功能不同（单核型通过ARG1/iNOS抑制，多形核型通过ROS抑制）；CAF和ECM重塑通过增加间质硬度促进免疫排斥。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"内在肿瘤机制\",\n            \"finding\": \"STK11缺失抑制I型干扰素信号，KEAP1缺失激活抗氧化程序，两者共同导致低PD-L1表达和低CD8+ T细胞浸润；EGFR/ALK驱动突变通常伴随低TMB和不活跃的免疫微环境。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"联合治疗证据等级\",\n            \"finding\": \"纳武利尤单抗+伊匹木单抗在CheckMate 227中显示疗效（受PD-L1/TMB影响）；阿替利珠单抗+贝伐珠单抗+化疗在IMpower150中证实对转移性非鳞状NSCLC有效，包括肝转移和EGFR突变TKI失败后人群；LAG-3/TIM-3抑制剂在NSCLC中的临床采纳仍需成熟试验确认。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"生物标志物应用\",\n            \"finding\": \"PD-L1检测存在空间异质性和平台间不完全互换性；高TMB预测价值受测序平台和肿瘤纯度影响；空间转录组学可区分免疫表型；液体活检（ctDNA/exosome）需前瞻性验证阈值。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"解决NSCLC中ICI原发性和获得性耐药限制长期临床获益的问题；阐明TME各组分如何协同导致不同免疫表型，并厘清当前联合治疗策略的证据等级与局限性。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者中，肿瘤微环境（TME）介导的免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药机制、联合治疗策略及生物标志物框架。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇叙事性综述，旨在将TME介导的ICI耐药视为一个相互连接的生物学过程，而非孤立因素的集合。文章系统梳理了免疫抑制细胞群、基质重塑、血管功能障碍、缺氧、代谢重编程以及抗原呈递缺陷和致癌驱动信号如何协同作用，形成免疫沙漠、免疫排斥或耗竭性炎症表型。在此基础上，综述评估了针对这些机制的联合治疗策略，区分了III期支持的临床标准与早期探索性方法，并提出了整合PD-L1、TMB、干扰素γ特征、空间组学、ctDNA等指标的操作化生物标志物框架，以指导NSCLC的精准免疫治疗。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"作为背景材料，帮助读者理解肠道微生物组只是影响ICI疗效的众多系统性因素之一，且其作用受到肿瘤类型和局部微环境的制约。\",\n            \"material\": \"介绍了NSCLC中ICI耐药的多维度机制，包括内在肿瘤基因改变、局部TME抑制和系统性因素。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供NSCLC中ICI治疗的临床背景和耐药复杂性，说明为何单一靶点治疗往往失败，从而引出多因素（包括微生物组）调控的重要性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"在评估微生物组 biomarker 的价值时，可作为参照系，说明新型生物标志物面临的挑战（如标准化、验证），并提示未来可能需要多组学整合模型。\",\n            \"material\": \"讨论了PD-L1、TMB、空间组学和液体活检等生物标志物的现状、局限性和整合需求。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供关于生物标志物和患者分层的信息，特别是当讨论微生物组作为预测因子时，需要与其他已知生物标志物（如PD-L1, TMB）进行比较和整合。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"明确指出微生物组相关干预目前仍处于“有强烈机制吸引力但需要前瞻性临床验证”的阶段，避免将其过度解读为已确立的标准治疗。\",\n            \"material\": \"区分了III期支持的联合疗法（如双免疫、免疫+抗血管）与早期探索性疗法（如微生物组调节、IDO抑制）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"提供关于联合治疗策略的证据分级，帮助判断基于微生物组的干预措施在当前临床实践中的地位。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"仅限NSCLC人群；关联多为观察性，因果机制未详述；Fusobacterium的作用在其他癌种更明确，此处仅为对比提示。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了NSCLC队列中与ICI疗效相关的特定肠道微生物菌群信息：Akkermansia muciniphila、Bifidobacterium spp. 和 Ruminococcus 被报道有助于促进有利的免疫微环境；而 Fusobacterium 的关联在结直肠癌中更强，在NSCLC中需谨慎外推。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节ICI疗效”的具体实例，展示有益菌和潜在有害菌在NSCLC中的不同表现。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制描述较为概括，未深入具体的微生物-宿主分子互作细节；主要基于综述转述的其他研究结果；强调需要前瞻性验证。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"阐述了系统性因素（包括微生物组、营养状态、全身炎症）如何作为TME介导耐药的一部分影响ICI疗效。指出微生物组特征可能通过塑造有利的免疫微环境来增强ICI反应，但同时也强调了这种关联需要NSCLC特异性数据集的支持，且目前微生物组干预（如FMT、益生菌）仍属探索性阶段，缺乏标准化评估和安全监测。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于解释微生物组影响ICI疗效的系统性背景机制，以及当前干预手段的局限性。帮助区分已观察到的关联与尚未验证的因果机制。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解Gut Microbiome如何影响ICI疗效提供了关键的宿主背景框架。虽然其主要焦点是NSCLC的TME，但它详细阐述了系统性因素（包括肠道微生物组）与局部TME及ICI疗效之间的关联，并指出了当前研究的局限性和未来方向。对于综述而言，它提供了关于微生物组在NSCLC中具体菌种作用的实证数据，以及关于系统性炎症和营养状态如何调节免疫应答的背景知识，同时强调了需要疾病特异性验证的重要性。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述聚焦于NSCLC，其关于微生物组的发现不能直接外推到所有实体瘤或其他癌症类型，尽管文中提到了结直肠癌的对比。\",\n          \"文中提到的微生物组与ICI疗效的关联主要基于观察性研究和初步数据，因果关系尚未完全确立，干预效果也未经验证。\",\n          \"综述本身是叙事性的，其结论依赖于作者对文献的选择和综合，可能存在选择偏倚。\",\n          \"对于Fusobacterium等菌种，文中明确指出其在NSCLC中的作用不如在结直肠癌中明确，需谨慎解读。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"NSCLC肿瘤微环境（TME）\",\n          \"免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）耐药机制\",\n          \"肠道微生物组（Gut Microbiome）与ICI疗效\",\n          \"系统性炎症与宿主状态\",\n          \"联合治疗策略证据分级\",\n          \"生物标志物框架（PD-L1, TMB, Spatial Omics, ctDNA）\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\d227bde3dc62e02cfe87a58fa5173e149048ed4e\\\\627d038304660198900f99576a1ce6c8\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0271\",\n      \"title\": \"The role of the gut microbiome on the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in Japanese responder patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer.\",\n      \"year\": \"2019\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.21037/tlcr.2019.10.23\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0271\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\\\\5a16b2ba1f3b6771b60cbdbf52912ac1\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用回顾性队列设计。纳入标准为确诊为晚期NSCLC、接受ICI治疗时长>3个月的日本患者。收集治疗期间的单次粪便样本，使用Illumina MiSeq平台对16S rRNA基因的V1-V2区域进行测序。利用QIIME流程进行OTU聚类（97%相似度）和Alpha多样性分析。使用LEfSe算法识别反应者与非反应者之间显著差异的分类群。临床结局以治疗失败时间（TTF）为主要终点，使用Kaplan-Meier法和Log-rank检验分析特定细菌属的高/低丰度与TTF的关系。PD-L1表达通过商业实验室的22C3 pharmDx检测进行评估。统计使用EZR软件，P<0.05视为显著。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"在亚洲（日本）NSCLC人群中，初步识别出与ICI疗效相关的特定肠道细菌属（如Lactobacillus, Clostridium, Bilophila等），提供了微生物组作为潜在疗效生物标志物的观察性证据。\",\n            \"limits\": \"样本量极小（仅17例），存在选择偏倚（仅纳入治疗>3个月的患者，可能排除早期进展或不耐受者）；单中心回顾性研究；仅采集单次粪便样本，无法反映治疗前后菌群动态变化；未控制抗生素使用等混杂因素的具体细节（虽记录但未深入校正）；部分关键图表不可读，具体统计细节受限。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"对比ICI反应者（n=6）与非反应者（n=11）的肠道菌群组成\",\n            \"finding\": \"反应者的肠道菌群中乳酸杆菌（Lactobacillus）、梭菌属（Clostridium）和Syntrophococcus显著富集；非反应者中Bilophila、Sutterella和Parabacteroides显著富集。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"特定细菌属丰度与治疗失败时间（TTF）的关系\",\n            \"finding\": \"高丰度的Bilophila与显著延长的TTF相关（HR 6.27, P=0.0037）。高丰度的Lactobacillus和Clostridium倾向于延长TTF，但统计学上不显著（P分别为0.219和0.352）。Syntrophococcus高丰度也显示延长TTF的趋势（P=0.057）。Parabacteroides丰度与TTF无显著关联。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"肠道菌群Alpha多样性\",\n            \"finding\": \"反应者与非反应者之间的Alpha多样性无显著差异，且未影响TTF。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"empirical\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"在晚期NSCLC患者中，是否存在特定的肠道微生物组特征可以作为预测免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）长期疗效的生物标志物？目前缺乏能够筛选出能从ICI中长期获益患者的强效生物标志物。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"日本晚期非小细胞肺癌（NSCLC）患者，接受免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）治疗超过3个月的人群。样本量为17例，分为反应者（R, n=6）和非反应者（NR, n=11）。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本研究旨在探讨肠道微生物组组成与晚期NSCLC患者对免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）疗效之间的关联。研究回顾性分析了17名在日本京都府立医科大学接受ICI治疗且持续时间超过3个月的患者的粪便样本。通过16S rRNA基因测序分析肠道菌群多样性及物种丰度，并结合临床疗效数据（基于RECIST 1.1标准定义的响应状态及治疗失败时间TTF），比较了反应者与非反应者在肠道微生物组成上的差异，并评估特定细菌属的丰度与临床预后指标的相关性。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供NSCLC这一特定实体瘤类型的微生物组数据，填补泛癌种综述中的瘤种特异性空白。\",\n            \"material\": \"日本NSCLC患者队列的微生物组构成数据及与TTF的关联。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为实体瘤中微生物组影响ICI疗效的一个具体案例，特别是强调亚洲人群的数据，有助于评估地域/种族对微生物组-疗效关系的潜在影响。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"展示单一时间点采样方法的局限性。\",\n            \"material\": \"研究仅在治疗期间采集一次粪便样本，未评估基线或治疗前后的动态变化。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"在综述的方法学讨论部分，可用此为例说明单次采样可能无法捕捉微生物组的动态演变及其对免疫治疗反应的复杂影响，支持需要纵向研究设计的观点。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"样本量小（n=17），仅为观察性关联，不能确立因果关系。仅针对日本人群，外推至其他种族需谨慎。仅测量了治疗期间的单次样本，未涵盖基线或动态变化。部分关键细菌（如Lactobacillus）的关联未达到统计学显著性（P>0.05），仅表现为趋势。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了在晚期NSCLC患者中，肠道菌群组成与ICI疗效关联的具体物种列表。发现反应者富集Lactobacillus, Clostridium, Syntrophococcus，而非反应者富集Bilophila, Sutterella, Parabacteroides。其中Bilophila高丰度与显著延长的TTF（即更好的疗效/更慢的疾病进展）相关，这与部分其他研究可能存在的负面关联形成对比或补充，提示需关注菌种特异性而非整体多样性。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物组如何调节实体瘤中ICI的疗效”这一子问题。可作为证据说明某些特定细菌的存在与更好的临床结果（更长TTF）相关，或者相反，某些细菌与较差结果相关。可用于讨论不同研究中微生物组标志物不一致的原因（如人群差异、菌种特异性）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些机制解释主要基于作者引用的既往研究（如小鼠模型），并非本研究直接证实的因果机制。本研究本身未进行机制验证实验（如动物共接种、免疫表型分析等）。因此，这些内容应被视为“假说”或“背景知识”，而非本研究的实证发现。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"作者在讨论中提出了潜在的机制假设：Lactobacillus可能通过促进树突状细胞成熟和调节宿主免疫来增强T细胞动员；而Sutterella和Bilophila可能在肿瘤微环境中引发炎症，导致对ICI不敏感。此外，提到小鼠模型中Lactobacillus可抑制Bilophila诱导的炎症和肠屏障功能障碍。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导该效应的机制是什么”这一子问题。虽然这是作者基于文献的推测而非本研究的直接实验验证，但它提供了将观察到的微生物组变化与已知免疫学机制联系起来的理论框架。可用于综述中讨论微生物组影响免疫治疗的潜在生物学通路（如DC成熟、T细胞浸润、炎症调控）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该研究提供了关于肠道微生物组如何关联固体肿瘤（NSCLC）免疫治疗疗效的直接观察数据。其核心价值在于指出了特定细菌属（有益菌与有害菌）与临床预后（TTF）的方向性关联，尽管由于样本量限制，部分关联未达到统计学显著性。对于综述而言，它提供了具体的“菌-效应”对应关系素材，特别是关于Bilophila与较差预后的负向关联，以及Lactobacillus/Clostridium的正向趋势，可用于支持或反驳不同研究中微生物组对ICI疗效影响的异质性。\",\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该研究样本量极小（17例），统计效力不足，许多具有生物学意义的趋势（如Lactobacillus）未达到统计学显著性。解读时应避免夸大其确定性，应视为探索性证据。\",\n          \"研究对象仅限于日本NSCLC患者，且均为长期治疗者（>3个月），存在严重的选择偏倚（幸存者偏差）。结果可能不适用于早期治疗失败者或其他种族群体。\",\n          \"作者提出的机制（如Lactobacillus促进DC成熟）是基于文献综述的推测，本研究并未提供直接的分子或细胞水平证据。在综述中引用时，必须明确区分这是作者的假设还是本研究的实证结果。\",\n          \"Bilophila高丰度与延长TTF（更好疗效）的关联可能与部分其他研究报道的负面作用相反，这突显了微生物组研究的高度情境依赖性和潜在矛盾，需谨慎对待单一研究的结论。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiome composition in NSCLC\",\n          \"immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy biomarkers\",\n          \"Lactobacillus abundance and ICI response\",\n          \"Clostridium abundance and ICI response\",\n          \"Bilophila abundance and ICI resistance\",\n          \"Time to treatment failure (TTF) as clinical endpoint\",\n          \"Japanese population specificity in microbiome studies\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"known_gaps\": [\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-b5c2e925a4132b88eff6\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-7f937d5566b4c12030ce\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-a96686bee2f7be7a8dd8\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-724817b1ad2fa81c8182\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-ba354d40740f64366cbf\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"asset_id\": \"asset-3aa25875e4222837763e\",\n            \"kind\": \"asset\",\n            \"reason\": \"caption_or_locator_without_readable_image\",\n            \"status\": \"caption_only\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\753e796b9f6fe2fe78f1b4c6074d39441710eda7\\\\5a16b2ba1f3b6771b60cbdbf52912ac1\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0387\",\n      \"title\": \"Immunotherapy with guts: a review of microbial therapeutic adjuncts for immunotherapy in solid tumors\",\n      \"year\": \"2026\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1093/oncolo/oyag131\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"a7aa74ce68cc3094e2a185d973f39ba70ae017d9\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"采用叙事性综述方法，梳理已发表的临床试验数据（包括已完成和正在进行的研究）。作者按干预类型（益生菌、菌群组合、FMT）分类整理证据，对比各试验的设计、患者人群、疗效指标（ORR, mPFS）及不良事件。同时，结合讨论部分，分析导致临床结果不一致的原因（如物种特异性、定植能力、抗生素预处理的影响），并评估当前监管框架和研究方法论（如小鼠模型的局限性）对转化研究的制约。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了关于微生物疗法作为ICI辅助治疗的全景式临床证据汇总，特别是区分了益生菌、菌群组合和FMT在不同实体瘤中的表现差异。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，未提供新的原始实验数据；对机制的解释主要基于作者对现有文献的综合推断，具体分子通路尚不明确；所引用的临床试验多为小样本或早期阶段，统计效力有限。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"指出了当前领域面临的非科学障碍，如监管标准不适用于复杂微生物产品、患者招募困难及抗生素预处理策略的潜在风险。\",\n            \"limits\": \"这些观点属于领域内的共识性讨论，并非该综述独有的实证发现；对于如何解决这些系统性问题，仅提出了方向性建议（如AI建模、标准化供体筛选），未提供具体解决方案。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"使用单一或少数菌株益生菌（如CBM588）联合ICI\",\n            \"finding\": \"在某些特定癌种（如转移性肾细胞癌）中显示出较高的ORR提升（58%-74% vs 对照20%），但在其他癌种（如微卫星稳定型结直肠癌）中效果不佳或无效。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"使用多菌株微生物菌群组合（如MET4, SER-401, VE800）\",\n            \"finding\": \"结果高度异质。MET4在部分队列中显示潜在获益，但受限于招募失败；SER-401因早期终止且初步数据显示疗效不优于安慰剂而终止；VE800整体ORR极低（1.9%），仅在黑色素瘤亚组中观察到一定益处。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"使用粪便微生物移植（FMT）联合ICI\",\n            \"finding\": \"在多种难治性实体瘤（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、MSI-H癌症等）中显示出一致的积极信号，ORR普遍较高（20%-77%），且安全性总体可控，提示其可能具有广谱抗肿瘤活性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"机制研究与模型转化\",\n            \"finding\": \"目前缺乏明确的单一因果细菌物种；机制涉及T细胞浸润、代谢物（如短链脂肪酸）产生及免疫基因集变化。小鼠模型与人类微生物组差异巨大，限制了直接外推。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管ICI疗法改变了部分实体瘤的治疗格局，但其对“冷”肿瘤或无响应患者的效果有限。本研究旨在解决如何安全有效地将微生物疗法作为ICI的辅助手段，以扩大受益人群并克服耐药性问题。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"固体肿瘤患者中，肠道微生物群作为免疫检查点抑制剂（ICI）辅助治疗的临床前与临床研究现状。涵盖益生菌、微生物菌群组合及粪便微生物移植（FMT）三种主要干预手段。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本文是一篇综述，旨在评估通过调节肠道微生物群来增强固体肿瘤免疫治疗疗效的潜力。文章系统回顾了基于单菌株/有限混合物的益生菌（如CBM588、EDP1503）、多菌株微生物菌群组合（如MET4、SER-401、VE800）以及全环境粪便微生物移植（FMT）在联合ICI治疗中的临床试验结果。研究重点分析了不同干预策略在客观缓解率（ORR）、无进展生存期（mPFS）及安全性方面的表现，并探讨了从动物模型到人体转化的挑战、监管障碍、患者招募困难以及现有机制理解的局限性。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为回答“肠道微生物如何调节ICI疗效”提供了关键的临床分层视角。它不侧重于单一机制的深度解析，而是通过对比三类不同复杂度的微生物干预措施（益生菌vs菌群组合vs FMT）的临床结局，揭示了“微生物复杂性/多样性”与“治疗效果”之间的潜在正相关关系。这对于解释为何某些简单补充剂失败而FMT成功具有重要参考价值，并为后续探讨机制提供了临床现象层面的约束条件。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些数据来自小样本早期临床试验，存在选择偏倚和统计效力不足的问题。不能据此断定某种特定菌株在所有情况下均有效或无效。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了三类微生物干预策略在多种实体瘤中与ICI联用的临床疗效对比数据。具体而言，指出FMT在多种难治性肿瘤中表现出较一致的ORR提升（20-77%），而益生菌和菌群组合的效果则呈现高度的癌种特异性和菌株依赖性（如CBM588在mRCC有效但在MSS CRC无效，VE800整体无效）。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“肠道微生物如何调节ICI疗效”中的效应大小和一致性部分。可作为证据说明：微生物疗法的疗效并非普适，而是依赖于干预措施的复杂度（全菌群vs单一菌）及肿瘤类型。可用于支持“微生物组成多样性对疗效至关重要”的论点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制描述多为相关性观察或基于动物模型的推测，文中明确承认“大部分未知”。不能将这些关联视为已证实的因果机制。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"讨论了微生物调节ICI疗效的可能机制线索，包括T细胞浸润增加、抗原呈递细胞（APCs）积累、IL-8产生髓系细胞减少以及短链脂肪酸（SCFAs）等代谢物的作用。同时指出目前缺乏单一因果物种的证据，倾向于网络效应和功能冗余。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答“介导效应的机制”。虽然未提供完整的因果链条，但提供了从临床观察反推的机制假设（如免疫激活状态改变）。可用于解释为何FMT比单一益生菌更可能成功（因为保留了复杂的生态互作网络）。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"背景与概念框架\",\n            \"material\": \"介绍了ICI的作用原理（PD-1/PD-L1, CTLA-4抑制，T细胞增殖）及其在“冷”肿瘤中的局限性。定义了“热”与“冷”肿瘤的概念，并引入肠道微生物群作为潜在的共治疗手段。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"为综述提供基础背景，解释为何需要研究微生物与ICI的相互作用，以及当前的临床痛点（即大部分患者无响应）。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"方法论反思与未来方向\",\n            \"material\": \"批评了传统还原论方法（寻找单一因果基因/蛋白）在微生物研究中的失效，提出需要机器学习/AI模型来处理微生物组的复杂性，并指出小鼠模型与人类微生物组的差异是转化研究的瓶颈。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述的方法论讨论部分，提示在综合现有文献时需注意研究设计的局限性（如动物模型的外推风险），并强调未来研究需关注复杂网络而非单一物种。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"实践障碍与监管环境\",\n            \"material\": \"详细描述了微生物疗法在临床试验中面临的监管难题（难以标准化复现的产品）、患者招募困难以及抗生素预处理带来的潜在危害（如SER-401试验中的负面结果）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于解释为何尽管有初步 promising 结果，但临床转化缓慢。这有助于理解现有文献中阴性或中止试验的背景原因，避免误读为生物学上的完全无效。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"该综述主要关注细菌疗法，虽提及真菌、古菌和病毒，但未深入展开，阅读时需限定在细菌层面。\",\n          \"所有临床数据均为早期阶段（Phase I/II）或小样本研究，结果存在较大不确定性，不可将其视为确证性证据。\",\n          \"作者强调FMT效果较好，但这可能源于FMT引入了更多样的菌群而非特定成分，需谨慎归因。\",\n          \"文中提到的“抗生素预处理”在SER-401试验中被证明可能有害，这一反面案例对于设计微生物干预方案具有重要警示意义。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"免疫检查点抑制剂 (ICIs)\",\n          \"固体肿瘤 (Solid Tumors)\",\n          \"肠道微生物群 (Gut Microbiome)\",\n          \"益生菌 (Probiotics: CBM588, EDP1503)\",\n          \"微生物菌群组合 (Microbial Consortia: MET4, SER-401, VE800)\",\n          \"粪便微生物移植 (FMT)\",\n          \"客观缓解率 (ORR)\",\n          \"无进展生存期 (mPFS)\",\n          \"微卫星稳定型结直肠癌 (MSS mCRC)\",\n          \"转移性肾细胞癌 (mRCC)\",\n          \"黑色素瘤 (Melanoma)\",\n          \"非小细胞肺癌 (NSCLC)\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0387\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\a7aa74ce68cc3094e2a185d973f39ba70ae017d9\\\\698d91b5627be0559331510882aeb17e\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\a7aa74ce68cc3094e2a185d973f39ba70ae017d9\\\\698d91b5627be0559331510882aeb17e\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0261\",\n      \"title\": \"Facts and Hopes for Gut Microbiota Interventions in Cancer Immunotherapy\",\n      \"year\": \"2022\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1158/1078-0432.ccr-21-1129\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"6f9060daa22a7c9efcd4a92919035c5e5c24367a\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"As a narrative review, the authors systematically summarize and categorize existing literature rather than conducting a new systematic meta-analysis. The approach involves: (1) reviewing preclinical mouse models to establish causal links between specific bacteria and ICI response; (2) analyzing human observational cohort studies to identify associations between microbiota composition (taxa and diversity) and clinical outcomes (response vs. non-response); (3) synthesizing proposed biological mechanisms (T-cell activation, mimicry, metabolites); and (4) evaluating early-phase clinical trial data for microbiome interventions (FMT, consortia, diet). The review explicitly compares findings across different studies to highlight inconsistencies in biomarker discovery and emphasizes the need for standardized methodologies.\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides a comprehensive synthesis of preclinical and clinical evidence linking the gut microbiome to ICI efficacy and toxicity, categorizing mechanisms into three distinct themes.\",\n            \"limits\": \"As a narrative review, it does not perform a quantitative meta-analysis; it highlights the lack of consensus in human biomarker studies rather than resolving it.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Critically evaluates the limitations of current human studies, including small sample sizes, geographic biases, and analytical pipeline differences, advocating for standardized large-scale datasets.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Does not provide definitive answers on which specific microbial signatures are universally predictive across all solid tumor types.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"Summarizes early-phase clinical trial data for various microbiome interventions (FMT, consortia, diet), offering a landscape of current therapeutic developments.\",\n            \"limits\": \"Most clinical trial data reported are from early-phase (I/II) studies with small sample sizes; long-term efficacy and safety profiles are not yet established.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Preclinical mouse models\",\n            \"finding\": \"Specific commensals (e.g., Bifidobacterium spp., Akkermansia muciniphila, Bacteroides fragilis) enhance anti-PD-1 or anti-CTLA-4 efficacy via dendritic cell activation, STING pathway signaling, and modulation of T-cell subsets.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Human observational studies (Melanoma, NSCLC, RCC)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Responders to ICIs often show higher abundance of specific taxa (e.g., Faecalibacterium, Ruminococcaceae, A. muciniphila), but there is no consensus on a universal predictive signature due to cohort-specific variations, geographic differences, and methodological heterogeneity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Antibiotic use during ICI therapy\",\n            \"finding\": \"Broad-spectrum antibiotic use is consistently associated with reduced progression-free survival and overall survival in ICI-treated patients, likely due to depletion of beneficial microbes.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Mechanistic pathways\",\n            \"finding\": \"Three primary mechanisms are identified: (1) direct stimulation of antitumor T-cells; (2) molecular mimicry between bacterial and tumor antigens; (3) production of immunomodulatory metabolites like short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), secondary bile acids, and inosine.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Immune-related adverse events (irAEs)\",\n            \"finding\": \"Specific microbiota profiles are linked to irAEs, particularly colitis. For example, certain Firmicutes are associated with anti-CTLA-4 colitis, and Streptococcus abundance correlates with arthritis and poor prognosis.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"Clinical trials of FMT\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT from responders to PD-1 refractory melanoma patients can restore sensitivity to PD-1 inhibitors, with some patients achieving objective responses, though engraftment stability varies.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"The review addresses the question of how the gut microbiome impacts the efficacy and toxicity of cancer immunotherapies, aiming to consolidate preclinical and clinical evidence to understand the rationale for microbiome-targeted therapies and identify gaps in current knowledge regarding predictive biomarkers and mechanistic pathways.\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"The review focuses on the role of the gut microbiome in modulating responses to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) targeting PD-1/PD-L1 and CTLA-4 in patients with advanced solid tumors (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC, GI cancers). It covers preclinical evidence from mouse models, observational human data linking microbiota composition to clinical outcomes and adverse events, potential biological mechanisms, and early-phase clinical trials of microbiome-based interventions.\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"This review synthesizes current knowledge on how the gut microbiome influences cancer immunotherapy. It begins by summarizing preclinical studies demonstrating that specific commensal bacteria enhance ICI efficacy through T-cell stimulation and metabolite production. It then reviews human observational studies, highlighting the inconsistent identification of predictive microbial signatures across different cohorts and cancer types, while noting the negative impact of broad-spectrum antibiotics. The paper outlines three main mechanistic themes: direct T-cell stimulation, molecular mimicry, and immunomodulatory metabolites (e.g., SCFAs, bile acids, inosine). It also discusses the microbiome's role in immune-related adverse events (irAEs), particularly colitis. Finally, it evaluates emerging therapeutic strategies, including fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), defined bacterial consortia, probiotics, and dietary interventions, based on results from early-phase clinical trials.\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"This review serves as a foundational resource for understanding the biological plausibility and clinical status of microbiome-immunotherapy interactions. It provides detailed mechanistic hypotheses (direct T-cell stimulation, mimicry, metabolites) and a critical assessment of why human biomarker discovery has been challenging (cohort heterogeneity). It also offers concrete examples of intervention strategies (FMT, consortia) and their preliminary clinical outcomes, which are essential for discussing the transition from association to causation and therapy.\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"The association is correlational in humans; causality is primarily established in mouse models. Predictive signatures are not consistent across all studies or cancer types.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Provides evidence that specific gut bacteria (e.g., Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia, Faecalibacterium) are associated with improved response to PD-1/CTLA-4 inhibitors in melanoma, NSCLC, and RCC. It details how broad-spectrum antibiotics negatively impact ICI efficacy.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to support the argument that the gut microbiome modulates ICI efficacy. Can be used to list specific taxa associated with better outcomes and to cite the negative impact of antibiotics as evidence of microbiome importance.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Many mechanisms are derived from preclinical mouse models; human validation is ongoing. The relative contribution of each mechanism in humans is not fully quantified.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Outlines three mechanistic categories: (1) Direct T-cell stimulation (e.g., via DC activation or TLR signaling); (2) Molecular mimicry (cross-reactive T-cells); (3) Metabolite production (SCFAs, bile acids, inosine) influencing the tumor microenvironment and systemic immunity.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to answer 'what mechanisms mediate that effect'. Provides specific biological pathways and molecules (e.g., STING pathway, NOD2, adenosine receptors, GPRs) that link microbial presence to immune function.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Links are largely observational; causal relationships for non-colitis irAEs are less established.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Discusses the role of the microbiome in irAEs, specifically colitis and arthritis, linking specific taxa (e.g., Firmicutes, Streptococcus) to these toxicities.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to discuss the dual role of the microbiome in both efficacy and toxicity. Highlights the complexity of microbiome modulation, as enhancing immunity may increase adverse events.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"Data are from small, early-phase trials; long-term durability and optimal donor selection criteria are not yet resolved.\",\n            \"contribution\": \"Reports early clinical trial results for FMT and defined consortia (e.g., CBM588, SER-401) showing potential to restore ICI sensitivity in refractory patients.\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"Use to illustrate the translational potential of microbiome interventions. Provides concrete examples of successful re-challenge strategies.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Contextualizes the field by highlighting the discrepancy between preclinical success and human biomarker inconsistency.\",\n            \"material\": \"Discussion of cohort-specific microbial signatures, geographic effects (enterotypes), and methodological heterogeneity (sequencing pipelines, response definitions).\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To explain why identifying a universal microbiome biomarker for ICI response remains difficult, providing context for future research directions.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Provides background on dietary interventions as a non-invasive way to modulate the microbiome.\",\n            \"material\": \"Details on high-fiber diets, ketogenic diets, and fasting-mimicking diets affecting microbiota composition and ICI outcomes.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To include lifestyle/dietary factors as part of the broader strategy for microbiome modulation in cancer therapy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"Offers a classification of current clinical trials involving microbiome therapeutics.\",\n            \"material\": \"Table 2 listing various trials (FMT, consortia, probiotics, prebiotics) with their phases, indications, and products.\",\n            \"purpose\": \"To map the current landscape of clinical development for microbiome-based adjuncts to immunotherapy.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"The review covers multiple solid tumor types (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC, GI cancers); findings may not be generalizable to hematologic malignancies or other cancer types not discussed.\",\n          \"Human data are primarily observational associations; causal claims should be attributed to preclinical models unless supported by interventional trial data.\",\n          \"Biomarker findings are inconsistent across studies; do not present any single taxon as a definitive universal predictor without noting the cohort-specific nature of the evidence.\",\n          \"Clinical trial results are from early phases; avoid overstating efficacy or safety based on small sample sizes.\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"Gut microbiome composition and ICI response\",\n          \"Mechanisms of microbiome-mediated immunotherapy enhancement\",\n          \"Microbiome and immune-related adverse events (irAEs)\",\n          \"Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) in cancer\",\n          \"Defined bacterial consortia and probiotics\",\n          \"Dietary interventions (fiber, ketogenic diet)\",\n          \"Methodological challenges in microbiome biomarker discovery\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0261\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\6f9060daa22a7c9efcd4a92919035c5e5c24367a\\\\cc59b1602065d8843b6a0ee6c2797d9c\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\6f9060daa22a7c9efcd4a92919035c5e5c24367a\\\\cc59b1602065d8843b6a0ee6c2797d9c\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0250\",\n      \"title\": \"Exploring the role of the gut microbiome in modulating response to anti-PD-1 immunotherapy in melanoma patients\",\n      \"year\": \"2025\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.18231/j.ijcbr.2024.033\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"67a11dcbb1430387e3630bb0ffa834eccc29fa4f\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"本文是一篇综述（Review）。作者通过梳理现有文献，系统阐述了肠道微生物组与宿主免疫系统之间的相互作用机制，重点分析了特定微生物物种、代谢产物（如短链脂肪酸）以及菌群失调对抗PD-1免疫治疗疗效的影响。文章进一步归纳了基于微生物组的干预策略，包括粪便微生物群移植（FMT）、饮食干预和益生菌使用，并讨论了其在黑色素瘤治疗中的潜力与挑战。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"综合了肠道微生物组影响抗PD-1疗法疗效的多种机制，包括T细胞激活、代谢物调节及免疫检查点表达调控；汇总了FMT、饮食和益生菌等干预策略的临床前及早期临床研究证据；明确了特定菌种（如Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium）和菌群多样性作为预测生物标志物的潜在价值。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，其结论依赖于所引用的原始研究，未提供新的实验数据或独立验证；对于不同干预策略的有效性和安全性比较缺乏直接的头对头证据；部分机制（如具体菌株与免疫细胞的精确分子通路）仍基于动物模型或小型临床试验，外推至所有实体瘤存在不确定性；摘要未交代具体的文献检索策略、纳入排除标准及质量评估方法。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"在黑色素瘤患者中，接受抗PD-1治疗且肠道菌群中含有特定有益菌（如Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium）或具有高多样性的个体。\",\n            \"finding\": \"这些患者表现出更好的治疗反应和生存获益，机制涉及增强CD8+ T细胞活化、增加肿瘤浸润及促进Th1/Th17分化。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"肠道菌群失调（Dysbiosis），表现为有益菌减少、有害菌增多或多样性降低。\",\n            \"finding\": \"与抗PD-1治疗耐药相关，机制包括调节性T细胞（Tregs）扩增、髓源性抑制细胞（MDSCs）作用增强以及效应T细胞向肿瘤微环境的浸润减少。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"使用粪便微生物群移植（FMT）将健康供体菌群转移至对免疫治疗耐药的黑色素瘤患者。\",\n            \"finding\": \"Davari等人（2021）的研究显示，FMT可恢复有利的免疫环境，增强T细胞活化，使部分先前耐药的患者重新获得对PD-1阻断的反应。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"摄入富含纤维的饮食以支持短链脂肪酸（SCFAs）产生菌的生长。\",\n            \"finding\": \"SCFAs（如丁酸、丙酸、乙酸）可通过抑制组蛋白去乙酰化酶（HDACs）和促进抗炎信号通路，增强抗肿瘤免疫反应，改善抗PD-1疗效。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"尽管抗PD-1免疫疗法显著改善了黑色素瘤患者的生存率，但疗效存在高度异质性。本研究旨在探讨肠道微生物组如何调节这种疗效差异，阐明其背后的免疫学机制，并评估通过调节微生物组（如FMT、饮食、益生菌）来增强免疫治疗效果的潜在策略。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"研究对象为黑色素瘤患者的肠道微生物组及其与抗PD-1免疫治疗的相互作用。情境涵盖从基础免疫机制（T细胞激活、代谢物作用）到临床干预策略（FMT、饮食、益生菌）的多个层面。范围主要聚焦于黑色素瘤，但也提及对其他癌症的潜在启示。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"本综述旨在解决抗PD-1免疫疗法在黑色素瘤治疗中疗效变异性大的问题。文章首先介绍了肠道微生物组在维持免疫稳态和启动抗肿瘤免疫中的核心作用，详细阐述了微生物通过直接接触（如GALT中的抗原呈递）和间接代谢产物（如SCFAs）调节宿主免疫系统的机制。随后，文章总结了与良好治疗反应相关的微生物特征（如高多样性、特定菌属丰度）以及与耐药相关的菌群失调状态。最后，综述评估了三种主要的微生物组干预策略：FMT在逆转耐药方面的初步成功、高纤维饮食对SCFA产生的促进作用，以及益生菌使用的潜在益处与安全风险。文章指出，理解并操纵微生物组有望实现更个性化的癌症免疫治疗，但仍需更多临床研究来优化方案并确保安全性。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物组如何调节固体肿瘤（特别是黑色素瘤）对免疫检查点抑制剂（如抗PD-1）的疗效提供了全面的背景信息和机制解释。它系统地整理了微生物组组成、代谢产物与免疫反应之间的关联，并评估了FMT、饮食和益生菌等干预策略的潜力。对于综述而言，此文是构建微生物组-免疫治疗相互作用理论框架的重要素材，尤其适用于阐述机制细节和讨论潜在的治疗增强策略。然而，由于其综述性质和主要聚焦于黑色素瘤，其在推广到其他实体瘤或直接比较不同干预策略有效性方面的证据力度有限，需谨慎引用。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"主要基于黑色素瘤数据，其他实体瘤的直接证据较少；机制多为相关性或动物模型推断，临床因果链条尚不完全清晰；FMT等证据来自小规模研究。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了肠道微生物组通过调节T细胞活化和肿瘤微环境来影响抗PD-1疗效的详细机制图谱，包括特定菌种、代谢物和免疫细胞亚群之间的相互作用。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"帮助解释微生物组影响ICIs疗效的具体生物学途径，特别是T细胞介导的免疫监视和代谢物介导的基因表达调控。可用于构建机制模型或解释观察到的疗效差异。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"主要关注黑色素瘤；未提供大规模多中心试验结果；不同干预策略的直接比较缺失。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"总结了目前可用的微生物组干预策略（FMT、饮食、益生菌）在增强ICIs疗效方面的临床前和早期临床证据，并指出了各自的潜在风险和局限性。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"用于回答微生物组如何被用来调节ICIs疗效的问题，特别是通过列举具体的干预手段及其预期效果。可为后续规划提供关于哪些干预策略值得深入调查的线索。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供肠道微生物组影响免疫检查点抑制剂疗效的背景知识和理论框架。\",\n            \"material\": \"微生物组与免疫系统相互作用的总体描述，包括GALT的作用、T细胞亚群的分化（Th1, Th17, Tregs）以及SCFAs的免疫调节功能。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述中解释“为什么”微生物组会影响ICIs疗效的基础生物学背景部分。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于特定微生物物种（Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium）和代谢物（SCFAs）与治疗效果关联的具体实例。\",\n            \"material\": \"列举了与良好预后相关的特定菌种和代谢物，以及菌群失调导致的负面后果。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"作为证据支持微生物组组成作为预测生物标志物的论点，或在讨论具体机制时引用。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于微生物组干预策略（FMT, 饮食, 益生菌）的现状、潜力和风险的综合概述。\",\n            \"material\": \"详细介绍了FMT在耐药患者中的应用案例、饮食干预的机制及益生菌的安全顾虑。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述中讨论“如何”利用微生物组改善ICIs疗效的策略部分，特别是比较不同干预方法的优缺点。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"提供关于当前领域局限性和未来方向的见解。\",\n            \"material\": \"指出需要更多临床试验来确定最佳供体、给药频率、长期安全性以及益生菌的具体菌株选择。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"用于综述的讨论或结论部分，指出当前研究的空白和未来研究方向。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"scope_interpretation_cautions\": [\n          \"本文主要聚焦于黑色素瘤，其结论在其他实体瘤中的适用性需结合其他研究进行验证，不能直接外推。\",\n          \"文中提到的机制（如特定菌种与T细胞活化的关系）多基于已发表的研究汇总，部分可能源自动物模型，临床因果关系尚未完全确立。\",\n          \"FMT的证据主要来自小规模研究（如Davari et al.），其长期安全性和最佳实践方案仍在探索中，不应视为成熟的标准治疗方案。\",\n          \"益生菌的使用存在安全争议，文中明确提到需谨慎，避免将其简单推荐为通用辅助疗法。\",\n          \"综述未提供具体的文献检索策略和质量评估细节，因此其结论的代表性取决于其所引用的原始研究的质量。\"\n        ],\n        \"topic_handles\": [\n          \"gut microbiome composition and diversity\",\n          \"anti-PD-1 immunotherapy response variability\",\n          \"melanoma treatment outcomes\",\n          \"short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) immunomodulation\",\n          \"fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)\",\n          \"dietary interventions for microbiome modulation\",\n          \"probiotics safety and efficacy in cancer\",\n          \"T-cell activation and tumor infiltration\",\n          \"dysbiosis and immunotherapy resistance\",\n          \"Akkermansia muciniphila role\",\n          \"Bifidobacterium species role\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"locator\": {\n        \"writer_packet\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\\\planning\\\\writer_packets\\\\Ch5.json\",\n        \"source_handle\": \"P0250\",\n        \"card_path\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\67a11dcbb1430387e3630bb0ffa834eccc29fa4f\\\\b1e9ef35368d28e0586b864b17950ba2\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n      },\n      \"card_material\": {\n        \"material_scope\": \"fulltext\"\n      },\n      \"material_read_from_disk\": \"F:\\\\OptoMind-Review-2\\\\outputs\\\\paper_cards\\\\20260922_microbiome_batch\\\\reading\\\\cards\\\\67a11dcbb1430387e3630bb0ffa834eccc29fa4f\\\\b1e9ef35368d28e0586b864b17950ba2\\\\attempt-01\\\\PAPER_READING_CARD.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_handle\": \"P0231\",\n      \"title\": \"Potential role of gut microbes in the efficacy and toxicity of immune checkpoints inhibitors\",\n      \"year\": \"2023\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.3389/fphar.2023.1170591\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"608ca9e37ce71dcbbe9281286a7b96096c05b73c\",\n      \"material_depth\": \"fulltext\",\n      \"material_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"study_summary_A\": {\n        \"approach\": \"本研究采用文献综述的方法。作者检索并整理了近年来关于肠道微生物群与ICI疗效及毒性关系的研究，重点分析了不同癌症类型中响应者与无响应者/发生irAEs患者之间的微生物组成差异（基于16S rRNA测序和宏基因组测序数据）。文章通过表格汇总了各研究中涉及的细菌门类、属种及其与临床结局的关联，并归纳了相关的代谢机制和动物实验验证结果。最后，综述了FMT及其他微生态调节策略的临床应用现状、局限性及未来方向。\",\n        \"contribution_and_limits\": [\n          {\n            \"contribution\": \"系统汇总了不同实体瘤中肠道微生物群与ICI疗效及irAEs关联的现有证据，提供了按癌症类型和微生物门类分类的详细参考表（Table 1, 2, 3, 4, 5），有助于识别潜在的生物标志物。\",\n            \"limits\": \"作为综述，其结论依赖于所纳入研究的原始数据质量，而原始研究多为观察性队列，存在混杂因素（如饮食、抗生素使用）和方法学差异。文章未提供新的实验数据，对因果机制的解释主要基于作者对现有文献的综合推断，缺乏大规模前瞻性验证。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"key_findings\": [\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"不同实体瘤类型（黑色素瘤、NSCLC、RCC、HCC等）\",\n            \"finding\": \"厚壁菌门（Firmicutes）和放线菌门（Actinobacteria）中的某些菌种（如双歧杆菌、普拉梭菌）常与ICI疗效正相关；拟杆菌门（Bacteroidetes）的作用具有情境依赖性，部分情况下与疗效负相关或与irAEs相关；变形菌门（Proteobacteria）通常与无响应或较差预后相关；粘质沙雷氏菌（Akkermansia muciniphila）在多种肿瘤中被发现与良好疗效相关。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的发生\",\n            \"finding\": \"irAEs的发生与特定的微生物组成有关。例如，厚壁菌门在某些研究中与高概率的irAEs相关，而在另一些研究中（如肝细胞癌）其高丰度可能具有保护作用。拟杆菌门在某些情况下与严重的irAEs（如结肠炎）相关。变形菌门、酸杆菌门（Acidobacteria）等在irAEs患者中富集。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"微生物代谢产物\",\n            \"finding\": \"有益代谢物如短链脂肪酸（SCFAs）、多糖A（PSA）、肌苷（inosine）可通过调节T细胞功能、抑制炎症或增强抗肿瘤免疫来促进ICI疗效。有害代谢物如脂多糖（LPS）、氨、酚类物质可能促进免疫逃逸或慢性炎症。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"conditions\": \"粪菌移植（FMT）\",\n            \"finding\": \"FMT在小样本临床试验和动物模型中显示出增强ICI疗效（如增加CD8+ T细胞浸润）和缓解难治性ICI相关结肠炎的潜力，但存在感染风险、供体选择异质性和长期效果不确定性等局限。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"paper_kind\": \"review\",\n        \"problem_or_question\": \"研究动机在于ICIs虽然显著改善了多种癌症患者的生存率，但响应率低（13%-69%）且伴随不可预测的免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）。现有证据表明肠道微生物群可能影响这些结果，但具体哪些微生物、通过何种机制影响疗效和毒性尚需系统总结，以便指导临床个性化治疗和开发新的干预策略。\",\n        \"research_scope\": \"免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）在实体瘤治疗中的应用，聚焦于肠道微生物群对疗效和免疫相关不良事件（irAEs）的影响，以及粪菌移植（FMT）等干预手段。\",\n        \"work_summary\": \"这是一篇2023年发表的综述文章，旨在探讨肠道微生物群如何调节免疫检查点抑制剂（ICIs）的疗效和毒性。文章首先介绍了肠道微生物作为“隐藏器官”在维持肠道屏障和免疫监视中的作用，随后系统梳理了不同实体瘤（如转移性黑色素瘤、非小细胞肺癌、肾细胞癌、肝细胞癌等）中，特定细菌门类和物种与ICI疗效及irAEs发生的相关性。文章还讨论了微生物代谢产物（如短链脂肪酸、肌苷等）在介导免疫反应中的潜在机制，并总结了通过粪菌移植（FMT）、益生菌、益生元等手段调节肠道菌群以增强疗效或减轻毒性的临床进展与挑战。\"\n      },\n      \"review_planning_B\": {\n        \"planning_summary\": \"该综述为理解肠道微生物群如何影响ICI疗效和毒性提供了全面的背景知识和证据汇总。它特别适用于构建综述中关于‘微生物群落特征与临床结局关联’的部分，以及讨论‘微生态干预策略（如FMT）’的章节。文中提供的详细细菌门类/物种列表及其在不同癌症类型中的表现，可作为跨论文比较的基础素材。\",\n        \"facet_contributions\": [\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"这些关联主要基于观察性研究，存在方法学差异和混杂因素，不能直接确立因果关系。不同研究间结果可能存在矛盾（如拟杆菌门的作用），需结合具体研究条件解读。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"提供了不同实体瘤类型中，与ICI疗效（响应/无响应）相关的特定肠道微生物门类及代表性菌种的详细列表。例如，指出厚壁菌门和放线菌门中的某些菌常与疗效正相关，而变形菌门常与无响应相关。同时列出了与irAEs发生相关的微生物特征。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F1\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于回答‘肠道微生物群如何调节ICI疗效’的问题，通过列举具体的微生物标志物及其在不同癌症中的表现，展示微生物组特征的异质性。\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"boundaries\": \"机制部分主要基于文献综述和动物模型，人类体内的确切分子通路仍需更多临床验证。作者提出的机制解释属于理论推测，尚未完全证实。\",\n            \"contribution\": \"概述了微生物影响ICI疗效和毒性的潜在机制，包括微生物代谢产物（如SCFAs、肌苷、多糖A）对免疫系统（如T细胞分化、炎症反应）的调节作用，以及动物实验中微生物干预对肿瘤微环境的影响。\",\n            \"facet_id\": \"F2\",\n            \"possible_uses\": \"可用于回答‘介导该效应的机制是什么’的问题，提供从微生物组成到免疫调节功能的理论连接点。\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"broader_review_uses\": [\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"为综述提供必要的背景信息，解释为何关注肠道微生物群与ICI的关系。\",\n            \"material\": \"介绍了肠道微生物群的基本生理功能（维持肠道屏障、免疫监视）及其作为‘隐藏器官’的重要性，以及ICIs治疗的现状和挑战（响应率低、irAEs频发）。\",\n            \"purpose\": \"背景介绍\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"connection_to_review\": \"为综述中讨论‘如何通过调节微生物群优化ICI治疗’提供策略层面的素材，特别是FMT的临床进展和局限性。\",\n            \"material\": \"总结了FMT、益生菌、益生元、合生元等调节肠道微生物群的临床和非临床干预手段，�
